ion directory (default: `.cypilot`), ...\n> +The dialog MUST ask: (1) installation directory (default: `cypilot`), ...\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/PRD.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `229-242`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Fix stale default install directory in Project Initialization text**\n> \n> Line 242 still says the default is `.cypilot`, which conflicts with the updated default `cypilot` used elsewhere in this PRD.\n> \n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n> - ... The dialog MUST ask: (1) installation directory (default: `.cypilot`), ...\n> + ... The dialog MUST ask: (1) installation directory (default: `cypilot`), ...\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/PRD.md around lines 229 - 242, The Project\n> Initialization section still mentions the old default install directory\n> \".cypilot\"; update that text to use the new default \"cypilot\" wherever the\n> default install dir is referenced (the paragraph describing the interactive\n> `cypilot init` command and the prompt about installation directory), and ensure\n> related placeholders like `{install_dir}` and `{cypilot_path}`/references to\n> AGENTS.md injection reflect the \"cypilot\" default consistently (e.g., the\n> suggested `ALWAYS open `@/`{install_dir}/config/AGENTS.md FIRST` and the created\n> paths such as `{cypilot_path}/config/core.toml` should assume the default\n> install_dir = \"cypilot\").\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🟠 Major comments (31)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/migrate.py-1816-1816 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1816-1816`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`migrate-config` now loses meaningful human-mode output.**\n\nLine 1816 calls `ui.result(result)` without a `human_fn`, but `run_migrate_config()` doesn’t provide `status`/`message`. In default human mode this falls back to a generic status line and hides conversion details.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n def cmd_migrate_config(argv: List[str]) -> int:\n@@\n     project_root = Path(args.project_root).resolve() if args.project_root else Path.cwd().resolve()\n\n     result = run_migrate_config(project_root)\n-    ui.result(result)\n+    def _human_migrate_config(data: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:\n+        sys.stderr.write(\n+            f\"Converted {data.get('converted_count', 0)} file(s); \"\n+            f\"skipped {data.get('skipped_count', 0)}.\\n\"\n+        )\n+        skipped = data.get(\"skipped\", [])\n+        if skipped:\n+            sys.stderr.write(\"Skipped details:\\n\")\n+            for item in skipped:\n+                sys.stderr.write(\n+                    f\"  - {item.get('file', '<unknown>')}: {item.get('reason', '')}\\n\"\n+                )\n+\n+    ui.result(result, human_fn=_human_migrate_config)\n\n     return 0\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/migrate.py at line\n1816, The call ui.result(result) is missing a human_fn so the migrate-config\ncommand shows only a generic status; update the call site that invokes\nui.result(result) (after run_migrate_config()) to either pass a human_fn that\nformats the conversion details (e.g., summarizes converted files, errors, and\nany warnings) or ensure run_migrate_config() returns a result object with\nexplicit status/message fields; reference ui.result(result) and\nrun_migrate_config() when making the change so the human-mode output shows\nmeaningful conversion details instead of the generic line.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py-84-84 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`84-84`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`list-ids` no longer renders IDs in default human mode.**\n\nAt Line 84 and Line 229, `ui.result(...)` is called without `status` and without `human_fn`. With the current UI fallback, users only get a generic status line instead of ID data.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-            ui.result({\"count\": 0, \"artifacts_scanned\": 0, \"ids\": []})\n+            ui.result({\"count\": 0, \"artifacts_scanned\": 0, \"ids\": []}, human_fn=_human_list_ids)\n@@\n-    ui.result(result)\n+    ui.result(result, human_fn=_human_list_ids)\n```\n\n```python\ndef _human_list_ids(data: dict) -> None:\n    ui.header(\"List IDs\")\n    for hit in data.get(\"ids\", []):\n        ui.substep(f\"{hit.get('id')} ({hit.get('artifact')}:L{hit.get('line')})\")\n    ui.success(f\"{data.get('count', 0)} id(s)\")\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 229-229\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py` at line 84, The UI\noutput for list-ids currently calls ui.result(...) without a human renderer or\nstatus, causing default human mode to show only a generic line; add a human\nrenderer function (e.g., _human_list_ids) that prints a header, each id with\nartifact and line, and a success summary, then update the ui.result(...) calls\nin list_ids.py (the ones creating {\"count\":..., \"ids\":[...]} responses) to pass\nstatus (e.g., \"ok\") and human_fn=_human_list_ids so human-mode displays the ID\nlist correctly.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_used.py-75-75 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`75-75`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`where-used` results are not visible in default human mode.**\n\nAt Line 75 and Line 101, payloads are sent through `ui.result(...)` without `human_fn`; fallback rendering drops `references` details.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        ui.result({\"id\": target_id, \"artifacts_scanned\": 0, \"count\": 0, \"references\": []})\n+        ui.result(\n+            {\"id\": target_id, \"artifacts_scanned\": 0, \"count\": 0, \"references\": []},\n+            human_fn=_human_where_used,\n+        )\n@@\n-    ui.result({\"id\": target_id, \"artifacts_scanned\": len(artifacts_to_scan), \"count\": len(references), \"references\": references})\n+    ui.result(\n+        {\"id\": target_id, \"artifacts_scanned\": len(artifacts_to_scan), \"count\": len(references), \"references\": references},\n+        human_fn=_human_where_used,\n+    )\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 101-101\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_used.py` at line 75, The\nui.result calls that emit {\"id\": target_id, \"artifacts_scanned\": 0, \"count\": 0,\n\"references\": []} (at the ui.result invocations in where_used.py) lack a\nhuman_fn, so the default renderer hides the references field; update both\nui.result calls (the one returning empty results and the one at the other\nlocation) to pass a human_fn that formats the references for human mode (e.g., a\nwhere_used_human or render_references function) or supply a minimal\nhuman-friendly summary string that includes references/count; ensure the\nhuman_fn accepts the same payload shape (id, artifacts_scanned, count,\nreferences) so human output shows the references details.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/get_content.py-42-45 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`42-45`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Human-mode output drops retrieved content and metadata.**\n\nAt Line 45 and Lines 88-98, `ui.result(...)` is called without a `human_fn`. In default human mode, the UI fallback only shows status/message, so users won’t see `text`, `id`, `artifact`, or line-range details.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        ui.result({\"status\": \"NOT_FOUND\", \"id\": args.id, \"inst\": args.inst})\n+        ui.result(\n+            {\"status\": \"NOT_FOUND\", \"id\": args.id, \"inst\": args.inst},\n+            human_fn=_human_get_content,\n+        )\n@@\n-        ui.result({\"status\": \"FOUND\", \"id\": args.id, \"inst\": args.inst, \"text\": content})\n+        ui.result(\n+            {\"status\": \"FOUND\", \"id\": args.id, \"inst\": args.inst, \"text\": content},\n+            human_fn=_human_get_content,\n+        )\n@@\n-    ui.result({\n+    ui.result({\n         \"status\": \"FOUND\",\n         \"id\": args.id,\n         \"text\": text,\n         \"artifact\": str(artifact_path),\n         \"start_line\": start_line,\n         \"end_line\": end_line,\n         \"kind\": artifact_meta.kind,\n         \"system\": system.name,\n         \"traceability\": artifact_meta.traceability,\n-    })\n+    }, human_fn=_human_get_content)\n```\n\n```python\ndef _human_get_content(data: dict) -> None:\n    status = data.get(\"status\")\n    if status == \"FOUND\":\n        ui.header(f\"Content: {data.get('id', '?')}\")\n        ui.info(data.get(\"text\", \"\"))\n    elif status == \"NOT_FOUND\":\n        ui.warn(f\"Not found: {data.get('id', '?')}\")\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 88-98\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/get_content.py` around lines 42 - 45,\nThe ui.result calls for the FOUND/NOT_FOUND responses are missing a human-mode\nrenderer so human-mode users won't see text/metadata; add a helper function\nnamed _human_get_content(data: dict) that renders status, id, and\ntext/artifact/line-range via ui.header/ui.info/ui.warn and then update the\nui.result(...) calls (both the initial NOT_FOUND/FOUND block and the later 88-98\nblock) to pass human_fn=_human_get_content so the human fallback shows the full\ncontent and metadata.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/SKILL.md-183-189 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`183-189`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Command reference entrypoint conflicts with the mandatory `--json` rule.**\n\nLine 185 currently shows `python3 ... cypilot.py <command> [options]`, but Line 151 requires `--json` as the first arg for agent invocation. Please align the entrypoint example.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-python3 {cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py <command> [options]\n+python3 {cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py --json <command> [options]\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/SKILL.md around lines 183 - 189, The\nentrypoint example conflicts with the rule that `--json` must be the first\nargument for agent invocation; update the \"All commands use the entrypoint\"\nexample in SKILL.md so it shows `--json` as the first arg (e.g. `python3\n{cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py --json <command>\n[options]`) or add a clarifying sentence near the `cypilot.py` entrypoint that\nagent invocations must prepend `--json` before the command; ensure references to\n`cypilot.py` and `--json` are consistent with the earlier mandatory rule.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>kits/sdlc/blueprints/CODEBASE.md-713-721 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`713-721`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Bump SDLC kit version to unblock CI.**\n\nThe blueprint changed, but CI reports the kit version was not incremented. Please bump `kits/sdlc/conf.toml` (pipeline says current=4, base=4). This is currently blocking merge.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n- version = 4\n+ version = 5\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@kits/sdlc/blueprints/CODEBASE.md` around lines 713 - 721, Update the SDLC kit\nversion in kits/sdlc/conf.toml to unblock CI: open kits/sdlc/conf.toml and\nincrement the kit version field (the version/key used by the pipeline) so it\nreflects the new blueprint change (e.g., bump the numeric value currently\nreported by the pipeline), save and commit the updated conf.toml; this ensures\nthe pipeline no longer rejects the merge due to an unchanged kit version.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/ui.py-1-255 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-255`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Add `@cpt-*` traceability markers to this new Python module.**\n\nThis file introduces command-facing behavior but does not include code-artifact traceability markers.\n\n\nAs per coding guidelines: `Implement code-artifact traceability using `@cpt-`* markers to link code to artifacts`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/ui.py` around lines 1 - 255, Add CPT\ntraceability markers as comments in this module: insert a module-level marker\n(e.g. \"@cpt-module: cypilot.utils.ui\") in the top docstring or immediately below\nit, add a class-level marker on the _UI class (e.g. \"@cpt-class: _UI\") and a\nsymbol-level marker for the exported singleton (e.g. \"@cpt-var: ui\") so\nautomated tooling can link this file and its public API; place markers as simple\ncomment lines near the docstring, above the class definition for _UI, and next\nto the ui = _UI() line.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_id_kinds.py-83-84 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`83-84`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Missing human formatter for success branches—users in default human mode see no output.**\n\nAll three success branches (lines 83, 157–162, 164–170) call `ui.result()` without a `human_fn` parameter. The fallback renderer only displays status/message fields, which these payloads don't include. Users running without `--json` won't see the kinds, counts, or template mappings.\n\nProvide a human formatter for this command to render results meaningfully in default mode.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_id_kinds.py` around lines 83 -\n84, The success branches call ui.result(...) without a human_fn, so default\nhuman renderer shows nothing; add a human formatter function in this module\n(e.g., def _human_format_kinds(result): ...) that accepts the result dict and\nreturns a readable string or list (rendering \"kinds\", \"kind_counts\",\n\"kind_to_templates\", \"template_to_kinds\", and \"artifacts_scanned\"), and pass it\nas human_fn to every ui.result(...) call in list_id_kinds.py (the branches that\ncurrently emit {\"kinds\": [], \"kind_counts\": ..., ...}) so users in default human\nmode see the kinds and counts. Ensure the formatter handles empty lists/maps\ngracefully and is referenced by name in each ui.result invocation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_defined.py-74-75 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`74-75`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`where-defined` command hides definition details in default human mode.**\n\nWithout a `human_fn` parameter, `ui.result()` falls back to printing only status and message, discarding the `definitions` payload. Users running interactively see `Status: NOT_FOUND` or `Status: FOUND` with no actual definition locations. The data is preserved in JSON mode (`--json` flag) but lost for human output.\n\nProvide a custom human formatter to display definition locations, artifact types, and line numbers for all three statuses: NOT_FOUND, FOUND, AMBIGUOUS.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_defined.py` around lines 74 -\n75, The ui.result call at the end of where_defined.py currently omits a human\nformatter so the definitions payload is hidden in interactive output; implement\na human_fn (e.g., human_formatter) and pass it to ui.result to render the\ndefinitions list for all statuses (NOT_FOUND, FOUND, AMBIGUOUS). The human_fn\nshould read the result dict's \"status\", \"definitions\" (list of objects), \"id\"\nand \"artifacts_scanned\", and produce a concise human-readable summary showing\neach definition's location/path, artifact type, and line numbers (start/end or\nsingle line) and handle the three statuses by printing \"No definitions found\"\nfor NOT_FOUND, the single location for FOUND, and a numbered list for AMBIGUOUS;\nthen call ui.result({\"status\":..., \"definitions\": ...},\nhuman_fn=human_formatter) so JSON mode remains unchanged but interactive users\nsee the details.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/ui.py-227-227 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`227-227`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Remove the extraneous f-string prefix from the constant string.**\n\nThe outer f-string on `\"Aborted\"` has no interpolation and is flagged by Ruff (`F541`), which will fail CI.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        warn(f\"Aborted\" + (f\": {message}\" if message else \"\"))\n+        warn(\"Aborted\" + (f\": {message}\" if message else \"\"))\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/ui.py` at line 227, The call to warn\ncurrently uses an unnecessary outer f-string for the constant \"Aborted\" which\ntriggers Ruff F541; remove the f-prefix from the constant and keep the\nconditional inner f-string that interpolates message so the line reads a normal\nstring concatenated with (f\": {message}\" if message else \"\"). Update the\nwarn(...) invocation referencing warn and the message variable accordingly.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/ui.py-252-252 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`252-252`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Remove the unused `is_json` alias or whitelist it to resolve the Vulture CI failure.**\n\nThe `ui.is_json` static method alias is not referenced anywhere in the codebase. The underlying `is_json_mode` function is imported directly when needed (e.g., in cli.py), bypassing this public API alias. Either document it as intentional public surface and add a Vulture whitelist, or remove it to unblock CI.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/ui.py` at line 252, Remove the unused\npublic alias to fix the Vulture CI failure: delete the static assignment is_json\n= staticmethod(is_json_mode) from ui.py (or alternatively, if you intend to keep\nit as part of the module public API, add it to the Vulture\nwhitelist/documentation). Locate the alias in ui.py (the is_json symbol\nreferencing is_json_mode) and either remove that line or add the symbol name to\nthe project's Vulture whitelist/config so the linter no longer flags it.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/cli.py-141-146 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`141-146`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Reset JSON mode explicitly to avoid sticky global state across `main()` calls.**\n\nCurrent logic only enables JSON mode. If `main()` is invoked again without `--json` in the same process, output mode can remain incorrectly enabled.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-    if \"--json\" in argv_list:\n-        argv_list.remove(\"--json\")\n-        set_json_mode(True)\n+    json_mode = \"--json\" in argv_list\n+    if json_mode:\n+        argv_list.remove(\"--json\")\n+    set_json_mode(json_mode)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/cli.py` around lines 141 - 146, The code only\nturns on JSON mode when \"--json\" is present, leaving a previously set global\nstate sticky across repeated main() invocations; to fix, explicitly reset JSON\nmode at the start of the CLI entry (call set_json_mode(False) before inspecting\nargv_list) and then, if \"--json\" in argv_list, remove it and call\nset_json_mode(True) so each main() invocation has a deterministic JSON mode;\nupdate the logic around argv_list and set_json_mode in main()/CLI initialization\naccordingly.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/SKILL.md-258-307 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`258-307`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Command examples are out of sync with the current CLI flags.**\n\nThis section currently documents options that don’t match implemented handlers (e.g., `kit install --yes`, `kit update --yes`, `init --cypilot-root`, `update --source/--force`).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested corrections</summary>\n\n```diff\n- kit install <source-path> [--dry-run] [--yes]\n+ kit install <source-path> [--force] [--dry-run]\n\n- kit update [--kit <slug>] [--dry-run] [--yes]\n+ kit update [--kit <slug>] [--force] [--dry-run]\n\n- init [--project-root <path>] [--cypilot-root <path>] [--project-name <string>] [--yes] [--dry-run] [--force]\n+ init [--project-root <path>] [--install-dir <path>] [--project-name <string>] [--yes] [--dry-run] [--force]\n\n- update [--source <path>] [--force] [--dry-run]\n+ update [--project-root <path>] [--dry-run] [--no-interactive] [--yes]\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/SKILL.md` around lines 258 - 307, The documented CLI options\nin SKILL.md are out of sync with the actual implemented flags for the commands\n(e.g., kit install, kit update, init, update); update the examples under the\nheadings (kit install, kit update, kit migrate, generate-resources, toc, info,\ninit, update) to exactly match the implemented flag names and behavior used by\nthe command handlers (e.g., replace or remove deprecated flags like --yes on kit\ninstall/update, remove/adjust --cypilot-root on init, and align update's\n--source/--force usage), verify each command example against the corresponding\ncommand implementation and tests, and ensure the usage lines and option lists\nreflect the current CLI parsing/handler signatures so the docs compile and run\nas shown.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py-1092-1115 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1092-1115`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Fix editor invocation, temp-file cleanup, and add code-artifact traceability.**\n\nThree issues in `_open_editor_for_marker` (lines 1048–1141):\n\n1. **Editor argument parsing (line 1104):** `subprocess.check_call([editor, tmp_path])` fails for editors configured with flags (e.g., `code --wait`, `nvim -f`). The entire editor string is passed as a single argv element instead of being parsed. Use `shlex.split()` to handle this correctly.\n\n2. **Unbound variable in cleanup (line 1113):** If `tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile()` fails during construction, `tmp_path` is never assigned, but the `finally` block unconditionally calls `os.unlink(tmp_path)`, raising `UnboundLocalError`. Initialize `tmp_path = None` and guard the cleanup with a conditional check.\n\n3. **Missing code-artifact traceability (line 1048):** The new helper function lacks `@cpt-*` markers required by the coding guidelines. This function participates in the kit-migrate flow and should be linked to the module's `@cpt-dod:cpt-cypilot-dod-blueprint-system-kit-migrate` artifact.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n+import shlex\n ...\n def _open_editor_for_marker(\n     key: str,\n     user_raw: str,\n     new_raw: str,\n+) -> Optional[str]:  # `@cpt-dod`:cpt-cypilot-dod-blueprint-system-kit-migrate:p1\n     \"\"\"...\"\"\"\n+    tmp_path: Optional[str] = None\n     try:\n         with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(\n             mode=\"w\",\n             suffix=\".md\",\n             prefix=f\"cypilot-migrate-{key.replace(':', '-').replace('#', '-')}-\",\n             delete=False,\n             encoding=\"utf-8\",\n         ) as tmp:\n             tmp.write(content)\n             tmp_path = tmp.name\n \n-        subprocess.check_call([editor, tmp_path])\n+        subprocess.check_call([*shlex.split(editor), tmp_path])\n \n         with open(tmp_path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n             edited = f.read()\n     except Exception as exc:\n         sys.stderr.write(f\"        editor failed: {exc}\\n\")\n         return None\n     finally:\n         try:\n-            os.unlink(tmp_path)\n+            if tmp_path:\n+                os.unlink(tmp_path)\n         except OSError:\n             pass\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py` around lines 1092 - 1115, _in\n_open_editor_for_marker: parse the editor string before invoking the process,\nfix temp-file cleanup, and add the kit-migrate traceability marker;\nspecifically, initialize tmp_path = None before the tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile\ncall, replace subprocess.check_call([editor, tmp_path]) with a call that runs\nshlex.split(editor) + [tmp_path] so flags like \"--wait\" are handled, guard the\nfinally cleanup with \"if tmp_path is not None: os.unlink(tmp_path)\" to avoid\nUnboundLocalError, and add the required\n`@cpt-dod`:cpt-cypilot-dod-blueprint-system-kit-migrate (or appropriate `@cpt-`*\ndecorator/marker) to the _open_editor_for_marker definition so the function is\nlinked to the kit-migrate artifact.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_id_kinds.py-83-84 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`83-84`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Human-mode output drops `list-id-kinds` result details.**\n\nAt Line 83 and Line 157–Line 170, `ui.result(...)` is called without a `human_fn` and without a status/message format suitable for fallback rendering. In non-JSON mode this collapses to generic status output, so `kinds`, counts, and mappings are not shown to the user.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n+def _human_dump(data: Dict[str, object]) -> None:\n+    print(json.dumps(data, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False))\n+\n@@\n-            ui.result({\"kinds\": [], \"kind_counts\": {}, \"kind_to_templates\": {}, \"template_to_kinds\": {}, \"artifacts_scanned\": 0})\n+            ui.result(\n+                {\"kinds\": [], \"kind_counts\": {}, \"kind_to_templates\": {}, \"template_to_kinds\": {}, \"artifacts_scanned\": 0},\n+                human_fn=_human_dump,\n+            )\n@@\n-        ui.result({\n+        ui.result({\n             \"artifact\": str(artifact_path),\n             \"artifact_type\": artifact_type,\n             \"kinds\": kinds_in_artifact,\n             \"kind_counts\": {k: kind_counts.get(k, 0) for k in kinds_in_artifact},\n-        })\n+        }, human_fn=_human_dump)\n@@\n-        ui.result({\n+        ui.result({\n             \"kinds\": all_kinds,\n             \"kind_counts\": {k: kind_counts.get(k, 0) for k in all_kinds},\n             \"kind_to_templates\": {k: sorted(v) for k, v in sorted(kind_to_templates.items())},\n             \"template_to_kinds\": {k: sorted(v) for k, v in sorted(template_to_kinds.items())},\n             \"artifacts_scanned\": len(artifacts_to_scan),\n-        })\n+        }, human_fn=_human_dump)\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 157-170\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_id_kinds.py\naround lines 83 - 84, The ui.result(...) calls in the list-id-kinds command\ncurrently omit a human_fn, so non-JSON/human-mode output shows only a generic\nstatus; update both early-return ui.result({\"kinds\": [], ...}) and the final\nui.result(...) (the result produced around the end of list-id-kinds) to include\na human_fn that formats and prints the kinds list, kind_counts,\nkind_to_templates, template_to_kinds, and artifacts_scanned (or alternatively\ninclude a descriptive \"message\" field) so human-mode displays the detailed\nresults; locate the two ui.result usages in the list-id-kinds command and add a\nconcise human_fn that renders the maps and counts in readable text.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_defined.py-74-75 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`74-75`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`where-defined` no longer shows definition locations in human mode.**\n\nAt Line 74–Line 75 and Line 96–Line 101, structured results are routed through `ui.result(...)` without human formatting, so users don’t get usable `definitions` detail unless JSON mode is enabled.\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 96-101\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_defined.py\naround lines 74 - 75, The code routes structured definition results through\nui.result(...) (see where_defined and the variables target_id and definitions)\nwhich suppresses human-readable definition locations; update both the \"not\nfound\" and the success branches to detect human mode (e.g., ui.mode or\nui.is_human) and emit a human-formatted output instead of raw structured JSON:\nwhen human mode is active, build a concise human-readable string/list from\ndefinitions (file path, line/column, snippet) and send that to the UI (or call\nthe UI's human output helper if available, e.g., ui.human_result/ui.print),\notherwise keep sending the structured object to ui.result for JSON mode. Ensure\nboth early-return (NOT_FOUND) and the normal-return paths use the same\nhuman-formatting logic.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_used.py-75-76 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`75-76`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`where-used` no longer emits actionable results in human mode.**\n\nAt Line 75 and Line 101, payloads are sent through `ui.result(...)` without a `human_fn`. With default fallback rendering, users won’t see the `references` details unless JSON mode is forced.\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 101-101\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_used.py\naround lines