mentions of `cypilot\n> generate`/`cypilot analyze`) to use the single chosen command name (`cpt` or\n> `cypilot`) consistently (e.g., change `cypilot init` => `cpt init`, `cypilot\n> generate` => `cpt generate`, etc.), and ensure the \"Rules Mode Summary\" table\n> and surrounding paragraphs use the same namespace everywhere so no conflicting\n> operator guidance remains.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>architecture/specs/kit/kit.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `126-136`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Keep this mirrored spec fully on canonical CLI naming.**\n> \n> Line 126 and the update command table still mix `cpt` and `cypilot`. Please standardize to one canonical form (preferably `cpt`) or mark legacy aliases explicitly.\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In `@architecture/specs/kit/kit.md` around lines 126 - 136, The spec mixes CLI\n> names (`cpt` vs `cypilot`) causing ambiguity; choose the canonical CLI name and\n> make aliases explicit: update occurrences of `cypilot` to the canonical `cpt`\n> (e.g., change `cypilot kit install`, `cypilot kit update`, `{cypilot_path}` to\n> use `cpt`/`{cpt_path}`) and, if you must preserve legacy aliases, add a\n> parenthetical note like “(legacy alias: cypilot)” next to the primary `cpt`\n> command names; ensure commands referenced in the flow (`cpt init`, `cpt kit\n> install`, `cpt generate-resources`, `cpt kit update`) and the Update modes table\n> consistently use `cpt` and that any legacy alias is marked once at the top of\n> the section.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/specs/kit/kit.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `126-136`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Standardize command namespace in this flow/update block.**\n> \n> Line 126 and the update mode table mix `cpt` and `cypilot` command forms. If `cpt` is the canonical CLI, keep this section fully `cpt` (or explicitly label `cypilot` as legacy alias) to avoid operator confusion.\n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>Proposed doc fix</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n> -1. `cpt init` / `cypilot kit install` saves kit source to `{cypilot_path}/kits/{slug}/` (reference copy)\n> +1. `cpt init` / `cpt kit install` saves kit source to `{cypilot_path}/kits/{slug}/` (reference copy)\n> \n> -| **Force** | `cypilot kit update --force` | Updates reference in `{cypilot_path}/kits/{slug}/`, overwrites all user blueprints, regenerates all outputs. User edits are discarded. |\n> -| **Additive** | `cypilot kit update` | Three-way diff: reference (`{cypilot_path}/kits/{slug}/`) vs. user blueprints vs. new kit version. User-modified sections are preserved; new markers are merged in; deleted markers stay removed. Reference is updated after merge. |\n> +| **Force** | `cpt kit update --force` | Updates reference in `{cypilot_path}/kits/{slug}/`, overwrites all user blueprints, regenerates all outputs. User edits are discarded. |\n> +| **Additive** | `cpt kit update` | Three-way diff: reference (`{cypilot_path}/kits/{slug}/`) vs. user blueprints vs. new kit version. User-modified sections are preserved; new markers are merged in; deleted markers stay removed. Reference is updated after merge. |\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/specs/kit/kit.md around lines 126 - 136, The\n> docs mix CLI names; standardize the namespace by replacing legacy occurrences of\n> \"cypilot\" with the canonical \"cpt\" (or explicitly annotate \"cypilot\" as a legacy\n> alias) across the flow and update-mode table—e.g., change \"cypilot kit install\"\n> to \"cpt kit install\", \"cypilot kit update\" to \"cpt kit update\", and ensure\n> commands like \"cpt init\" and \"cpt generate-resources\" use the same namespace\n> consistently, or add a single note at the top stating \"cypilot\" is a legacy\n> alias for \"cpt\" and keep all examples using \"cpt\".\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>src/cypilot_proxy/cli.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `286-289`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Inconsistent command reference: `cypilot update` should be `cpt update`.**\n> \n> The version mismatch notice on line 288 still uses `cypilot update`, while all other command references in this file have been updated to use `cpt`. This should be updated for consistency with the PR's CLI naming standardization.\n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🔧 Proposed fix</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n>          if cached_version != project_version:\n>              # `@cpt-begin`:cpt-cypilot-flow-core-infra-cli-invocation:p1:inst-show-update-notice\n>              sys.stderr.write(\n> -                f\"cypilot: update available ({project_version} → {cached_version}). \"\n> -                f\"Run: cypilot update\\n\"\n> +                f\"cypilot: update available ({project_version} → {cached_version}). \"\n> +                f\"Run: cpt update\\n\"\n>              )\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In `@src/cypilot_proxy/cli.py` around lines 286 - 289, Update the user-facing\n> command reference in the version-notice sys.stderr.write call so it uses the\n> standardized CLI name \"cpt\" instead of \"cypilot\"; locate the sys.stderr.write\n> statement that formats f\"cypilot: update available ({project_version} →\n> {cached_version}). Run: cypilot update\" (referencing project_version and\n> cached_version) and change the embedded command text to \"cpt update\" to match\n> the rest of the CLI.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/migrate.py (2)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `1819-1920`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n> \n> **Add tests for the new output branches to recover the coverage gate.**\n> \n> CI is currently failing per-file coverage for this module. The newly added formatter and CLI UI wiring should have direct tests for PASS/DRY_RUN/ERROR/VALIDATION_FAILED and migrate-config human rendering.\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/migrate.py` around lines 1819 - 1920,\n> Add unit tests that cover the new human output branches by mocking run_migrate\n> to return dicts for each status and asserting ui output and return codes: call\n> cmd_migrate(...) with run_migrate patched to return\n> {\"status\":\"PASS\",\"message\":...,\"warnings\":...,\"backup_dir\":...,\"cypilot_dir\":...},\n> {\"status\":\"DRY_RUN\",\"plan\":{...}},\n> {\"status\":\"ERROR\",\"message\":...,\"backup_dir\":...}, and\n> {\"status\":\"VALIDATION_FAILED\"} and verify _human_migrate_result (via\n> ui.result/human_fn) produces the expected messages and cmd_migrate returns 0 for\n> PASS/DRY_RUN/CANCELLED and 1 for others; also add a test for cmd_migrate_config\n> by patching run_migrate_config to return a sample result and asserting ui.result\n> is invoked and human rendering behaves as expected. Use test\n> fixtures/monkeypatch to replace run_migrate/run_migrate_config and capture ui\n> output rather than changing _human_migrate_result or CLI code.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> ---\n> \n> `438-453`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n> \n> **Add `--` separator before core_path in git subprocess calls to prevent option parsing of dash-prefixed values.**\n> \n> `core_path` is loaded from untrusted config JSON without validation. If it starts with `-`, git will interpret it as an option rather than a path argument. Add `--` before the path argument in both `git submodule deinit` (line 439) and `git rm` (line 453) to ensure safe parsing.\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🔧 Proposed fix</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n> -            deinit = subprocess.run(\n> -                [\"git\", \"submodule\", \"deinit\", \"-f\", core_path],\n> +            deinit = subprocess.run(\n> +                [\"git\", \"submodule\", \"deinit\", \"-f\", \"--\", core_path],\n>                  cwd=str(project_root),\n>                  capture_output=True,\n>                  text=True,\n>                  check=False,\n>              )\n> @@\n> -            subprocess.run(\n> -                [\"git\", \"rm\", \"-f\", core_path],\n> +            subprocess.run(\n> +                [\"git\", \"rm\", \"-f\", \"--\", core_path],\n>                  cwd=str(project_root),\n>                  capture_output=True,\n>                  text=True,\n>                  check=False,  # May fail if already removed by deinit\n>              )\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/migrate.py` around lines 438 - 453,\n> The git subprocess invocations that use core_path (the subprocess.run call that\n> assigns to deinit for \"git submodule deinit\" and the subsequent subprocess.run\n> for \"git rm -f\") are vulnerable if core_path begins with a dash; update both\n> calls to insert a \"--\" argument before core_path (e.g.,\n> [\"git\",\"submodule\",\"deinit\",\"-f\",\"--\", core_path] and [\"git\",\"rm\",\"-f\",\"--\",\n> core_path]) so git treats core_path as a pathname, not an option.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `907-1251`: _🛠️ Refactor suggestion_ | _🟠 Major_\n> \n> **Add `@cpt-*` traceability markers for the newly introduced helpers.**\n> \n> The new helper flow (`_resolve_agents_context`, `cmd_agents`, `_build_result`, and human renderers) is not consistently linked with `@cpt-*` begin/end markers like adjacent flow blocks.\n> \n> \n> As per coding guidelines `**/*.{js,ts,tsx,jsx,py,java,cs,go,rb,php,swift,kt,scala,rs,cpp,c,h,cc,cxx,h++,hpp}`: Implement code-artifact traceability using `@cpt-`* markers to link code to artifacts.\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py` around lines 907 - 1251,\n> The new helper functions (_resolve_agents_context, cmd_agents, _build_result,\n> _human_agents_list, _human_generate_agents_preview, _human_generate_agents_ok)\n> lack `@cpt-`* begin/end traceability markers; wrap each logical block (the shared\n> resolver, the cmd_agents command, the generate flow helpers including\n> _build_result, and the human-friendly formatter group) with appropriately named\n> `@cpt-begin`:cpt-cypilot-flow-... and matching `@cpt-end`:cpt-cypilot-flow-...\n> markers (using consistent artifact identifiers already used in nearby code,\n> e.g., cpt-cypilot-flow-agent-integration-*) so each helper block is traceable,\n> placing markers immediately before and after the function groupings to mirror\n> the style used around cmd_generate_agents.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>architecture/specs/cli.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `557-564`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Python version check text is inconsistent with the new baseline.**\n> \n> Line 563 still says `≥ 3.10`, but Line 102 defines Python 3.11+ as the requirement.\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In `@architecture/specs/cli.md` around lines 557 - 564, The \"cpt doctor\" checks\n> table still shows the Python requirement as \"≥ 3.10\" which conflicts with the\n> project baseline (Python 3.11+); update the Python version row under the \"cpt\n> doctor\" section (the table containing the \"Python version\" check) to read \"≥\n> 3.11\" or \"3.11+\" so it matches the requirement defined elsewhere (e.g., the\n> Python 3.11+ baseline at the top of the spec).\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>skills/cypilot/cypilot.clispec (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `287-297`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **`generate-agents` synopsis/options are inconsistent with behavior.**\n> \n> Line 287 implies `--agent` is required, but Line 292 says it’s optional. Also Line 292 says “init all supported agents,” and the `-y/--yes` option used by the command flow is missing from this section.\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>Suggested doc fix</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n> -SYNOPSIS: python3 scripts/cypilot.py generate-agents --agent <name> [options]\n> +SYNOPSIS: python3 scripts/cypilot.py generate-agents [--agent <name> | --openai] [options]\n> @@\n> -  --agent  <string>  Agent/IDE key (windsurf, cursor, claude, copilot, openai). Omit to init all supported agents.\n> +  --agent  <string>  Agent/IDE key (windsurf, cursor, claude, copilot, openai). Omit to target all supported agents.\n>    --openai  <boolean>  Shortcut for --agent openai (OpenAI Codex)\n> +  -y, --yes  <boolean>  Skip confirmation prompt\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In `@skills/cypilot/cypilot.clispec` around lines 287 - 297, Update the\n> generate-agents CLI synopsis/options block to match actual behavior: mark\n> --agent as optional (clarify \"Omit to init all supported agents\"), add the\n> -y/--yes flag used by the command flow, and ensure the --openai shortcut\n> description is consistent with the --agent list; specifically edit the SYNOPSIS\n> and OPTIONS entries for the generate-agents command and the option lines\n> (--agent, --openai, --root, --cypilot-root, --config, --dry-run) so they reflect\n> optionality and include the missing -y/--yes flag and its semantics.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🟠 Major comments (21)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/update.py-439-452 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`439-452`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Surface agent-regeneration failures instead of silently dropping them.**\n\n`_process_single_agent(...)` can return failure statuses (e.g., `CONFIG_ERROR`), but this block only counts updated/created files. Failed regenerations are currently invisible in the update report.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        result = _process_single_agent(\n-            agent, project_root, cypilot_dir, cfg, None, dry_run=False,\n-        )\n+        try:\n+            result = _process_single_agent(\n+                agent, project_root, cypilot_dir, cfg, None, dry_run=False,\n+            )\n+        except Exception as exc:\n+            ui.warn(f\"{agent}: regeneration failed: {exc}\")\n+            continue\n+\n+        status = result.get(\"status\")\n+        if status in {\"CONFIG_ERROR\", \"ERROR\", \"FAIL\"}:\n+            ui.warn(f\"{agent}: regeneration failed ({result.get('message', status)})\")\n+            continue\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/update.py around\nlines 439 - 452, The update loop currently only treats agents with\ncreated/updated workflows/skills as regenerated and ignores failed runs from\n_process_single_agent; change it to check the result status (e.g.,\nresult.get(\"status\")) after calling _process_single_agent and surface failures\n(for example CONFIG_ERROR) by adding the agent to a separate failures list or\ninclude a failure marker when appending to regenerated so they show in the\nupdate report; update the logic around result, wf, sk, n_changed and regenerated\nto append agents with non-success statuses (and optionally log\nresult.get(\"error\") or result) rather than silently dropping them.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/traceability-validation.md-448-449 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`448-449`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Acceptance text appears outdated for output mode expectations.**\n\nThese lines require JSON output explicitly, but the broader CLI changes introduce human + JSON modes. This criterion should be updated to avoid enforcing JSON-only behavior.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/traceability-validation.md around\nlines 448 - 449, Update the acceptance criteria for the `cpt validate` and `cpt\nvalidate --artifact <path>` items to no longer mandate JSON-only output; change\nthe text to state that the commands must produce validation reports in both\nhuman-readable and JSON modes (e.g., support a --output or --json flag to switch\nformats) and accept JSON output when invoked with the JSON mode flag, while\ndefaulting to human-friendly output otherwise. Reference the `cpt validate` and\n`cpt validate --artifact <path>` entries and ensure the wording reflects both\nmodes and how to select JSON (e.g., --json) rather than enforcing JSON-only\nbehavior.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/migrate.py-1920-1920 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1920-1920`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Add human formatter for `migrate-config` output to display conversion results.**\n\n`run_migrate_config()` returns counts and file lists, but lacks `status`/`message` fields. Without a custom human formatter, `ui.result()` falls back to generic output. Add `human_fn` parameter following the established pattern used throughout the codebase (e.g., validate.py, update.py, agents.py).\n\nThe proposed fix is correct: define `_human_migrate_config_result()` to format the data with `ui.header()`, `ui.detail()` calls for converted/skipped counts, and pass it to `ui.result()` via the `human_fn` parameter.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/migrate.py` at line 1920, Define a\nhuman formatter function named _human_migrate_config_result(result) that uses\nui.header() and ui.detail() to present converted and skipped counts and their\nfile lists (mirroring patterns in validate.py/update.py/agents.py), then call\nui.result(result, human_fn=_human_migrate_config_result) instead of\nui.result(result) in run_migrate_config so migrate-config outputs a readable\nconversion summary; ensure the formatter reads the returned counts and lists\nfrom run_migrate_config's result structure and formats them into header/detail\nblocks.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/migrate.py-1420-1422 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1420-1422`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Do not suppress non-zero `SystemExit` from agent generation.**\n\nCatching `SystemExit` unconditionally hides argument parsing errors (e.g., invalid project root) in `cmd_generate_agents`. Additionally, the function's return value (0 for success, 1 for failure) is never captured, so failures go undetected.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        except SystemExit:\n-            pass  # cmd_generate_agents may raise SystemExit on success\n+        except SystemExit as e:\n+            code = e.code if isinstance(e.code, int) else (0 if e.code in (None, \"\") else 1)\n+            if code != 0:\n+                raise RuntimeError(\n+                    f\"Agent entry point regeneration failed with exit code {code}\"\n+                )\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/migrate.py` around lines 1420 - 1422,\nThe current except block around cmd_generate_agents catches SystemExit\nunconditionally and ignores its return, which hides non-zero exits and parsing\nerrors; change the handling so you call and capture the return/exit value from\ncmd_generate_agents (e.g., assign its result to a variable like rc) and only\nsuppress SystemExit when its code is 0 while re-raising for any non-zero\nSystemExit; ensure that when cmd_generate_agents returns or exits with a\nnon-zero code you propagate that failure (set the function's return value or\nraise an appropriate exception) so failures are not silently ignored.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py-1001-1004 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1001-1004`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`agents` is reporting drift, not installed integration state.**\n\nLine 1003 uses `_process_single_agent(..., dry_run=True)`, which primarily models create/update deltas. Up-to-date integrations can be shown as “no files,” which is incorrect for a status/list command.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py` around lines 1001 - 1004,\nThe status/list command is calling _process_single_agent with dry_run=True which\nreturns create/update deltas (drift) instead of actual installed state; update\nthe loop over agents_to_process to call _process_single_agent with dry_run=False\n(or add/choose a dedicated mode/flag like status=True) so that\n_process_single_agent returns the real installed integration state for each\nagent before storing into results[agent]; ensure any downstream handling expects\nthe non-dry-run output shape.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py-1044-1050 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1044-1050`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Confirmation gating misses rename/delete operations.**\n\nLines 1044-1050 only count create/update changes. Rename/delete actions can still run in the write phase without confirmation when those counters are zero.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-    total_create = 0\n-    total_update = 0\n+    total_create = 0\n+    total_update = 0\n+    total_rename = 0\n+    total_delete = 0\n     for r in preview_results.values():\n         wf = r.get(\"workflows\", {})\n         sk = r.get(\"skills\", {})\n         total_create += len(wf.get(\"created\", [])) + len(sk.get(\"created\", []))\n         total_update += len(wf.get(\"updated\", [])) + len(sk.get(\"updated\", []))\n+        total_rename += len(wf.get(\"renamed\", []))\n+        total_delete += len(wf.get(\"deleted\", []))\n\n-    if total_create == 0 and total_update == 0:\n+    if total_create == 0 and total_update == 0 and total_rename == 0 and total_delete == 0:\n         ui.info(\"No changes needed — agent files are up to date.\")\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 1059-1061\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py` around lines 1044 - 1050,\nThe confirmation gating currently only sums created/updated counts\n(total_create, total_update) using preview_results -> wf and sk, so\nrename/delete operations can bypass confirmation; update the aggregation to also\ninclude the lengths of wf.get(\"renamed\", []) and wf.get(\"deleted\", []) and\nsk.get(\"renamed\", []) and sk.get(\"deleted\", []) when computing totals (and apply\nthe same change at the other occurrence around lines 1059-1061) so any rename or\ndelete in preview_results contributes to the confirmation counters.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/PRD.md-397-397 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`397-397`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Use a valid canonical command in the pre-commit hook requirement.**\n\nLine 397 specifies `cypilot lint`, which is inconsistent with the documented `cpt` command surface and likely not an implemented command.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested wording fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n- The system MUST provide a `cpt hook install` command that installs a git pre-commit hook running lightweight validation (`cypilot lint`) on changed artifacts before commit.\n+ The system MUST provide a `cpt hook install` command that installs a git pre-commit hook running lightweight validation (`cpt validate`) on changed artifacts before commit.\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/PRD.md at line 397, Update the PRD requirement\nto use the correct canonical command: replace the incorrect `cypilot lint`\nreference with the documented `cpt lint` (so the sentence reads that the\npre-commit hook runs `cpt lint` on changed artifacts before commit) and ensure\nthe clause still references `cpt hook install` and `cpt hook uninstall` for\ninstallation/removal consistency.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/SKILL.md-302-316 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`302-316`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`update` and `agents` command docs are out of sync with current CLI behavior.**\n\nLine 304 documents `update [--source] [--force]`, but the current parser uses `--project-root`, `--dry-run`, `--no-interactive`, and `--yes`.  \nLines 308-316 still describe `agents` as the generator command, while generation is now `generate-agents` and `agents` is status/read-only.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested doc sync patch</summary>\n\n```diff\n-#### update\n-```bash\n-update [--source <path>] [--force] [--dry-run]\n-```\n+#### update\n+```bash\n+update [--project-root <path>] [--dry-run] [--no-interactive] [--yes]\n+```\n\n #### agents\n ```bash\n-agents --agent <name> [--root <path>] [--cypilot-root <path>] [--dry-run]\n+agents [--agent <name> | --openai] [--root <path>] [--cypilot-root <path>] [--config <path>]\n ```\n-Generates agent-specific workflow proxies and skill entry points.\n+Shows generated agent integration status (read-only).\n\n+#### generate-agents\n+```bash\n+generate-agents [--agent <name> | --openai] [--root <path>] [--cypilot-root <path>] [--config <path>] [--dry-run] [--yes]\n+```\n+Generates/updates agent-specific workflow proxies and skill entry points.\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/SKILL.md` around lines 302 - 316, The docs for the CLI\ncommands are outdated: update is documented with old flags and agents is\ndescribed as a generator while the real CLI uses different flags and a separate\ngenerate-agents command. Update the SKILL.md entries for the update command to\nshow the current flags (--project-root, --dry-run, --no-interactive, --yes),\nchange the agents section to reflect that the agents command is read-only\n(supporting --agent/--openai and --root/--cypilot-root/--config), and add a new\ngenerate-agents section documenting the generator command (generate-agents with\n--agent/--openai, --root, --cypilot-root, --config, --dry-run, --yes) so the\ndocumented usage for update, agents, and generate-agents matches the actual CLI\nbehavior.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/PRD.md-242-242 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`242-242`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Align the `init` default install directory across the PRD.**\n\nLine 242 still says the default install directory is `.cypilot`, while Line 663 and Line 939 describe `cypilot`. This creates conflicting implementation requirements.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested wording fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n- The dialog MUST ask: (1) installation directory (default: `.cypilot`), ...\n+ The dialog MUST ask: (1) installation directory (default: `cypilot`), ...\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/PRD.md at line 242, Pick one canonical default\ninstall directory (either \".cypilot\" or \"cypilot\"), then update the PRD so all\nreferences use that exact string: change the `cpt init` description (currently\nline 242), the `ALWAYS open `@/`{install_dir}/config/AGENTS.md FIRST` example, and\nthe other mentions around lines 663 and 939 to the chosen value; ensure\n`{cypilot_path}`, `install_dir`, and any example CLI output (`cypilot on` /\n`cypilot help`) consistently reflect the same default and update the\nroot-system/config file paths and AGENTS.md injection examples accordingly.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DESIGN.md-1152-1152 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1152-1152`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**JSON→TOML migration table uses a TOML file as the “old” source.**\n\nLine 1152 and Line 1158 reference `.cypilot-adapter/artifacts.toml` as the source in a JSON→TOML migration flow. The old-side filename should be JSON to match the migration narrative and steps.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested wording fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `.cypilot-adapter/artifacts.toml` | `{cypilot_path}/config/artifacts.toml` | Config Manager |\n+| `.cypilot-adapter/artifacts.json` | `{cypilot_path}/config/artifacts.toml` | Config Manager |\n...\n-   - `.cypilot-adapter/artifacts.toml` migrates to `{cypilot_path}/config/artifacts.toml` (new location)\n+   - `.cypilot-adapter/artifacts.json` migrates to `{cypilot_path}/config/artifacts.toml` (new location)\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 1158-1158\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DESIGN.md at line 1152, The migration table\nentries that list the \"old\" source as `.cypilot-adapter/artifacts.toml` are\nincorrect for a JSON→TOML migration; update those table cells (the entries\nreferencing `.cypilot-adapter/artifacts.toml`, including the repeated occurrence\nnoted) so the old-side filename is `.cypilot-adapter/artifacts.json` (while\nkeeping the new-side `{cypilot_path}/config/artifacts.toml` and the component\n`Config Manager`) to accurately reflect a JSON→TOML migration.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py-1134-1150 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1134-1150`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Installed-file reporting is inaccurate in `cmd_agents` human output.**\n\nLine 1134 treats `updated` as “installed files”, but unchanged files are not included there, so up-to-date setups can be shown as “no files”.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py around\nlines 1134 - 1150, The reporting incorrectly treats wf.get(\"updated\", []) and\nsk.get(\"updated\", []) as the full set of installed files; include unchanged\nfiles when computing installed/existing counts. Update the logic in cmd_agents\nwhere existing_wf, existing_sk, created_wf, created_sk are derived (look for\nvariables existing_wf, existing_sk, created_wf, created_sk and the wf/sk dict\naccess) to also collect wf.get(\"unchanged\", []) and sk.get(\"unchanged\", []) into\nthe “existing/installed” lists, recompute total_existing and total_missing from\nthose combined lists, and adjust the ui.step/ui.substep branches so up-to-date\nagents show their unchanged files instead of “no files.”\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py-1044-1050 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1044-1050`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Preview confirmation misses rename/delete operations.**\n\nLines 1044–1050 only count create/update. If a run only renames or deletes files, Line 1059 treats it as “no changes needed”, but writes still happen in execution.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n     total_create = 0\n     total_update = 0\n+    total_rename = 0\n+    total_delete = 0\n     for r in preview_results.values():\n         wf = r.get(\"workflows\", {})\n         sk = r.get(\"skills\", {})\n         total_create += len(wf.get(\"created\", [])) + len(sk.get(\"created\", []))\n         total_update += len(wf.get(\"updated\", [])) + len(sk.get(\"updated\", []))\n+        total_rename += len(wf.get(\"renamed\", []))\n+        total_delete += len(wf.get(\"deleted\", []))\n@@\n-    if total_create == 0 and total_update == 0:\n+    if total_create == 0 and total_update == 0 and total_rename == 0 and total_delete == 0:\n         ui.info(\"No changes needed — agent files are up to date.\")\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 1059-1061\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py around\nlines 1044 - 1050, The preview_results summary only accumulates creates/updates\n(total_create, total_update) and ignores deletions/renames, so update the loop\nthat iterates preview_results.values() to also extract wf.get(\"deleted\", []) and\nwf.get(\"renamed\", []) (and the same for sk) and increment new counters (e.g.,\ntotal_delete and total_rename) or add them into a single total_changes variable;\nthen change the later check that currently treats zero of create+update as \"no\nchanges needed\" (the logic around the condition using total_create/total_update)\nto instead consider the sum of all change types (created, updated, deleted,\nrenamed) so that preview runs that only rename/delete are handled as non-empty\npreviews and will perform writes during execution. Ensure you reference\npreview_results, wf, sk, total_create, total_update, and the later conditional\nwhen making the change.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py-995-999 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`995-999`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`agents` read-only command can still copy files into the project.**\n\nLine 995 invokes shared context resolution that calls `_ensure_cypilot_local(...)`; with default flags this can write into `cypilot/` during a read-only listing command.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-def _resolve_agents_context(argv: List[str], prog: str, description: str, *, allow_yes: bool = False) -> Optional[tuple]:\n+def _resolve_agents_context(\n+    argv: List[str],\n+    prog: str,\n+    description: str,\n+    *,\n+    allow_yes: bool = False,\n+    ensure_local: bool = True,\n+) -> Optional[tuple]:\n@@\n-    cypilot_root, copy_report = _ensure_cypilot_local(cypilot_root, project_root, args.dry_run)\n+    if ensure_local:\n+        cypilot_root, copy_report = _ensure_cypilot_local(cypilot_root, project_root, args.dry_run)\n+    else:\n+        copy_report = {\"action\": \"none\", \"reason\": \"read_only\"}\n@@\n-    ctx = _resolve_agents_context(argv, prog=\"agents\", description=\"Show generated agent integration files\")\n+    ctx = _resolve_agents_context(\n+        argv,\n+        prog=\"agents\",\n+        description=\"Show generated agent integration files\",\n+        ensure_local=False,\n+    )\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 967-968\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/agents.py around\nlines 995 - 999, The agents listing path calls _resolve_agents_context which\nunconditionally triggers _ensure_cypilot_local and can write into cypilot/ even\nfor read-only commands; change _resolve_agents_context (or its callers in agents\ncommand and the other place at the 967–968 region) to accept and propagate a\nread_only/skip_ensure flag and when true avoid invoking _ensure_cypilot_local\n(or add a new parameter like ensure_local=False), so the agents command performs\na pure read-only listing without copying files into the project; update callers\nto pass the flag for the read-only \"agents\" command.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_cli_integration.py-560-560 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`560-560`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Remove unused `code` assignments (Ruff F841 blocker).**\n\n`code` is assigned but never read at these call sites, which will fail linting.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-                code = main([\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", \"--root\", str(root), \"--cypilot-root\", str(root), \"--config\", str(cfg_path)])\n+                main([\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", \"--root\", str(root), \"--cypilot-root\", str(root), \"--config\", str(cfg_path)])\n...\n-                code = main([\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", \"--root\", str(root), \"--cypilot-root\", str(root), \"--config\", str(cfg_path)])\n+                main([\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", \"--root\", str(root), \"--cypilot-root\", str(root), \"--config\", str(cfg_path)])\n...\n-                code = main([\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", \"--root\", str(root), \"--cypilot-root\", str(root), \"--config\", str(cfg_path)])\n+                main([\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", \"--root\", str(root), \"--cypilot-root\", str(root), \"--config\", str(cfg_path)])\n...\n-                code = main([\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", \"--root\", str(root), \"--cypilot-root\", str(root), \"--config\", str(cfg_path)])\n+                main([\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", \"--root\", str(root), \"--cypilot-root\", str(root), \"--config\", str(cfg_path)])\n...\n-                code = main([\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", \"--root\", str(root), \"--cypilot-root\", str(root), \"--config\", str(cfg_path)])\n+                main([\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", \"--root\", str(root), \"--cypilot-root\", str(root), \"--config\", str(cfg_path)])\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 591-591, 618-618, 646-646, 674-674\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_cli_integration.py` at line 560, The test assigns the return value\nof main(...) to an unused variable named code causing Ruff F841; remove the\nunused assignment and call main(...) directly at each site (replace \"code =\nmain([...])\" with \"main([...])\") for the occurrences in\ntests/test_cli_integration.py where main is invoked (e.g., the call with\narguments [\"generate-agents\", \"--agent\", \"windsurf\", ...] and the other similar\nmain(...) calls flagged in the comment).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_agents_coverage.py-219-230 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`219-230`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Add a traceability marker to the new helper block.**\n\nThe newly added helper is missing an `@cpt-*` traceability marker required for Python code artifacts.\n\n\n\nAs per coding guidelines: `**/*.{js,ts,tsx,jsx,py,java,cs,go,rb,php,swift,kt,scala,rs,cpp,c,h,cc,cxx,h++,hpp}`: Implement code-artifact traceability using `@cpt-`* markers to link code to artifacts.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_agents_coverage.py` around lines 219 - 230, Add a code-artifact\ntraceability marker for the new helper _with_human_mode by inserting an `@cpt-`*\nmarker (e.g. `@cpt-`<artifact-id>) as a comment or single-line docstring\nimmediately above the def _with_human_mode(...) declaration so the helper is\nlinked to its artifact; ensure the marker follows the project convention (starts\nwith `@cpt-`) and is placed adjacent to the function definition for automated\ndetection.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>architecture/DECOMPOSITION.md-336-337 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`336-337`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Use the generator command name in Agent Integration API docs.**\n\nLine 336 currently shows `cpt agents [--agent A]`, but generation flows use `cpt generate-agents ...`. This is easy to misread and execute incorrectly.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-  - `cpt agents [--agent A]`\n+  - `cpt generate-agents [--agent A]`\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@architecture/DECOMPOSITION.md` around lines 336 - 337, Update the Agent\nIntegration API docs to use the actual generator command name: replace the\nincorrect invocation `cpt agents [--agent A]` with `cpt generate-agents [--agent\nA]` in the DECOMPOSITION documentation so examples and usage match the\ngeneration flows; ensure any surrounding text that references the old `cpt\nagents` string is updated to `cpt generate-agents` for consistency.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_agents_coverage.py-464-467 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`464-467`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Strengthen this test and remove the unused variable.**\n\nLine 464 assigns `result` but never uses it (F841), and Line 467 asserts `\"SKILL.md\"` which does not verify kit-description enrichment.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-            result = _process_single_agent(\"windsurf\", root, cpt, cfg, None, dry_run=False)\n+            _process_single_agent(\"windsurf\", root, cpt, cfg, None, dry_run=False)\n             # Skill file should contain kit description\n             content = ws_skill.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n-            self.assertIn(\"SKILL.md\", content)\n+            self.assertIn(\"SDLC workflow kit\", content)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_agents_coverage.py` around lines 464 - 467, Remove the unused\nlocal variable by not assigning the return value from _process_single_agent (or\nuse it if needed) and replace the weak assertion that only checks for \"SKILL.md\"\nwith a stronger assertion that verifies the kit-description enrichment is\npresent in the skill file content; specifically, after calling\nws_skill.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\") assert that the returned content includes\nboth the expected filename marker (e.g., \"SKILL.md\") if still desired and a\nkit-description marker such as \"Kit description\" or a project-specific token\nused by the enrichment process so the test verifies actual enrichment rather\nthan only filename presence.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>architecture/DESIGN.md-1156-1159 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1156-1159`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Migration path wording regresses to legacy directory semantics.**\n\nLines 1157-1159 say migration scans `config/` directly, which conflicts with the `{cypilot_path}/config/...` model established across this document. Please align this section to the install-dir placeholder semantics so path resolution is unambiguous.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@architecture/DESIGN.md` around lines 1156 - 1159, The migration wording\ncurrently refers to scanning `config/` directly; update the Migrator (`cpt\nmigrate-config`) docs so all path mentions use the install-dir placeholder\nsemantics (e.g., `{cypilot_path}/config/` instead of `config/`) and clarify that\n`.cypilot-adapter/artifacts.toml` migrates to\n`{cypilot_path}/config/artifacts.toml`; ensure the step descriptions and\nexamples consistently use `{cypilot_path}` for source/destination resolution so\nreaders unambiguously resolve install paths for the Migrator command.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>architecture/DESIGN.md-260-260 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`260-260`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Inconsistent command name: `generate-agents` vs `agents`.**\n\nLine 260 uses `cpt generate-agents`, but the same doc’s API sections describe `agents` as the command surface. Please standardize one canonical command name across the architecture docs to avoid operator confusion and broken copy/paste flows.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@architecture/DESIGN.md` at line 260, The architecture documentation has\ninconsistent command naming where line 260 references `cpt generate-agents` but\nthe API sections of the same document describe the command surface as `agents`.\nIdentify the canonical command name that is actually implemented (either `cpt\ngenerate-agents` or `cpt agents`) and standardize all references throughout the\nDESIGN.md document to use this single consistent command name. Replace the\nconflicting command reference in the workflow registrations section to match the\ncanonical name used elsewhere in the API documentation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DECOMPOSITION.md-156-160 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`156-160`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**CLI alias migration is incomplete in this API block.**\n\nLines 156-158 still use `cypilot kit ...` while adjacent lines have moved to `cpt`. Please migrate this block fully to the canonical alias to keep DECOMPOSITION consistent with the rest of the PR.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DECOMPOSITION.md around lines 156 - 160,\nUpdate the CLI examples so the canonical alias `cpt` is used consistently:\nreplace the `cypilot kit install <path>`, `cypilot kit update [--force]`, and\n`cypilot kit migrate [--kit SLUG] [--dry-run]` entries with `cpt kit install\n<path>`, `cpt kit update [--force]`, and `cpt kit migrate [--kit SLUG]\n[--dry-run]` respectively to match the surrounding `cpt` usage and keep the\nDECOMPOSITION CLI block consistent.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DECOMPOSITION.md-336-336 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`336-336`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`agents` command name conflicts with other updated architecture docs.**\n\nLine 336 documents `cpt agents [--agent A]`, while `architecture/DESIGN.md` Line 260 now documents `cpt generate-agents`. Please reconcile to a single command name across both artifacts.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DECOMPOSITION.md at line 336, DECOMPOSITION.md\ncurrently lists the CLI as \"cpt agents [--agent A]\" which conflicts with the\nupdated name \"cpt generate-agents\" documented in DESIGN.md; pick the canonical\ncommand name (preferably \"cpt generate-agents\" to match DESIGN.md) and update\nthe DECOMPOSITION.md occurrence of \"cpt agents [--agent A]\" to \"cpt\ngenerate-agents [--agent A]\" (or vice versa if the team prefers the shorter\nname), ensuring any surrounding examples or references use the same command\nstring so both docs are consistent.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/spec-coverage.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`49-256`: **LGTM! All command references updated consistently.**\n\nThis file contains identical changes to `architecture/features/spec-coverage.md`, and all command references have been correctly updated from `cypilot` to `cpt`. The documentation changes are accurate and complete.\n\n\n\n**Consider consolidating duplicate documentation files.**\n\nBoth `architecture/features/spec-coverage.md` and `.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/spec-coverage.md` contain identical content. This duplication could lead to maintenance issues if one file is updated without the other.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggestions to reduce duplication</summary>\n\nConsider one of these approaches:\n1. Use symbolic links if one file should always mirror the other\n2. Generate one from the other during build/deployment\n3. Document clearly which is the source of truth and when each is used\n4. Consolidate to a single file if both serve the same purpose\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/spec-coverage.md around lines 49 -\n256, There are duplicate documentation files for the same content\n(spec-coverage.md) in both architecture/features/spec-coverage.md and\n.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/spec-coverage.md; pick one canonical\nsource of truth (e.g., keep architecture/features/spec-coverage.md), remove the\nduplicate, or replace the duplicate with a symlink or build-time generation\nstep, and update repo docs or CI/build scripts (or add a README note) to\ndocument which path is authoritative so future edits are made in the single\ncanonical location; ensure references to spec-coverage.md still resolve after\nconsolidation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/cli.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`314-322`: **Unused lambda parameter `d` — consider using `_` to suppress lint warning.**\n\nStatic analysis (Ruff ARG005) flags the unused lambda argument. While the code is functional, using `_` for intentionally ignored parameters is idiomatic Python and silences the warning.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n         from .utils.ui import ui\n         ui.result(\n             {\"status\": \"ERROR\", \"message\": f\"Unknown command: {cmd}\", \"available\": all_commands},\n-            human_fn=lambda d: (\n+            human_fn=lambda _: (\n                 ui.error(f\"Unknown command: {cmd}\"),\n                 ui.hint(f\"Available commands: {', '.join(all_commands)}\"),\n                 ui.hint(\"Run 'cpt --help' for usage.\"),\n             ),\n         )\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/cli.py around lines 314 -\n322, The lambda passed as human_fn to ui.result uses an unused parameter named d\nwhich triggers Ruff ARG005; update the lambda signature to use an underscore\n(e.g., lambda _: ...) to mark the parameter as intentionally ignored—locate the\nui.result(...) call in cli.py and replace the human_fn lambda parameter d with _\n(no other logic changes).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1910-1914`: **Make help-usage alias assertions migration-safe.**\n\nLines 1911 and 1924 hardcode `\"cypilot\"` in `usage`. If canonical usage is shifting to `cpt`, these tests can fail for the wrong reason. Consider asserting either alias or the expected canonical one.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        self.assertIn(\"cypilot\", out[\"usage\"])\n+        self.assertTrue(any(alias in out[\"usage\"] for alias in (\"cpt\", \"cypilot\")))\n@@\n-        self.assertIn(\"cypilot\", out[\"usage\"])\n+        self.assertTrue(any(alias in out[\"usage\"] for alias in (\"cpt\", \"cypilot\")))\n```\n</details>\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 1923-1925\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py` around lines 1910 - 1914, The test currently\nasserts that \"cypilot\" appears in out[\"usage\"] which will break if the canonical\nCLI name changes to \"cpt\"; update the assertions in\ntests/test_cli_py_coverage.py (around the checks using out, out[\"usage\"],\nout[\"commands\"], out[\"sections\"]) to accept either alias or canonical name by\nchecking for membership of either string (e.g. assert that out[\"usage\"] contains\n\"cypilot\" or \"cpt\") and apply the same dual-check approach to the other\noccurrences that hardcode \"cypilot\" so the test is m