PR-STATUS-REPORT-TEMPLATE.md`\n* `kits/sdlc/blueprints/PRD.md`\n* `kits/sdlc/conf.toml`\n* `kits/sdlc/example/codebase/rules.md`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/__init__.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/update.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/blueprint.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/codebase.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/toc.py`\n* `src/cypilot_proxy/cli.py`\n* `tests/test_kit.py`\n* `tests/test_toc.py`\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pull/85#pullrequestreview-3879230695","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pulls/85","author_association":"NONE","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pull/85#pullrequestreview-3879230695"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pulls/85"}},"submitted_at":"2026-03-02T23:03:57Z","commit_id":"f08b5eb060c1d655548d00628630bff74e6e818e"},{"id":3881158097,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOQ7VXwc7nVcXR","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 2**\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> Some comments are outside the diff and can’t be posted inline due to platform limitations.\n> \n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>⚠️ Outside diff range comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `141-144`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🔴 Critical_\n> \n> **Sanitize workflow names before using them as filenames.**\n> \n> `wf[\"name\"]` is used directly to build output paths. A crafted name (e.g. `../...` or absolute path) can escape the workflows directory and overwrite unintended files.\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🔒 Proposed fix</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n>      for wf in summary.get(\"workflows\", []):\n> -        wf_name = wf[\"name\"]\n> +        wf_name = str(wf[\"name\"]).strip()\n> +        if not re.fullmatch(r\"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*\", wf_name):\n> +            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid workflow name: {wf_name!r}\")\n>          wf_path = gen_kits_dir / kit_slug / \"workflows\" / f\"{wf_name}.md\"\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py around lines\n> 141 - 144, The code uses wf[\"name\"] directly to build wf_path which allows path\n> traversal; sanitize and validate the workflow name before using it: ensure it is\n> not absolute and does not contain parent-dir segments, derive a safe filename\n> (e.g., take only the final name component or replace/strip path separators and\n> invalid chars) and fall back to a safe default if empty; then build wf_path from\n> gen_kits_dir / kit_slug / \"workflows\" / safe_name + \".md\" so workflows cannot\n> escape the target directory (update places that reference\n> summary.get(\"workflows\", []), wf[\"name\"], and wf_path accordingly).\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`643-643`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Add `sysprompt` to singleton marker identities.**\n\nMerge identity treats singletons specially, but `sysprompt` is currently missing from this singleton set. That can cause legacy sysprompt markers to be indexed positionally and merged inconsistently.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-_SINGLETON_MARKERS = frozenset({\"blueprint\", \"skill\", \"system-prompt\", \"rules\", \"checklist\"})\n+_SINGLETON_MARKERS = frozenset({\"blueprint\", \"skill\", \"system-prompt\", \"sysprompt\", \"rules\", \"checklist\"})\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py at line 643,\nThe singleton marker set _SINGLETON_MARKERS is missing the legacy identifier\n\"sysprompt\", causing sysprompt markers to be treated positionally; update the\nfrozenset assigned to _SINGLETON_MARKERS (the variable named _SINGLETON_MARKERS\nin kit.py) to include \"sysprompt\" alongside \"blueprint\", \"skill\",\n\"system-prompt\", \"rules\", and \"checklist\" so sysprompt is handled as a singleton\nduring merge/indexing.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`946-950`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Don’t drop identity upgrades when type counts differ.**\n\nSkipping the whole type on count mismatch loses valid mappings for shared positions and can produce false inserted/deleted outcomes during merge.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n     for mtype, t_segs in text_by_type.items():\n         r_segs = ref_by_type.get(mtype, [])\n-        if len(t_segs) != len(r_segs):\n-            continue  # counts differ — can't safely map positionally\n-        for t_seg, r_seg in zip(t_segs, r_segs):\n+        limit = min(len(t_segs), len(r_segs))\n+        for idx in range(limit):\n+            t_seg = t_segs[idx]\n+            r_seg = r_segs[idx]\n             if not t_seg.explicit_id and r_seg.explicit_id:\n                 upgrade_map[id(t_seg)] = (mtype, r_seg.explicit_id)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py around lines\n946 - 950, The current code skips all segment comparisons for a type when\nlen(t_segs) != len(r_segs), dropping valid positional identity upgrades; change\nthe logic so you still examine corresponding positions up to the shorter\nsequence instead of continuing. Replace the len-check + continue with computing\nn = min(len(t_segs), len(r_segs)) and iterate over the first n elements (e.g.\nfor t_seg, r_seg in zip(t_segs[:n], r_segs[:n])) and keep the existing mapping\nlogic that sets upgrade_map[id(t_seg)] = (mtype, r_seg.explicit_id) when not\nt_seg.explicit_id and r_seg.explicit_id. Ensure extras beyond n are left\nunhandled so you don’t assume positional mapping for unmatched tails.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py:\n- Around line 1722-1729: The current logic treats any \"declined\" action as\ncontent change via has_content_changes (bp_results) but still allows a version\nbump (version_bump) to proceed even when only declined changes exist; update the\nconditional that decides whether to bump config/conf.toml so it requires a real\napplied change (e.g., actions \"merged\" or \"created\" only) rather than including\n\"declined\", and adjust the related check around the version bump handling (the\nsame block around has_content_changes, bp_results, and version_bump and the\nsubsequent code at the second occurrence shown near lines 1738-1744) so that\ndeclining migrations does not trigger updating config/conf.toml or marking\nmigrations as current.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/toc.py:\n- Around line 46-55: The fence-closing logic is too permissive: after matching\n_FENCE_RE you must verify that the remainder of the original line contains only\nwhitespace (no extra content like \"python\"). Change the match to operate on the\nraw line (e.g., use line.rstrip(\"\\n\") instead of line.strip()), and before\nreturning None as a closer in the block that compares char and length (the\nbranch that currently does \"if char == state[0] and length >= state[1]: return\nNone\"), add a check that line[m.end():].strip() == \"\" (only whitespace after the\nfence token); only treat it as a closing fence when that condition is true,\notherwise return the current state. Ensure you reference _FENCE_RE, opener,\nm.end(), char, length and state in the change.\n\n---\n\nOutside diff comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py:\n- Around line 141-144: The code uses wf[\"name\"] directly to build wf_path which\nallows path traversal; sanitize and validate the workflow name before using it:\nensure it is not absolute and does not contain parent-dir segments, derive a\nsafe filename (e.g., take only the final name component or replace/strip path\nseparators and invalid chars) and fall back to a safe default if empty; then\nbuild wf_path from gen_kits_dir / kit_slug / \"workflows\" / safe_name + \".md\" so\nworkflows cannot escape the target directory (update places that reference\nsummary.get(\"workflows\", []), wf[\"name\"], and wf_path accordingly).\n\n---\n\nDuplicate comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py:\n- Line 643: The singleton marker set _SINGLETON_MARKERS is missing the legacy\nidentifier \"sysprompt\", causing sysprompt markers to be treated positionally;\nupdate the frozenset assigned to _SINGLETON_MARKERS (the variable named\n_SINGLETON_MARKERS in kit.py) to include \"sysprompt\" alongside \"blueprint\",\n\"skill\", \"system-prompt\", \"rules\", and \"checklist\" so sysprompt is handled as a\nsingleton during merge/indexing.\n- Around line 946-950: The current code skips all segment comparisons for a type\nwhen len(t_segs) != len(r_segs), dropping valid positional identity upgrades;\nchange the logic so you still examine corresponding positions up to the shorter\nsequence instead of continuing. Replace the len-check + continue with computing\nn = min(len(t_segs), len(r_segs)) and iterate over the first n elements (e.g.\nfor t_seg, r_seg in zip(t_segs[:n], r_segs[:n])) and keep the existing mapping\nlogic that sets upgrade_map[id(t_seg)] = (mtype, r_seg.explicit_id) when not\nt_seg.explicit_id and r_seg.explicit_id. Ensure extras beyond n are left\nunhandled so you don’t assume positional mapping for unmatched tails.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>ℹ️ Review info</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defaults\n\n**Review profile**: CHILL\n\n**Plan**: Pro\n\n<details>\n<summary>📥 Commits</summary>\n\nReviewing files that changed from the base of the PR and between f08b5eb060c1d655548d00628630bff74e6e818e and f1611e049e89f6948c31e25fc035a05d03082450.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📒 Files selected for processing (16)</summary>\n\n* `.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py`\n* `.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/update.py`\n* `.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/blueprint.py`\n* `.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/toc.py`\n* `.bootstrap/.core/whatsnew.toml`\n* `.bootstrap/kits/sdlc/blueprints/PRD.md`\n* `.bootstrap/kits/sdlc/conf.toml`\n* `kits/sdlc/blueprints/PRD.md`\n* `kits/sdlc/conf.toml`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/update.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/blueprint.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/toc.py`\n* `tests/test_kit.py`\n* `tests/test_update.py`\n* `whatsnew.toml`\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pull/85#pullrequestreview-3881158097","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pulls/85","author_association":"NONE","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pull/85#pullrequestreview-3881158097"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pulls/85"}},"submitted_at":"2026-03-03T08:56:14Z","commit_id":"f1611e049e89f6948c31e25fc035a05d03082450"},{"id":3881291368,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOQ7VXwc7nV85o","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 2**\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1656-1656`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Replace Unicode minus in stderr output.**\n\nLine 1656 still uses `−`, which can fail lint and render inconsistently in terminals.\n\n \n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-                    sys.stderr.write(f\"    − {k} — removed from reference (will be deleted from config)\\n\")\n+                    sys.stderr.write(f\"    - {k} — removed from reference (will be deleted from config)\\n\")\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py at line\n1656, Replace the Unicode minus character in the stderr output with a plain\nASCII hyphen; locate the sys.stderr.write call that currently writes f\"    − {k}\n— removed from reference (will be deleted from config)\\n\" and change the \"−\" to\n\"-\" (and optionally change the em dash \"—\" to \"--\" or \"—\" consistently if\ndesired) so the string uses ASCII dashes and avoids lint/terminal rendering\nissues.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`944-950`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Avoid all-or-nothing positional ID mapping when counts differ.**\n\nLine 946-Line 947 still drops valid upgrade mappings for shared markers when one side has inserts/removals, which can destabilize identity-based merges.\n\n \n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        if len(t_segs) != len(r_segs):\n-            continue  # counts differ — can't safely map positionally\n-        for t_seg, r_seg in zip(t_segs, r_segs):\n-            if not t_seg.explicit_id and r_seg.explicit_id:\n-                upgrade_map[id(t_seg)] = (mtype, r_seg.explicit_id)\n+        # Map what we safely can even when counts differ.\n+        used_ref: set[int] = set()\n+        for t_idx, t_seg in enumerate(t_segs):\n+            if t_seg.explicit_id:\n+                continue\n+            for r_idx, r_seg in enumerate(r_segs):\n+                if r_idx in used_ref or not r_seg.explicit_id:\n+                    continue\n+                upgrade_map[id(t_seg)] = (mtype, r_seg.explicit_id)\n+                used_ref.add(r_idx)\n+                break\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py around lines\n944 - 950, The current loop over text_by_type/ref_by_type skips all mappings\nwhen counts differ, dropping valid upgrades; instead, when len(t_segs) !=\nlen(r_segs) build a best-effort positional mapping by matching segments within\nthe same mtype using a stable key (e.g., segment text/content or a hash of its\ncanonical representation) and only create entries in upgrade_map for pairs where\nt_seg.explicit_id is false and r_seg.explicit_id is true and the match is\nunique; keep the original positional zip behavior when counts are equal, but\nreplace the unconditional continue with a fallback matching pass that iterates\nt_segs and finds a single unique r_seg candidate before setting\nupgrade_map[id(t_seg)] = (mtype, r_seg.explicit_id).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/toc.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`46-57`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Restrict fence detection to CommonMark’s max 3 leading spaces.**\n\nAt Line 47, `_fence_update` matches on `stripped.lstrip()`, so 4+ indented fence-like lines are treated as real fences. That can flip fence state incorrectly and hide valid headings/separators in downstream scans.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n def _fence_update(\n     line: str, state: Optional[Tuple[str, int]],\n ) -> Optional[Tuple[str, int]]:\n@@\n-    stripped = line.rstrip(\"\\n\")\n-    m = _FENCE_RE.match(stripped.lstrip())\n+    stripped = line.rstrip(\"\\n\")\n+    # CommonMark §4.5: fence markers may be indented by at most 3 spaces.\n+    m = re.match(r\"^[ \\t]{0,3}(`{3,}|~{3,})\", stripped)\n     if not m:\n         return state\n@@\n-    if char == state[0] and length >= state[1]:\n-        if stripped.lstrip()[m.end():].strip() == \"\":\n+    if char == state[0] and length >= state[1]:\n+        if stripped[m.end():].strip() == \"\":\n             return None\n     return state\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/toc.py around lines 46\n- 57, The fence detection currently strips all leading spaces before matching\n(_FENCE_RE on stripped.lstrip()), which treats indented (4+ space) fence-like\nlines as real fences; update the _fence_update logic to enforce CommonMark’s max\n3 leading spaces rule by measuring leading spaces on stripped (use len(stripped)\n- len(stripped.lstrip(' '))) and if leading > 3 return state (no fence) before\ncalling _FENCE_RE; then proceed to match m = _FENCE_RE.match(stripped.lstrip())\nand the existing fence handling (opener, char, length, closing checks)\nunchanged.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/toc.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`119-129`: **Consider extracting a shared non-fenced line iterator to reduce drift.**\n\nThe same fence-transition loop is duplicated in many places. A small helper (e.g., yielding non-fenced `(index, line)` pairs) would centralize behavior and reduce maintenance risk when fence rules change again.\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 227-233, 295-300, 356-361, 399-404, 441-446, 549-554, 740-745\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/toc.py around lines\n119 - 129, Extract a shared generator (e.g., iter_non_fenced(lines)) that\nencapsulates the repeated fence-tracking loop which calls _fence_update,\nmaintains the local fence state and first_skipped behavior, and yields only\nnon-fenced (index, line) pairs; then replace each duplicated loop that currently\ndeclares fence: Optional[Tuple[str,int]] = None, calls _fence_update, and\ncontinues while fence is set with calls to that generator. Ensure the new helper\npreserves the exact transition behavior (treating fence change as a continue)\nand update all places in this module that perform the same pattern so they\nconsume iter_non_fenced(lines) instead of duplicating the logic.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py:\n- Around line 1238-1239: The \"upgraded\" dict entry currently uses \"upgraded or\nlist(norm_upgraded_details)\" which discards normalization-origin keys when\nmerge-time \"upgraded\" is non-empty; change it to merge both sources instead:\nproduce a combined list from \"norm_upgraded_details\" and \"upgraded\" (preserving\norder and deduplicating, e.g., by walking norm_upgraded_details then upgraded\nand keeping first occurrence) and assign that to the \"upgraded\" key while\nleaving \"upgraded_details\" as the merged dict {**norm_upgraded_details,\n**upgraded_details}.\n- Around line 780-823: Derived IDs returned by _derive_marker_id can contain\nspaces or symbols which break marker regexes (_MIG_OPEN_RE/_MIG_CLOSE_RE);\nnormalize every returned ID to a safe kebab-case token: lowercase, replace any\nsequence of non-alphanumeric characters with a single hyphen, collapse multiple\nhyphens, and strip leading/trailing hyphens (apply to the values returned from\n_toml_val and preceding_heading_id). Update _derive_marker_id to run that\nnormalization before returning for all branches (heading, id, workflow, check,\nrule, prompt/example) so all outputs are safe for tag rewriting and regex\nparsing.\n\n---\n\nDuplicate comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py:\n- Line 1656: Replace the Unicode minus character in the stderr output with a\nplain ASCII hyphen; locate the sys.stderr.write call that currently writes f\"   \n− {k} — removed from reference (will be deleted from config)\\n\" and change the\n\"−\" to \"-\" (and optionally change the em dash \"—\" to \"--\" or \"—\" consistently if\ndesired) so the string uses ASCII dashes and avoids lint/terminal rendering\nissues.\n- Around line 944-950: The current loop over text_by_type/ref_by_type skips all\nmappings when counts differ, dropping valid upgrades; instead, when len(t_segs)\n!= len(r_segs) build a best-effort positional mapping by matching segments\nwithin the same mtype using a stable key (e.g., segment text/content or a hash\nof its canonical representation) and only create entries in upgrade_map for\npairs where t_seg.explicit_id is false and r_seg.explicit_id is true and the\nmatch is unique; keep the original positional zip behavior when counts are\nequal, but replace the unconditional continue with a fallback matching pass that\niterates t_segs and finds a single unique r_seg candidate before setting\nupgrade_map[id(t_seg)] = (mtype, r_seg.explicit_id).\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/toc.py:\n- Around line 46-57: The fence detection currently strips all leading spaces\nbefore matching (_FENCE_RE on stripped.lstrip()), which treats indented (4+\nspace) fence-like lines as real fences; update the _fence_update logic to\nenforce CommonMark’s max 3 leading spaces rule by measuring leading spaces on\nstripped (use len(stripped) - len(stripped.lstrip(' '))) and if leading > 3\nreturn state (no fence) before calling _FENCE_RE; then proceed to match m =\n_FENCE_RE.match(stripped.lstrip()) and the existing fence handling (opener,\nchar, length, closing checks) unchanged.\n\n---\n\nNitpick comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/toc.py:\n- Around line 119-129: Extract a shared generator (e.g., iter_non_fenced(lines))\nthat encapsulates the repeated fence-tracking loop which calls _fence_update,\nmaintains the local fence state and first_skipped behavior, and yields only\nnon-fenced (index, line) pairs; then replace each duplicated loop that currently\ndeclares fence: Optional[Tuple[str,int]] = None, calls _fence_update, and\ncontinues while fence is set with calls to that generator. Ensure the new helper\npreserves the exact transition behavior (treating fence change as a continue)\nand update all places in this module that perform the same pattern so they\nconsume iter_non_fenced(lines) instead of duplicating the logic.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>ℹ️ Review info</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defaults\n\n**Review profile**: CHILL\n\n**Plan**: Pro\n\n<details>\n<summary>📥 Commi