details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/__init__.py` at line 18, The __version__\nassignment lacks the required `@cpt-`* traceability marker; update the string or\nadd an adjacent comment to include the repo's `@cpt-`<id> annotation (matching the\npattern used elsewhere) so the version bump in __version__ = \"v3.0.4-beta\"\ncarries the proper traceability marker; ensure the marker follows the\nestablished format and is applied consistently with other files using `@cpt-`*\n(e.g., next to __version__ or in the same file header).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/kits/sdlc/scripts/prompts/pr/adr-review.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`5-13`: **Add explicit ADR completeness check (context, decision, consequences).**\n\nThe current focus points are good, but adding an explicit “context/options/decision/consequences” check would make ADR review stricter and more consistent.\n\n\n\nBased on learnings ADR artifacts must document architecture decisions with context, options considered, decision made, and consequences.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/kits/sdlc/scripts/prompts/pr/adr-review.md around lines 5 - 13,\nAdd an explicit ADR completeness check to the checklist ensuring each ADR\ncontains \"Context\", \"Options/Alternatives\", \"Decision\", and \"Consequences\"\nsections; update the checklist referenced as {checklist} and the PR Review Focus\n(ADR) guidance so reviews validate that ADRs are module/system/service scoped,\nnot already solved in {existing_artifacts}, and formatted per the {template},\nand ensure Alternatives are genuinely distinct and Decision rationale is\ntraceable to project constraints (refer to the \"PR Review Focus (ADR)\" block and\nthe checklist entries to insert this new four-part completeness item).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/__init__.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`18-18`: **Add `@cpt-*` traceability marker around this version change.**\n\nThis Python change currently has no nearby traceability marker, which makes artifact linkage inconsistent with the rest of the tracked code paths.  \n\n\nAs per coding guidelines `**/*.{js,ts,tsx,jsx,py,java,cs,cpp,c,go,rb,php}`: Use `@cpt-`* markers to establish code-to-artifact traceability.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/__init__.py at line 18, This\nchange needs an `@cpt-`* traceability marker added around the version constant;\nlocate the __version__ = \"v3.0.4-beta\" assignment and add a Python comment\nmarker pair immediately before and after it (for example \"# `@cpt-v3.0.4-beta`\nSTART\" and \"# `@cpt-v3.0.4-beta` END\") so the version change is bracketed by\n`@cpt-`* tags and consistent with project traceability conventions.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/kits/sdlc/scripts/prompts/pr/prd-review.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`5-15`: **Make mandatory PRD dimensions explicit in the focus list.**\n\nPlease add explicit checks for actors, problem statement, FR, NFR, use cases, and success criteria so reviews don’t miss core PRD completeness dimensions.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested patch</summary>\n\n```diff\n Focus on:\n - Completeness and clarity of requirements\n - Testability and acceptance criteria\n - Traceability to business goals\n+- Presence and quality of: actors, problem statement, FR, NFR, use cases, and success criteria\n - Compliance with `{template}` template\n - Ensure requirements are aligned with the best industry standard practices in `{project_domain}`\n - Ensure this problem is not already solved by other existing PRDs in `{existing_artifacts}`\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on learnings: PRD artifacts must define product intent with actors, problems, functional requirements (FR), non-functional requirements (NFR), use cases, and success criteria.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/kits/sdlc/scripts/prompts/pr/prd-review.md around lines 5 - 15,\nUpdate the PRD focus checklist to explicitly require the core PRD dimensions:\nadd items \"Actors\", \"Problem statement\", \"Functional requirements (FR)\",\n\"Non-functional requirements (NFR)\", \"Use cases\", and \"Success criteria\" to the\nexisting focus list (the block that currently mentions `{template}`,\n`{project_domain}`, and `{existing_artifacts}`); ensure each new item is phrased\nas a mandatory review check so reviewers cannot skip them and keep consistency\nwith the existing bullets and placeholders.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>kits/sdlc/scripts/prompts/pr/prd-review.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`5-17`: **Strengthen PRD review prompt with required artifact structure and rule-source anchoring.**\n\nAdd explicit PRD structure checks (actors/problem/FR/NFR/use cases/success criteria) and direct reviewers to validate against generated artifact rules to reduce drift across reviews.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested patch</summary>\n\n```diff\n Focus on:\n - Completeness and clarity of requirements\n - Testability and acceptance criteria\n - Traceability to business goals\n+- Presence and quality of: actors, problem statement, FR, NFR, use cases, and success criteria\n - Compliance with `{template}` template\n - Ensure requirements are aligned with the best industry standard practices in `{project_domain}`\n - Ensure this problem is not already solved by other existing PRDs in `{existing_artifacts}`\n@@\n Use `{checklist}` as the structured review guide.\n+Also validate against `{cypilot_path}/.gen/kits/sdlc/artifacts/PRD/rules.md` when applicable.\n Pay attention to the \"PR Review Focus (Requirements)\" section at the end of the checklist.\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on learnings: PRD artifacts must define product intent with actors, problems, functional requirements (FR), non-functional requirements (NFR), use cases, and success criteria.  \nBased on learnings: Follow validation rules defined in {cypilot_path}/.gen/kits/sdlc/artifacts/{KIND}/rules.md for each artifact kind (PRD, ADR, DESIGN, DECOMPOSITION, FEATURE).\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@kits/sdlc/scripts/prompts/pr/prd-review.md` around lines 5 - 17, The current\nPRD review prompt lacks explicit artifact-structure checks and rule-source\nanchoring; update the prompt text to require reviewers to verify PRDs include\nactors, problem statement, functional requirements (FR), non-functional\nrequirements (NFR), use cases, and success criteria, and instruct reviewers to\nvalidate each artifact against the generated rules at\n{cypilot_path}/.gen/kits/sdlc/artifacts/{KIND}/rules.md; ensure the prompt\nreferences the existing {checklist} and the \"PR Review Focus (Requirements)\"\nsection, mandates splitting requirements by topic with 1-10 ratings, and\npreserves placeholders like {template}, {project_domain}, and\n{existing_artifacts} so reviewers check for template compliance and duplication.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/blueprint.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1307-1307`: **Add a mismatch guard before overriding parsed kit metadata.**\n\nLine 1307 forces caller kit context, which is good, but it can silently hide misfiled blueprints. Consider surfacing a warning/error when parsed `@cpt:blueprint.kit` conflicts with `kit_slug`.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested patch</summary>\n\n```diff\n         bp = parse_blueprint(bp_file)\n-        bp.kit_slug = kit_slug  # kit slug comes from caller, not blueprint TOML\n+        if bp.kit_slug and bp.kit_slug != kit_slug:\n+            errors.append(\n+                f\"{bp_file}: blueprint kit '{bp.kit_slug}' does not match target kit '{kit_slug}'\"\n+            )\n+        bp.kit_slug = kit_slug  # kit slug comes from caller, not blueprint TOML\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/blueprint.py at line\n1307, The code unconditionally sets bp.kit_slug = kit_slug, which can silently\nhide a mismatch between the caller-provided kit_slug and any parsed blueprint\nmetadata (parsed `@cpt`:blueprint.kit); update the logic around bp.kit_slug to\nfirst compare the parsed metadata value (e.g.,\nbp.metadata.get(\"cpt:blueprint.kit\") or similar) with the incoming kit_slug and\nif they differ, emit a clear warning (or raise an error based on config) that\nreports both values and the blueprint identifier before overriding; keep the\noverride only after logging/handling the mismatch so misfiled blueprints are\nsurfaced (refer to bp.kit_slug and the parsed `@cpt`:blueprint.kit metadata).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.gen/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`4-6`: **Same misleading comment as source file.**\n\nThis is the generated kit default at `.gen/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml`, but the comment on line 5 says \"user-editable, this file\". The user-editable copy would be at `{cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml`.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested comment fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n # Resolution order:\n-#   1. {cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml   (user-editable, this file)\n+#   1. {cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml   (user-editable)\n #   2. {cypilot_path}/.gen/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml  (default)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.gen/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml around lines 4 - 6, Update\nthe misleading comment in the generated default file\n`.gen/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml` so it does not claim “user-editable,\nthis file”; instead clearly state this is the generated default and point users\nto the actual user-editable location `{cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml`.\nEdit the comment block that currently reads \"1.\n{cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml   (user-editable, this file)\" to something\nlike \"1. {cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml   (user-editable) — this file is a\ngenerated default\" so the distinction between the generated default and the\neditable config is explicit.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`4-6`: **Misleading resolution order comment.**\n\nThe comment on line 5 says \"user-editable, this file\" but this file is located at `.bootstrap/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml`, not at `{cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml`. Additionally, the resolution order lists `.gen/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml` as position 2, but this file is in `kits/sdlc/scripts/` (bootstrap source), not `.gen/`.\n\nConsider updating the comment to accurately reflect this file's role as the source template that gets copied to `.gen/` during kit installation.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested comment fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n # Resolution order:\n-#   1. {cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml   (user-editable, this file)\n-#   2. {cypilot_path}/.gen/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml  (default)\n+#   1. {cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml   (user-editable)\n+#   2. {cypilot_path}/.gen/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml  (kit default, this file's target)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml around lines 4 - 6, Update the\nmisleading comment block that begins with \"Resolution order:\" so it correctly\ndescribes that this file (.bootstrap/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml) is the\nsource template that gets copied into the generated location, and that the\nactual user-editable config is {cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml or the file\nunder .gen/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml; specifically change the two listed\npaths so the order reflects (1) {cypilot_path}/config/pr-review.toml\n(user-editable), (2) .gen/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml (generated copy), and\n(3) .bootstrap/kits/sdlc/scripts/pr-review.toml (template source), and update\nthe parenthetical note from \"user-editable, this file\" to indicate this file is\nthe template source copied into .gen/ during kit installation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/blueprint.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1306-1308`: **Validate declared kit mismatch before overriding `bp.kit_slug`.**\n\nLine 1307 currently masks any conflicting kit metadata from the blueprint. Guarding mismatches prevents silent cross-kit contamination.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🛠️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n         bp = parse_blueprint(bp_file)\n-        bp.kit_slug = kit_slug  # kit slug comes from caller, not blueprint TOML\n+        parsed_kit = bp.kit_slug.strip()\n+        if parsed_kit and parsed_kit != kit_slug:\n+            errors.append(\n+                f\"{bp_file}: blueprint kit '{parsed_kit}' does not match expected kit '{kit_slug}'\"\n+            )\n+            continue\n+        bp.kit_slug = kit_slug  # caller-owned source of truth\n         all_blueprints.append(bp)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/blueprint.py` around lines 1306 - 1308,\nThe code currently unconditionally overwrites the blueprint's declared kit slug\n(bp.kit_slug) with the caller-provided kit_slug, risking silent cross-kit\ncontamination; update the logic in the block that calls parse_blueprint to first\ncheck if bp.kit_slug is present and differs from kit_slug, and if so raise an\nerror or log and skip/abort (depending on project policy) instead of\noverwriting; only assign bp.kit_slug = kit_slug when there is no declared kit or\nthey match, and ensure any failure uses the same error/logging pathway as other\nblueprint validation before appending to all_blueprints.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_agents_coverage.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`213-217`: **Use a temp-path miss instead of an absolute “nonexistent” path.**\n\n`Path(\"/nonexistent/file.json\")` is environment-dependent; a missing file under `TemporaryDirectory()` is more deterministic.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Proposed tweak</summary>\n\n```diff\n     def test_nonexistent_file_returns_none(self):\n         from cypilot.commands.agents import _load_json_file\n-\n-        self.assertIsNone(_load_json_file(Path(\"/nonexistent/file.json\")))\n+        with TemporaryDirectory() as tmpdir:\n+            missing = Path(tmpdir) / \"does-not-exist.json\"\n+            self.assertIsNone(_load_json_file(missing))\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_agents_coverage.py` around lines 213 - 217, Replace the\nenvironment-dependent Path(\"/nonexistent/file.json\") in\ntest_nonexistent_file_returns_none with a deterministic missing path under a\ntemporary directory: create a TemporaryDirectory (or use pytest's tmp_path\nfixture), build a Path inside it that is guaranteed not to exist (e.g.,\nPath(tmpdir) / \"file.json\"), and assert that\ncypilot.commands.agents._load_json_file on that path returns None; update the\ntest to use TemporaryDirectory or tmp_path and reference the test method name\ntest_nonexistent_file_returns_none and the function _load_json_file when making\nthe change.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/kit.py:\n- Around line 1158-1164: The call to process_kit in cmd_kit_migrate is\nswallowing exceptions and still marking the operation as \"PASS\"; wrap the\nprocess_kit call in a try/except around both occurrences (the block at summary,\n_errors = process_kit(...) and the similar block at 1168-1173), catch Exception\nas err, set result[\"status\"] = \"FAIL\", populate result[\"regenerated\"] with an\n\"error\" field containing str(err) (and optionally any _errors returned), and log\nthe exception via the existing logger so failures are reported and visible\ninstead of silently passing.\n- Around line 624-628: The heading marker key is too coarse (only level +/-\npositional index) and should include a stable identifier so reordering or\ninsertions don't remap markers; update the branch that handles marker_type ==\n\"heading\" (the block using _toml_val(\"level\") and returning f\"heading:L{level}\"\n/ \"heading\") to incorporate a deterministic, stable token derived from the\nheading content (e.g., slugified or hashed value obtained via _toml_val(\"text\")\nor the heading/title field) into the key (for example heading:L{level}:{slug} or\nheading:{slug}), and apply the same change to the other heading-handling block\nreferenced around lines 696-705 so both