hy) otherwise\na safe fallback Path() or Path(name); i.e., set path=resolved_path or\n(Path(src_entry.path) if src_entry.path else Path()) while keeping name, role,\nreachable=False and the error message referencing src_entry.path or indicating\nno path.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>requirements/workspace.md-68-69 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`68-69`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Clarify how a standalone workspace becomes discoverable.**\n\nThese sections currently conflict: the file is described as discoverable from a parent/super-root, but the discovery rules later say only `config/core.toml.workspace` is consulted and parent traversal is disabled. As written, the `workspace-init` parent-dir flow is incomplete unless it also writes a `workspace = \"...\"` reference into each participating repo.\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 141-146, 399-400\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@requirements/workspace.md` around lines 68 - 69, The docs conflict about\nworkspace discovery: clarify how a standalone workspace file becomes\ndiscoverable by specifying that either (A) placing the workspace file at the\nproject root makes it discoverable by the discovery algorithm, or (B) if using\nworkspace-init parent-dir flow you must also write a `workspace = \"...\"`\nreference into each participating repo so they point to the standalone\nworkspace; update the text around the phrases \"project root\", \"workspace-init\",\nand the rule that only `config/core.toml.workspace` is consulted (and parent\ntraversal is disabled) to explicitly describe this behavior and the required\n`workspace = \"...\"` backlink so the two sections (lines around 68-69, 141-146,\n399-400) are consistent.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/requirements/workspace.md-68-69 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`68-69`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Clarify the standalone discovery contract in the shipped spec too.**\n\nThis copy repeats the same contradiction: the file sounds auto-discoverable from a super-root, but the spec later says discovery only follows `config/core.toml.workspace`. Without an explicit reference-writing step in `workspace-init`, the documented parent-dir flow cannot work.\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 141-146, 399-400\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/requirements/workspace.md around lines 68 - 69, The\ndocument currently contradicts the discovery behavior: either describe the\nworkspace discovery as a standalone contract (a discoverable workspace file\nusable from a project super-root) or require discovery to follow the specific\npointer in config/core.toml.workspace; decide and make the spec consistent.\nUpdate the prose to explicitly state the chosen contract, and if keeping the\nparent-dir flow, add a concrete reference-writing step to workspace-init that\nwrites or registers a config/core.toml.workspace pointer so the discovery\nalgorithm can find child repos; otherwise remove the parent-dir/autodiscover\nwording and point readers to config/core.toml.workspace as the sole discovery\nmechanism. Ensure you update the sections that repeat this claim (the current\nintro, the later paragraph describing discovery, and the workspace-init section)\nso they all reference the same behavior (either autodiscoverable file or\nconfig/core.toml.workspace pointer) and mention the exact symbol names\nworkspace-init and config/core.toml.workspace.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>README.md-279-289 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`279-289`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Document workspace commands through `cpt`, not the internal script path.**\n\nThese examples assume a `cypilot/.core/...` tree exists under the current directory. That breaks the parent-workspace flow and any project using a non-default install dir; the public docs should use `cpt`/`cypilot` instead.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🛠️ Proposed doc fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n- python3 cypilot/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py workspace-init\n+ cpt workspace-init\n\n- python3 cypilot/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py workspace-add --name docs --path ../docs-repo --role artifacts\n- python3 cypilot/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py workspace-add --name shared-kits --path ../shared-kits --role kits\n+ cpt workspace-add --name docs --path ../docs-repo --role artifacts\n+ cpt workspace-add --name shared-kits --path ../shared-kits --role kits\n\n- python3 cypilot/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py workspace-add --inline --name docs --path ../docs-repo\n+ cpt workspace-add --inline --name docs --path ../docs-repo\n\n- python3 cypilot/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py validate\n+ cpt validate\n\n- python3 cypilot/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py validate --local-only\n+ cpt validate --local-only\n\n- python3 cypilot/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py where-defined --id cpt-myapp-req-001\n+ cpt where-defined --id cpt-myapp-req-001\n\n- python3 cypilot/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py list-ids --source docs-repo\n+ cpt list-ids --source docs-repo\n\n- python3 cypilot/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py workspace-info\n+ cpt workspace-info\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 302-317\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@README.md` around lines 279 - 289, The README examples call the internal\nscript path (python3 cypilot/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py) which\nbreaks non-default installs; update all examples that run workspace-init and\nworkspace-add (including the inline variant) to use the public CLI (cpt or\ncypilot) instead — e.g., replace invocations of python3 cypilot/.core/...\ncypilot.py workspace-init and workspace-add --name/--path/--role/--inline with\ncpt workspace-init and cpt workspace-add (or cypilot workspace-init / cypilot\nworkspace-add) so docs reference the public entrypoint consistently (also apply\nthe same change to the other occurrences mentioned).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py-92-126 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`92-126`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Default discovery misses the common multi-repo layout.**\n\n`scan_root` defaults to the current repo, and `_scan_nested_repos()` only walks direct children. Running `workspace-init` inside `/repos/docs` therefore never finds sibling repos like `/repos/api`, and anything deeper than one level under the scan root is skipped as well.\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 244-246\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py` around lines 92 -\n126, The discovery only lists direct children of scan_root (scan_root.iterdir())\nso running workspace-init from a subrepo (e.g., /repos/docs) misses sibling\nrepos and deeper nested repos; change _scan_nested_repos to perform a recursive\nwalk (or use scan_root.rglob / os.walk) instead of a single iterdir(), skipping\nhidden dirs and non-directories, and ensure scan_root is normalized to the\nworkspace root when possible (e.g., climb parents until a repo/workspace marker\nor accept an explicit workspace root); update the loop that currently calls\n_is_project_dir, _find_adapter_path, _compute_source_path, and _infer_role to\noperate on the recursively discovered directories so discovered[entry.name]\nincludes all nested repos and siblings.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py-46-63 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`46-63`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`--source` currently resolves artifacts from the wrong root and drops workspace context.**\n\nRelative `--artifact` paths are resolved from the caller’s cwd before the selected source is applied, and both branches then replace `WorkspaceContext` with a plain `CypilotContext`. That makes `validate --source docs --artifact architecture/PRD.md` hit the primary repo (or fail) and also bypass later workspace-only logic like `get_all_artifact_ids()`.\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 118-137\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py-180-205 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`180-205`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`_write_standalone()` does not register the workspace for discovery.**\n\nThe `find_workspace_config()` function discovers workspaces only via the `workspace` key in `core.toml` (lines 303-308 in workspace.py). It has no fallback to probe `.cypilot-workspace.toml` directly. After `_write_standalone()` creates the file, it remains invisible to `workspace-info`, `workspace-add`, and other workspace-aware commands until the user manually adds a `workspace` key to `core.toml` (either as a string path or by migrating to inline mode).\n\n`_write_standalone()` must update `core.toml` with `workspace = \"<path-to-.cypilot-workspace.toml>\"` to make the newly created workspace discoverable.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py` around lines 180 -\n205, _write_standalone currently writes .cypilot-workspace.toml but does not\nregister it for discovery; update _write_standalone to also open or create the\nrepo/core.toml (use the same config utilities used elsewhere), set or update the\nworkspace key to the relative or absolute path of the newly created\n.cypilot-workspace.toml (e.g., workspace = \"<path>\"), and persist core.toml\nbefore returning so find_workspace_config will discover the workspace; reference\n_write_standalone (writer) and find_workspace_config (reader) to ensure you\nmodify the same core.toml structure and use the same toml utilities used\nelsewhere in this module.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_add.py-79-96 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`79-96`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Don't coerce malformed inline workspace state into a new table or overwrite an existing source.**\n\nIf `workspace` or `workspace.sources` has the wrong type, this code resets it to `{}` and then always assigns `args.name`. A malformed config or repeated `workspace-add` can therefore erase the existing source map instead of surfacing an error.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Add shape checks before mutating the table</summary>\n\n```diff\n     ws = existing.get(\"workspace\")\n     if isinstance(ws, str):\n         print(_json_error(\"Workspace is defined as external file reference. Run without --inline to add to standalone file.\"))\n         return 1\n\n-    if not isinstance(ws, dict):\n+    if ws is None:\n         ws = {\"sources\": {}}\n-    if not isinstance(ws.get(\"sources\"), dict):\n-        ws[\"sources\"] = {}\n+    elif not isinstance(ws, dict):\n+        print(_json_error(f\"Invalid workspace section in {config_path} (expected table)\"))\n+        return 1\n+    elif \"sources\" in ws and not isinstance(ws.get(\"sources\"), dict):\n+        print(_json_error(f\"Invalid workspace.sources in {config_path} (expected table)\"))\n+        return 1\n+    else:\n+        ws.setdefault(\"sources\", {})\n+\n+    if args.name in ws[\"sources\"]:\n+        print(_json_error(f\"Source '{args.name}' already exists\"))\n+        return 1\n\n     source_entry: dict = {\"path\": args.path}\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_add.py` around lines 79 -\n96, The code currently coerces a malformed existing[\"workspace\"] or\nexisting[\"workspace\"][\"sources\"] into dicts and unconditionally overwrites a\nsource; instead validate shapes and bail with errors: check\nexisting.get(\"workspace\") and if it's a str or not a dict return a JSON error\n(don't reset ws), verify ws.get(\"sources\") is a dict and if not return an error\n(don't replace it), and before assigning ws[\"sources\"][args.name] ensure\nargs.name is not already present to avoid overwriting an existing source; use\nthe existing symbols ws, existing, args.name, source_entry and return non-zero\non error.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_add.py-159-167 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`159-167`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Abort on workspace-discovery errors before writing inline config.**\n\n`find_workspace_config()` returns `(None, error)` for unreadable or invalid workspace files. Ignoring that error here lets `workspace-add --inline` create a second workspace definition next to a broken standalone config, which hides the real failure and breaks the single-workspace invariant.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Propagate the discovery failure first</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        existing, _ = _find_ws(project_root)\n+        existing, ws_err = _find_ws(project_root)\n+        if ws_err:\n+            print(_json_error(ws_err))\n+            return 1\n         if existing is not None and not existing.is_inline:\n             print(_json_error(\n                 f\"Standalone workspace already exists at {existing.workspace_file}. \"\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_add.py` around lines 159 -\n167, The code ignores errors from find_workspace_config (aliased _find_ws) and\nproceeds to call _add_to_inline even when the second return value indicates a\ndiscovery failure; update the flow in the workspace_add handler to check the\ntuple returned by _find_ws(project_root) for a non-None error, and if present\nprint a descriptive JSON error (using _json_error) and return a non-zero status\ninstead of calling _add_to_inline; only call _add_to_inline when the discovery\nerror is None and existing is either None or inline.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/cypilot.clispec-785-795 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`785-795`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`workspace-info` is documented with the wrong contract.**\n\nThe implementation returns `WORKSPACE_FOUND`/`NO_WORKSPACE`, exits `0` when no workspace exists, and emits `workspace_location` plus a list of source objects. This section still advertises status `OK`, exit code `1` for “no workspace config”, `workspace_file`, and `sources` as a mapping, so agents using the spec will be misled.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/cypilot.clispec` around lines 785 - 795, The spec for the\nworkspace-info command is out of sync with the implementation: update the\ndocumented contract in skills/cypilot/cypilot.clispec to match the actual\nbehavior by (1) replacing documented status values \"OK\"/\"NO_WORKSPACE\" with the\nimplemented \"WORKSPACE_FOUND\"/\"NO_WORKSPACE\", (2) document that the command\nexits 0 even when no workspace exists (instead of exit code 1), (3) rename the\noutput key workspace_file to the implemented workspace_location, and (4) change\nsources from a mapping to a list/array of source objects (each with path, role,\nreachable, adapter, artifact_count) and note that the implementation emits\nworkspace_location plus a list of source objects; make these exact name changes\nso agents consuming workspace-info conform to the implementation.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py-46-63 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`46-63`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Keep the selected source when `resolve_adapter_context()` returns `None`.**\n\nThe no-adapter fallback currently leaves `ctx` as a `WorkspaceContext` but does not preserve `args.source`. The later loops still walk unfiltered `meta`, so `validate --source remote` validates the whole workspace—or rejects a remote `--artifact`—instead of validating that source with primary-adapter rules.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py` around lines 46 - 63,\nWhen resolve_adapter_context(sc) returns None we currently leave ctx as the\nWorkspaceContext but lose the intent to restrict validation to args.source;\npreserve the selected source on the context so downstream validation can honor\nit. After computing sc and adapter_ctx in validate.py (the block handling\nargs.source), if adapter_ctx is None set a property on ctx (for example\nctx.selected_source or ctx.source_name = args.source or ctx.source_obj = sc) so\nlater code that iterates meta can detect this filter and only validate that\nsource; if adapter_ctx is not None keep assigning ctx = adapter_ctx as before.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_info.py-56-66 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`56-66`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Don’t hide adapter metadata failures in the bootstrap copy.**\n\n`load_artifacts_meta()` can fail via its `err` return or by raising. This code ignores `err` and swallows exceptions, so a broken source adapter can still be reported as reachable with `adapter_found: true`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n def _enrich_with_artifact_counts(info: dict, adapter_dir: Path) -> None:\n     \"\"\"Add artifact/system counts to source info dict.\"\"\"\n     try:\n         from ..utils.artifacts_meta import load_artifacts_meta\n\n         meta, err = load_artifacts_meta(adapter_dir)\n-        if meta and not err:\n+        if err:\n+            info[\"metadata_error\"] = str(err)\n+            return\n+        if meta:\n             info[\"artifact_count\"] = sum(1 for _ in meta.iter_all_artifacts())\n             info[\"system_count\"] = len(meta.systems)\n-    except Exception:\n-        pass\n+    except Exception as exc:\n+        info[\"metadata_error\"] = str(exc)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_info.py\naround lines 56 - 66, The helper _enrich_with_artifact_counts currently swallows\nerrors from load_artifacts_meta; change it to check the returned err and handle\nexceptions explicitly: call load_artifacts_meta(adapter_dir), and if err is\ntruthy or meta is falsy, set info[\"artifact_count\"]=0, info[\"system_count\"]=0,\nset info[\"adapter_found\"]=False (or add info[\"adapter_error\"]=str(err)), and\nreturn; also wrap the call in a try/except Exception as e where the except sets\nartifact/system counts to 0, sets info[\"adapter_found\"]=False and sets\ninfo[\"adapter_error\"]=str(e) so metadata failures are visible instead of being\nsilently ignored. Ensure you reference load_artifacts_meta and\niter_all_artifacts when computing counts and only iterate when meta is valid.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py-171-184 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`171-184`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Carry the owning adapter context with each workspace artifact.**\n\n`resolve_artifact_path()` chooses the right file, but every later lookup (`ctx.kits`, `registered_systems`, `known_kinds`, `constraints_path`) still comes from the single `ctx` selected before the loop. In whole-workspace validation, a remote source with its own adapter is therefore checked against the wrong kit/config.\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 233-258\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py` around lines 171 - 184,\nThe workspace validation loop stores artifact paths but drops the owning\nworkspace/adapter context causing later lookups (ctx.kits, registered_systems,\nknown_kinds, constraints_path) to use the wrong context; when iterating\nmeta.iter_all_artifacts() (in the block using WorkspaceContext and\nresolve_artifact_path) include the owning context (e.g., the WorkspaceContext\ninstance or its adapter reference) in artifacts_to_validate tuples\n(artifact_path, template_path, kind, traceability, system_node.kit, ctx) and\nupdate the later validation code (the other loop around lines 233-258) to unpack\nand use that per-artifact context for all kit/config/constraints lookups instead\nof the outer `ctx`. Ensure both places that build artifacts_to_validate and\nlater consume it are updated consistently.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py-2982-3133 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`2982-3133`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**This test scaffolding currently trips the lint gate.**\n\nThe fake types in these blocks pack class members onto one line and use shared mutable class attributes (`[]`, `{}`, `set()`), which Ruff is already flagging with E701/E702/RUF012. The tests may pass, but CI can still fail here, and the shared state makes these helpers easier to leak across cases. Please expand them to normal multi-line definitions and initialize mutable state per instance.\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 3152-3577, 3856-4025\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py` around lines 2982 - 3133, Several test helper\nclasses (e.g., _FakeMeta, _FakeInnerCtx, _FakePrimary, _KP, _SN, _AM, _Meta,\n_Prim, _Ctx) are defined with single-line member assignments and shared mutable\nclass attributes like []/{} which trips lint and risks state leakage; change\neach helper to a normal multi-line class with an __init__ that initializes\nmutable fields (systems, kits, registered_systems, _errors, etc.) as instance\nattributes, expand single-line methods (e.g., is_cypilot_format,\nget_template_path, iter_all_artifacts, get_known_id_kinds) into proper def\nblocks, and remove any class-level mutable defaults so each test gets an\nindependent instance state.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py-246-252 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`246-252`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Respect `SourceContext.role` before aggregating remote artifacts.**\n\nThese paths currently treat every reachable source as artifact-bearing. A source declared as `codebase` or `kits` will still contribute registered systems, artifact IDs, and scan targets, which breaks the workspace manifest contract and can skew `list-ids`, `where-defined/used`, and cross-repo validation results.\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 295-302, 463-470, 540-553\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py` around lines 246 - 252, The\naggregation currently treats every reachable SourceContext as a contributor;\nchange get_all_registered_systems (and the other aggregation points that collect\nartifact IDs and scan targets) to respect SourceContext.role by only aggregating\ndata from sources whose role indicates they actually provide that artifact type\n(e.g., only include registered_systems from sources with role == \"systems\" or a\nrole that permits systems), i.e., inside the loop over self.sources.values() add\na role check (and keep the existing reachable and non-empty checks) before\nupdating the accumulator so remote codebase/kits sources no longer contribute\ninappropriate entries.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py-303-359 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`303-359`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**This bootstrap copy never discovers the standalone workspace manifest.**\n\n`find_workspace_config()` only loads a workspace when project config contains a `workspace` key. A repo that just adds the documented `.cypilot-workspace.toml` beside the project root will still be treated as single-repo mode here.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py around\nlines 303 - 359, find_workspace_config currently only respects a \"workspace\" key\nin load_project_config output and ignores a standalone workspace manifest; add\nlogic (after reading cfg and before returning None) to detect and load a\nstandalone workspace file (e.g. \".cypilot-workspace.toml\") next to project_root\n(also consider the _read_cypilot_var relocation if used) and call\nWorkspaceConfig.load on that resolved path (preserving the existing return shape\nfrom WorkspaceConfig.load); update find_workspace_config to return the loaded\nWorkspaceConfig (or error tuple) when that file exists instead of falling\nthrough to (None, None).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py-367-396 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`367-396`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Load remote adapter contexts from `sc.adapter_dir`, not the repo root.**\n\n`_load_reachable_source()` can discover an explicit adapter under `source.adapter` even when the source root is not itself a Cypilot install. Re-running discovery from `sc.path` throws that information away, so those sources behave as if they have no adapter.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        loaded = CypilotContext.load(sc.path)\n+        loaded = CypilotContext.load(sc.adapter_dir)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py` around lines 367 - 396, The\ncode loads the adapter context from sc.path which discards explicit adapter\ndiscovery; change the load call to use the resolved adapter directory\n(sc.adapter_dir) instead of sc.path: in the block where CypilotContext.load is\ninvoked, pass sc.adapter_dir (or its string) to CypilotContext.load so that\ndiscovered adapter contexts are preserved, then continue to set\nsc.adapter_context, sc._adapter_resolved and return the loaded context as\nbefore.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py-317-366 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`317-366`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`find_workspace_config()` still ignores the default workspace manifest.**\n\nIf `load_project_config()` returns no `workspace` key, or there is no core config at all, this helper falls through without checking `project_root / WORKSPACE_CONFIG_FILENAME`. That makes the advertised standalone `.cypilot-workspace.toml` path impossible to activate.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n def find_workspace_config(project_root: Path) -> Tuple[Optional[WorkspaceConfig], Optional[str]]:\n@@\n     cfg = load_project_config(project_root)\n+    default_workspace = (project_root / WORKSPACE_CONFIG_FILENAME).resolve()\n     if cfg is None:\n         # Distinguish \"no config file\" from \"parse error\":\n         # load_project_config returns None for both. Check if the file exists.\n         cypilot_rel = _read_cypilot_var(project_root)\n         if cypilot_rel:\n@@\n                 if candidate.is_file():\n                     return None, f\"Failed to parse config: {candidate}\"\n+        if default_workspace.is_file():\n+            return WorkspaceConfig.load(default_workspace)\n         return None, None\n     if cfg is not None:\n         ws_value = cfg.get(\"workspace\")\n+        if ws_value is None and default_workspace.is_file():\n+            return WorkspaceConfig.load(default_workspace)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py` around lines 317 - 366,\nfind_workspace_config currently skips checking the standalone workspace manifest\nwhen cfg has no \"workspace\" key (or there is no core config); update\nfind_workspace_config to, after handling cfg is None and after the existing cfg\nhandling for str/dict, check for the default WORKSPACE_CONFIG_FILENAME at\nproject_root (e.g. project_root / WORKSPACE_CONFIG_FILENAME). If that file\nexists, call WorkspaceConfig.load on its resolved path and return that result\n(or return the parsed WorkspaceConfig and no error), otherwise preserve the\nexisting return None, None behavior; reference find_workspace_config,\nload_project_config, WORKSPACE_CONFIG_FILENAME, and\nWorkspaceConfig.load/from_dict to locate where to add this check.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (5)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`251-254`: **Clarify output_dir computation logic.**\n\nThe logic for determining `output_dir` handles three cases but could benefit from a brief comment explaining the intent.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Optional clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n-    # Determine output dir for relative path computation\n+    # Determine output dir for relative path computation:\n+    # - If --output specified: use parent of output file\n+    # - If --inline: use project_root (paths relative to project)\n+    # - Otherwise: use scan_root\n     output_dir = Path(args.output).resolve().parent if args.output else scan_root\n     if args.inline:\n         output_dir = project_root\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py\naround lines 251 - 254, The output_dir assignment branch is unclear; add a\nconcise comment above the block explaining the three intended cases: if\nargs.output is provided use its parent (so relative paths are computed against\nthe output file's directory), otherwise default to scan_root, and if args.inline\nis set override to project_root to keep generated content inside the project;\nreference the symbols output_dir, args.output, args.inline, scan_root,\nproject_root and the Path(...).resolve().parent expression so reviewers\nunderstand the rationale.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/artifacts_meta.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`54-62`: **Consider extracting repeated source parsing logic.**\n\nThe source parsing pattern is duplicated across `Kit.from_dict`, `Artifact.from_dict`, and `CodebaseEntry.from_dict`:\n\n```python\nraw_source = (data or {}).get(\"source\", None)\nsource = str(raw_source).strip() if isinstance(raw_source, str) and str(raw_source).strip() else None\n```\n\nThis could be extracted into a helper similar to `_parse_single_line_comments`.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Optional helper extraction</summary>\n\n```python\ndef _parse_optional_str(data: dict, key: str) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"Parse an optional string field, returning None if empty or missing.\"\"\"\n    raw = (data or {}).get(key, None)\n    return str(raw).strip() if isinstance(raw, str) and str(raw).strip() else None\n```\n\nThen use as: `source = _parse_optional_str(data, \"source\")`\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/artifacts_meta.py\naround lines 54 - 62, Extract the repeated source parsing into a small helper\nand use it from Kit.from_dict, Artifact.from_dict, and CodebaseEntry.from_dict:\nimplement a function like _parse_optional_str(data, key) (or reuse/align with\n_parse_single_line_comments) that returns None for missing/empty strings and the\nstripped string otherwise, then replace the duplicated raw_source/source logic\nin the three from_dict methods with calls to that helper (referencing\nKit.from_dict, Artifact.from_dict, CodebaseEntry.from_dict and the new helper\nname).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`51-56`: **Consider defensive typing for namespace rule lookup.**\n\n`_lookup_namespace` accesses `rule.host` without type checking. If the rules list contains unexpected types (e.g., dicts instead of objects), this would raise an AttributeError.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Optional defensive check</summary>\n\n```diff\n def _lookup_namespace(host: str, rules: list) -> Optional[object]:\n     \"\"\"Find a namespace rule matching the given host by exact match.\"\"\"\n     for rule in rules:\n-        if rule.host == host:\n+        if getattr(rule, \"host\", None) == host:\n             return rule\n     return None\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py around\nlines 51 - 56, The _lookup_namespace function currently assumes each entry in\nrules has a .host attribute which can raise AttributeError for unexpected types;\nupdate _lookup_namespace to defensively handle both objects with a host\nattribute and dict-like entries (and skip or ignore any other types) by using\nhasattr/getattr or key access (e.g., rule.get(\"host\")) to retrieve the host\nvalue safely, compare it to the host param, and return the matching rule or None\nwithout raising on bad types.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`80-98`: **Consider adding a warning when `--source` is used but `cross_repo` is disabled.**\n\nWhen `--source` is provided in workspace mode but `cross_repo` is `False` (line 89), the code returns an empty result silently. This could confuse users who expect `--source` to work regardless of traceability settings.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed improvement to warn users</summary>\n\n```diff\n         if args.source and not is_workspace:\n             import sys\n             print(f\"Warning: --source filter '{args.source}' ignored (not in workspace mode)\", file=sys.stderr)\n\n         if not args.source:\n             # No source filter — use shared collection helper\n             artifacts_to_scan, _ = collect_artifacts_to_scan(ctx)\n         else:\n             # --source filter: skip primary, scan only matching remote source\n-            if is_workspace and ctx.cross_repo:\n+            if is_workspace:\n+                if not ctx.cross_repo:\n+                    import sys\n+                    print(f\"Warning: --source filter '{args.source}' requires cross_repo=true in workspace config\", file=sys.stderr)\n                 for sc in ctx.sources.values():\n+                    if not ctx.cross_repo:\n+                        break\n                     if not sc.reachable or sc.meta is None:\n                         continue\n                     if sc.name != args.source:\n                         continue\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py around\nlines 80 - 98, When handling the --source filter in list_ids.py, add a user\nwarning when args.source is provided in workspace mode but ctx.cross_repo is\nFalse so the command doesn't silently return nothing; inside the branch that\nchecks \"if is_workspace and ctx.cross_repo:\" (the block that iterates\nctx.sources.values()), add an else path for \"if is_workspace and not\nctx.cross_repo\" that prints a stderr warning (e.g., using import sys and\nprint(..., file=sys.stderr)) stating that --source is ignored because\ncross_repo/remote scanning is disabled, referencing args.source and suggesting\nenabling cross-repo scanning.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/schemas/workspace.schema.json (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-10`: **Consider updating placeholder `$id` URL before release.**\n\nThe schema uses `https://example.com/cypilot/workspace.schema.json` as the `$id`. While this works for validation, you may want to update this to an actual hosted URL (e.g., a GitHub raw URL or documentation site) before the feature is released to production, for better schema discoverability.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding agains