ected for processing (4)</summary>\n\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_add.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py`\n* `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py`\n* `tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py`\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pull/81#pullrequestreview-3915020465","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pulls/81","author_association":"NONE","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pull/81#pullrequestreview-3915020465"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pulls/81"}},"submitted_at":"2026-03-09T12:19:29Z","commit_id":"4a7302fedb1b5286db753b7fc30986f1395af6c4"},{"id":3915071871,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOQ7VXwc7pW0F_","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`46-50`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Invalid role still defaults to `full` — consider failing fast instead.**\n\nWhile a warning is now printed (line 49), invalid roles still fall back to `\"full\"` (line 50), which is the most permissive role. A typo in the config silently broadens what gets scanned. The previous review suggested raising a `ValueError` eagerly in `from_dict` so misconfigurations fail fast at parse time.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🐛 Proposed fix to fail fast on invalid roles</summary>\n\n```diff\n         raw_role = str((data or {}).get(\"role\", \"full\")).strip().lower()\n-        if raw_role and raw_role not in VALID_ROLES:\n-            import sys\n-            print(f\"Warning: source '{name}' has invalid role '{raw_role}' (valid: {', '.join(sorted(VALID_ROLES))}); defaulting to 'full'\", file=sys.stderr)\n-        role = raw_role if raw_role in VALID_ROLES else \"full\"\n+        if raw_role and raw_role not in VALID_ROLES:\n+            raise ValueError(\n+                f\"Invalid role '{raw_role}' for source '{name}'. \"\n+                f\"Valid roles are: {', '.join(sorted(VALID_ROLES))}\"\n+            )\n+        role = raw_role if raw_role else \"full\"\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py` around lines 46 - 50, The\ncode in from_dict currently warns on invalid raw_role then silently defaults to\n\"full\"; instead, detect when raw_role is non-empty and not in VALID_ROLES and\nraise a ValueError immediately (include the offending name and raw_role in the\nmessage) rather than falling back to \"full\". Update the logic around raw_role,\nVALID_ROLES and role assignment in the from_dict method so invalid roles trigger\na ValueError (with clear context) and remove the permissive default behavior.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`374-377`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Standalone workspace manifests are not discovered when `core.toml` lacks a `workspace` key.**\n\nWhen `cfg.get(\"workspace\")` is absent or not a string/dict, this returns `(None, None)` without checking if a standalone `.cypilot-workspace.toml` exists at `project_root`. The `workspace-init` command can create standalone files, but they won't be discovered by subsequent workspace-aware commands.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🐛 Proposed fix to check for standalone manifest</summary>\n\n```diff\n     # `@cpt-begin`:cpt-cypilot-algo-workspace-find-config:p1:inst-find-return-none\n+    # Check for standalone workspace manifest before returning None\n+    standalone = (project_root / WORKSPACE_CONFIG_FILENAME).resolve()\n+    if standalone.is_file():\n+        return WorkspaceConfig.load(standalone)\n     return None, None\n     # `@cpt-end`:cpt-cypilot-algo-workspace-find-config:p1:inst-find-return-none\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`93-96`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Surface scan-root I/O failures as errors instead of empty results.**\n\nWhen `scan_root.iterdir()` fails (e.g., permission denied), this catches `OSError` and sets `entries = []`, which masks the failure as a successful scan with no sources. The command later reports `NO_SOURCES`, hiding the real problem.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🐛 Proposed fix to propagate the error</summary>\n\n```diff\n     # `@cpt-begin`:cpt-cypilot-algo-workspace-discover-nested:p1:inst-disc-list-entries\n     try:\n         entries = sorted(scan_root.iterdir(), key=lambda p: p.name)\n-    except OSError:\n-        entries = []\n+    except OSError as e:\n+        import sys\n+        print(f\"Error: cannot scan directory {scan_root}: {e}\", file=sys.stderr)\n+        return {}  # Caller will see empty dict, but error is logged\n     # `@cpt-end`:cpt-cypilot-algo-workspace-discover-nested:p1:inst-disc-list-entries\n```\n\nAlternatively, refactor to return `Tuple[Dict[str, dict], Optional[str]]` to propagate the error message to the caller for proper JSON error reporting.\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`413-423`: **Consider sorting `__all__` alphabetically.**\n\nThe `__all__` list is not sorted, which is flagged by Ruff (RUF022). Sorting improves readability and makes diffs cleaner when adding new exports.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Sorted `__all__`</summary>\n\n```diff\n __all__ = [\n-    \"VALID_ROLES\",\n-    \"SourceEntry\",\n-    \"TraceabilityConfig\",\n+    \"_json_error\",\n+    \"_load_inline_config\",\n+    \"find_workspace_config\",\n     \"NamespaceRule\",\n     \"ResolveConfig\",\n+    \"SourceEntry\",\n+    \"TraceabilityConfig\",\n+    \"VALID_ROLES\",\n     \"WorkspaceConfig\",\n-    \"find_workspace_config\",\n-    \"_json_error\",\n-    \"_load_inline_config\",\n ]\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py` around lines 413 - 423,\nThe module-level __all__ export list is unsorted (RUF022); reorder the entries\nin __all__ into alphabetical order to satisfy the linter and improve readability\n— e.g. sort the symbols VALID_ROLES, SourceEntry, TraceabilityConfig,\nNamespaceRule, ResolveConfig, WorkspaceConfig, find_workspace_config,\n_json_error, and _load_inline_config so the list is alphabetized (use the exact\nsymbol names shown to locate the __all__ definition).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`209-212`: **Consider catching specific exceptions instead of bare `Exception`.**\n\nThe blind `except Exception` at line 211 could mask unexpected errors. Catching specific exceptions like `(ValueError, OSError, TypeError)` would be more precise while still handling known failure modes.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n         try:\n             data = toml_utils.load(workspace_path)\n-        except Exception as e:\n+        except (ValueError, OSError, TypeError) as e:\n             return None, f\"Failed to read workspace file {workspace_path}: {e}\"\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py` around lines 209 - 212,\nReplace the broad except Exception in the workspace loader with targeted\nexception handling: catch the likely failure types (e.g., ValueError, OSError,\nTypeError and the TOML parser’s decode error if available) around\ntoml_utils.load(workspace_path) in the function that reads the workspace file;\non those specific exceptions return the same None and formatted error string\nincluding the exception details, and let unexpected exceptions propagate (or\nre-raise) so they are not silently masked. Use the function name and symbol\naround the current try/except that calls toml_utils.load to locate the change.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`303-308`: **Move success return to `else` block and catch specific exceptions.**\n\nThe `return None` at line 306 should be in an `else` block for cleaner try/except flow. Additionally, catching `Exception` broadly could mask unexpected errors.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n         try:\n             toml_utils.dump(self.to_dict(), path)\n-            self.workspace_file = path.resolve()\n-            return None\n-        except Exception as e:\n+        except OSError as e:\n             return f\"Failed to save workspace config to {path}: {e}\"\n+        else:\n+            self.workspace_file = path.resolve()\n+            return None\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py` around lines 303 - 308,\nThe save routine currently returns None inside the try and catches Exception\nbroadly; update the method that calls toml_utils.dump(self.to_dict(), path) so\nthe successful path (setting self.workspace_file = path.resolve() and returning\nNone) is placed in an else block after the try, and replace \"except Exception as\ne\" with targeted exception types that toml_utils.dump and path.resolve may raise\n(e.g., toml encode/decode errors and IO-related errors such as TomlEncodeError,\nTomlDecodeError, OSError/IOError or the specific exceptions your toml_utils\nexposes), returning a formatted error string on those specific exceptions and\nletting other unexpected exceptions propagate.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nDuplicate comments:\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py`:\n- Around line 46-50: The code in from_dict currently warns on invalid raw_role\nthen silently defaults to \"full\"; instead, detect when raw_role is non-empty and\nnot in VALID_ROLES and raise a ValueError immediately (include the offending\nname and raw_role in the message) rather than falling back to \"full\". Update the\nlogic around raw_role, VALID_ROLES and role assignment in the from_dict method\nso invalid roles trigger a ValueError (with clear context) and remove the\npermissive default behavior.\n\n---\n\nNitpick comments:\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py`:\n- Around line 413-423: The module-level __all__ export list is unsorted\n(RUF022); reorder the entries in __all__ into alphabetical order to satisfy the\nlinter and improve readability — e.g. sort the symbols VALID_ROLES, SourceEntry,\nTraceabilityConfig, NamespaceRule, ResolveConfig, WorkspaceConfig,\nfind_workspace_config, _json_error, and _load_inline_config so the list is\nalphabetized (use the exact symbol names shown to locate the __all__\ndefinition).\n- Around line 209-212: Replace the broad except Exception in the workspace\nloader with targeted exception handling: catch the likely failure types (e.g.,\nValueError, OSError, TypeError and the TOML parser’s decode error if available)\naround toml_utils.load(workspace_path) in the function that reads the workspace\nfile; on those specific exceptions return the same None and formatted error\nstring including the exception details, and let unexpected exceptions propagate\n(or re-raise) so they are not silently masked. Use the function name and symbol\naround the current try/except that calls toml_utils.load to locate the change.\n- Around line 303-308: The save routine currently returns None inside the try\nand catches Exception broadly; update the method that calls\ntoml_utils.dump(self.to_dict(), path) so the successful path (setting\nself.workspace_file = path.resolve() and returning None) is placed in an else\nblock after the try, and replace \"except Exception as e\" with targeted exception\ntypes that toml_utils.dump and path.resolve may raise (e.g., toml encode/decode\nerrors and IO-related errors such as TomlEncodeError, TomlDecodeError,\nOSError/IOError or the specific exceptions your toml_utils exposes), returning a\nformatted error string on those specific exceptions and letting other unexpected\nexceptions propagate.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>ℹ️ Review info</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>⚙️ Run configuration</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defau