 ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_add.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`55-58`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Move the `inst-config-update-inline` end marker below the actual write path.**\n\nRight now this begin/end pair wraps only the function declaration. The inline mutation (`existing[\"workspace\"] = ws`) and `toml_utils.dump(...)` call are outside the traced block, so `spec-coverage` will not attribute the implementation to `inst-config-update-inline`.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-# `@cpt-begin`:cpt-cypilot-state-workspace-config-lifecycle:p1:inst-config-update-inline\n def _add_to_inline(args: argparse.Namespace, project_root: Path) -> int:\n     \"\"\"Add source inline to the current repo's config/core.toml.\"\"\"\n-    # `@cpt-end`:cpt-cypilot-state-workspace-config-lifecycle:p1:inst-config-update-inline\n     from ..utils.workspace import _load_inline_config\n     from ..utils import toml_utils\n@@\n     if replaced:\n         result[\"replaced\"] = True\n     print(json.dumps(result, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False))\n+    # `@cpt-end`:cpt-cypilot-state-workspace-config-lifecycle:p1:inst-config-update-inline\n     return 0\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 88-95, 117-118\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_add.py` around lines 55 -\n58, The cpt marker pair for inst-config-update-inline currently wraps only the\n_add_to_inline function signature; move the \"#\n`@cpt-end`:cpt-cypilot-state-workspace-config-lifecycle:p1:inst-config-update-inline\"\nso it sits after the inline mutation and file write (the existing[\"workspace\"] =\nws assignment and toml_utils.dump(...) call) inside _add_to_inline so the\nimplementation is traced; apply the same adjustment to the other two occurrences\nnoted (the marker pairs around the other inline update blocks at the locations\nreferenced in the review) so each begin/end pair encloses the mutation plus\ndump/write operations.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`2841-2861`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Expand these compressed helpers onto separate lines again.**\n\nThe same semicolon-joined pattern that previously tripped Ruff is back here. Lines 2841, 2849, 2855, 2861, 2914, 2922, 2932, 3023, 3052, 3202, 3204, 3216, 3221, and 3222 are all `E702` hits, so this file still will not pass lint as written.\n\n\n\nVerify with Ruff; expected result is zero `E702` findings in `tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py`.\n\n```shell\n#!/bin/bash\npython3 -m pip install ruff -q\nruff check tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py --select E702 -n\n```\n\n\nAlso applies to: 2914-2932, 3023-3052, 3202-3222\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py` around lines 2841 - 2861, Several statements\nare compressed with semicolons (e.g., \"systems = []; kits = {}\",\n\"registered_systems = set(); kits = {}; _errors = []\", and single-line method\ndefs) causing E702; split those into separate lines. Edit the helper classes and\nthe TemporaryDirectory block shown (look for _FakeMeta, _FakeInnerCtx,\n_FakePrimary, the single-line defs\nget_artifact_by_path/iter_all_artifacts/is_ignored/get_known_id_kinds, and the\nassignments like systems = []; kits = {}) and replace each semicolon-joined\nstatement with one statement per line and expand single-line def bodies to\nmulti-line defs so no semicolons remain; then re-run Ruff to confirm zero E702\nfindings.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`446-450`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Honor `traceability.resolve_remote_ids` before importing remote IDs.**\n\n`--local-only` is not the only opt-out. This branch still pulls IDs from every workspace source even when the workspace disables remote ID resolution, so code-validation results can change despite `resolve_remote_ids = false`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-    if not args.local_only:\n+    if not args.local_only and getattr(ctx, \"resolve_remote_ids\", True):\n         from ..utils.context import WorkspaceContext\n         if isinstance(ctx, WorkspaceContext):\n             artifact_ids.update(ctx.get_all_artifact_ids())\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py` around lines 446 - 450,\nThe current branch imports remote artifact IDs whenever not args.local_only,\nignoring the workspace setting; update the conditional to also check the\nworkspace traceability flag (e.g., check ctx.traceability.resolve_remote_ids or\nWorkspaceContext.traceability.resolve_remote_ids) before calling\nctx.get_all_artifact_ids(); specifically, in validate.py where you have \"if not\nargs.local_only: if isinstance(ctx, WorkspaceContext):\nartifact_ids.update(ctx.get_all_artifact_ids())\", change it to require both not\nargs.local_only and that the workspace allows remote resolution, and skip\ncalling get_all_artifact_ids() when resolve_remote_ids is false.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`309-311`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Register standalone workspaces in `config/core.toml` too.**\n\nThis branch writes the standalone manifest, but later discovery goes through `find_workspace_config()`, which only resolves workspaces referenced by the `workspace` key in `core.toml`. A fresh standalone init is therefore not reopenable by `workspace-add`, `workspace-info`, or workspace-aware validation until the user edits config manually.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n     else:\n         output_path = Path(args.output).resolve() if args.output else (scan_root / WORKSPACE_CONFIG_FILENAME)\n         exit_code, output = _write_standalone(output_path, workspace_data)\n+        if exit_code == 0:\n+            from ..utils.workspace import _load_inline_config\n+            from ..utils import toml_utils\n+\n+            written_path = output_path / WORKSPACE_CONFIG_FILENAME if output_path.is_dir() else output_path\n+            config_path, existing, err = _load_inline_config(project_root)\n+            if err:\n+                print(_json_error(err))\n+                return 1\n+\n+            try:\n+                workspace_ref = written_path.relative_to(project_root).as_posix()\n+            except ValueError:\n+                workspace_ref = str(written_path)\n+\n+            existing[\"workspace\"] = workspace_ref\n+            toml_utils.dump(existing, config_path)\n```\n</details>\n\n\nBased on learnings Workspace location MUST be explicitly configured via the `workspace` key in `core.toml`. If no `workspace` key is present, Cypilot operates in single-repo mode for backward compatibility.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py` around lines 309 -\n311, After writing the standalone manifest with _write_standalone(output_path,\nworkspace_data), also register that workspace path under the workspace key in\ncore.toml so discovery via find_workspace_config will see it; open or create the\ncore.toml (scan_root / \"config\" / \"core.toml\"), parse/update the workspace key\nto point to the written WORKSPACE_CONFIG_FILENAME (or the relative/absolute\noutput_path as the project expects), and persist the file so\nworkspace-add/workspace-info and workspace-aware validation will find the new\nstandalone workspace.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_workspace.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-49`: _🛠️ Refactor suggestion_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Add the missing `@cpt-*` traceability markers to this test module.**\n\nThe file still lacks the required module/class marker comments, so the new workspace test flows are not linked into the repo’s code↔artifact traceability map.\n\n \n\nBased on learnings: Maintain code↔artifact traceability using cpt-* markers.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_workspace.py` around lines 1 - 49, Add the missing cpt\ntraceability markers by inserting a module-level cpt comment (e.g., a\ntop-of-file comment containing an `@cpt-module` or `@cpt-file` marker with an\nappropriate identifier) and a class-level cpt comment above the TestSourceEntry\nclass (e.g., an `@cpt-class` or `@cpt-test` marker) so the test module and the\nTestSourceEntry class are linked into the code↔artifact traceability map; ensure\nthe markers follow the project’s existing cpt comment format and include\nmeaningful identifiers that match other repo traceability entries.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`46-50`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Don't widen invalid roles to `full`.**\n\nA typo currently becomes the most permissive scope, and the original invalid value is lost before `validate()` can report it. At minimum, preserve the raw role so validation can reject it instead of normalizing it here.\n\n \n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        if raw_role and raw_role not in VALID_ROLES:\n-            import sys\n-            print(f\"Warning: source '{name}' has invalid role '{raw_role}' (valid: {', '.join(sorted(VALID_ROLES))}); defaulting to 'full'\", file=sys.stderr)\n-        role = raw_role if raw_role in VALID_ROLES else \"full\"\n+        role = raw_role if raw_role else \"full\"\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py` around lines 46 - 50, The\ncode currently defaults unknown/typo roles to \"full\" which loses the original\ninvalid value; change the logic so raw_role preserves the original normalized\ninput and only default to \"full\" when no role was provided (empty string), e.g.\ncompute raw_role = str((data or {}).get(\"role\", \"\")).strip().lower() and then\nset role = raw_role if raw_role else \"full\" (do NOT widen invalid values to\n\"full\"); keep the validation against VALID_ROLES (and let validate() report\ninvalid roles) — update references to raw_role and role accordingly so\nVALID_ROLES checks and validate() see the original invalid string.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`98-109`: **Consider using unpacking for cleaner list construction.**\n\nPer static analysis, `[\"git\", *args]` is more idiomatic than concatenation.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Minor style improvement</summary>\n\n```diff\n     try:\n         result = subprocess.run(\n-            [\"git\"] + args,\n+            [\"git\", *args],\n             cwd=cwd,\n             capture_output=True,\n             text=True,\n             timeout=_GIT_TIMEOUT,\n         )\n         return (result.returncode, result.stdout, result.stderr)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py` around lines 98 - 109,\nReplace the list concatenation [\"git\"] + args with the more idiomatic unpacking\nform [\"git\", *args] in the subprocess.run call inside git_utils.py (the block\nthat calls subprocess.run with variables args and _GIT_TIMEOUT); keep all other\nparameters (cwd, capture_output, text, timeout) and the existing exception\nhandling unchanged.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`67-88`: **Fetch-then-checkout on existing repos returns path even on fetch failure.**\n\nWhen `git fetch` fails (line 70-73), the function still returns `local_path`. This is documented behavior (\"returns local_path on success, None on clone failure\") but may be surprising — a failed fetch means the local repo is stale. Consider whether downstream callers handle this gracefully or if a warning-only approach is sufficient.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py` around lines 67 - 88, The\nfunction _clone_or_fetch returns local_path even when git fetch fails, leaving\ncallers with a potentially stale repo; change the behavior so that when\n_run_git([\"fetch\", \"--quiet\"], cwd=local_path) returns rc != 0 the function\nreturns None (and similarly return None on a failed checkout), and update the\ndocstring to state it returns local_path only on successful fetch/checkout and\nNone on failure so callers can detect and handle a failed update.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`427-437`: **Consider sorting `__all__` for consistency.**\n\nPer static analysis (RUF022), the `__all__` list could be sorted alphabetically.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Sort __all__ alphabetically</summary>\n\n```diff\n __all__ = [\n+    \"NamespaceRule\",\n+    \"ResolveConfig\",\n+    \"SourceEntry\",\n+    \"TraceabilityConfig\",\n     \"VALID_ROLES\",\n-    \"SourceEntry\",\n-    \"TraceabilityConfig\",\n-    \"NamespaceRule\",\n-    \"ResolveConfig\",\n     \"WorkspaceConfig\",\n+    \"_json_error\",\n+    \"_load_inline_config\",\n     \"find_workspace_config\",\n-    \"_json_error\",\n-    \"_load_inline_config\",\n ]\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py around\nlines 427 - 437, The __all__ list in workspace.py is unsorted; update the module\nexport list named \"__all__\" so its string entries are ordered alphabetically\n(e.g., \"NamespaceRule\", \"ResolveConfig\", \"SourceEntry\", \"TraceabilityConfig\",\n\"VALID_ROLES\", \"WorkspaceConfig\", \"_json_error\", \"_load_inline_config\",\n\"find_workspace_config\" — adjust exact ordering to true alphabetical order),\npreserving the same identifiers and quotes so the exported symbols remain\nunchanged.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_defined.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`18-18`: **Drop the unused resolver context binding.**\n\nLine 18 unpacks `ctx`, but it is never read afterward, so this now trips the Ruff/Sonar warning already attached to the PR. Rename it to `_ctx` (or `_`) to keep the command lint-clean.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_defined.py` at line 18, The\nunpacked resolver context variable returned from resolve_target_and_artifacts is\nunused and triggers a linter warning; change the unpack target from \"ctx\" to a\nthrowaway name (e.g., \"_ctx\" or \"_\") in the assignment \"target_id, ctx,\nartifacts_to_scan, path_to_source, err = resolve_target_and_artifacts(args)\" so\nthe function call and the other variables (target_id, artifacts_to_scan,\npath_to_source, err) remain unchanged but the unused context no longer produces\na lint error.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/workspace.md:\n- Line 17: The TOC entry \"[3. Processes / Business Logic\n(CDSL)](`#3-processes-business-logic-cdsl`)\" has a mismatched anchor; update the\nfragment to match the actual header slug by editing the link target for that TOC\nitem (the link text \"[3. Processes / Business Logic (CDSL)]\") so it points to\nthe correct fragment used by the \"Processes / Business Logic (CDSL)\" header\n(normalize spaces to hyphens and strip/encode punctuation as your markdown\nflavor expects).\n- Around line 197-206: The spec's discovery flow incorrectly states that\nfind_workspace_config() will auto-discover a standalone .cypilot-workspace.toml\nwhen the workspace key is absent; update the spec sections (including the steps\nlabeled inst-find-return-none and related mentions at lines ~409-410 and ~546)\nto reflect the actual contract: the runtime requires an explicit workspace key\nin core.toml and does NOT perform implicit parent traversal or standalone-file\nauto-discovery, so remove or reword any steps and DoD/acceptance text that claim\na fallback to .cypilot-workspace.toml and replace with a clear statement that\nabsence of the workspace key results in no workspace (single-repo mode).\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/requirements/workspace.md:\n- Around line 66-70: Update the \"standalone-file\" section to remove any\nimplication of implicit discovery and state that .cypilot-workspace.toml is not\nauto-discovered; instead document that workspace location must be explicitly set\nvia the workspace key in config/core.toml and mention find_workspace_config()\ndoes NOT traverse parent directories or auto-locate standalone\n.cypilot-workspace.toml files, aligning this text with the later\n\"discovery-order\" section and the runtime behavior.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_used.py:\n- Line 19: The variable binding for the context returned by\nresolve_target_and_artifacts is unused; rename the ctx variable to a throwaway\nname (e.g., _ctx or _) in the assignment target_id, ctx, artifacts_to_scan,\npath_to_source, err = resolve_target_and_artifacts(args) so linters stop\nflagging the unused binding; update that exact assignment expression to use the\nchosen underscore name and keep the other bindings unchanged.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_add.py:\n- Around line 68-76: The code currently replaces any non-str, non-dict workspace\nvalue (ws from existing.get(\"workspace\")) with a new inline dict, which hides\nmalformed config like workspace = 42; modify the logic in workspace_add.py so\nthat if existing.get(\"workspace\") is present and not a dict (and not a str), you\nemit an error via _json_error (explaining the malformed workspace) and return\nnon-zero instead of creating a fresh {\"version\":\"1.0\",\"sources\":{}}; only\ncreate/initialize the inline workspace when no existing workspace key is present\nor when it is explicitly a dict.\n\nIn `@architecture/features/workspace.md`:\n- Around line 197-206: The spec incorrectly documents an auto-discovery fallback\nfor find_workspace_config(); remove the standalone `.cypilot-workspace.toml`\nfallback behavior from the feature spec by deleting or updating the step labeled\ninst-find-return-none (and any duplicate references at lines noted like 409-410\nand 546) so the doc clearly states that workspace resolution only happens when\nthe `workspace` key is present in `core.toml` (either as a string or inline dict\nparsed by\ninst-find-parse-inline/inst-find-return-external/inst-find-return-inline) and\notherwise returns `(None, None)` to remain in single-repo mode.\n- Line 17: The TOC entry \"[3. Processes / Business Logic\n(CDSL)](`#3-processes-business-logic-cdsl`)\" has a mismatched fragment causing\nmarkdownlint MD051; update that TOC fragment to exactly match the generated\nheading slug for the \"Processes / Business Logic (CDSL)\" heading (or regenerate\nthe entire TOC) so the anchor resolves correctly—locate the TOC line shown and\nreplace the fragment portion (the text inside #...) with the exact slug produced\nby the actual heading.\n\nIn `@architecture/PRD.md`:\n- Around line 344-348: Update the PRD to match the implemented contract: remove\nthe statement that the system falls back to auto-discovering a standalone\n.cypilot-workspace.toml at the project root and instead state that workspace\ndiscovery requires an explicit workspace entry in core.toml (i.e.,\ncore.toml.workspace) and that absence of that key keeps the project in\nsingle-repo mode; also change the wording that the Git clone working directory\ndefaults to the repository-local .workspace-sources (not the current directory)\nand note that standalone workspace files are only used when explicitly\nreferenced (per find_workspace_config()) and that inline workspace config\n(config/core.toml) does not support Git URL sources.\n\nIn `@README.md`:\n- Around line 295-299: The README's \"Workspace config\" section incorrectly\nimplies a standalone .cypilot-workspace.toml at a super-root is sufficient;\nupdate the text to state that a repository only enters workspace mode when its\nconfig/core.toml either embeds a [workspace] section or has a workspace key\npointing to the standalone .cypilot-workspace.toml, because\nfind_workspace_config() does not auto-discover parent directories and requires\nexplicit workspace configuration in core.toml.\n\nIn `@schemas/workspace.schema.json`:\n- Around line 75-96: The schema currently allows empty strings for the \"path\",\n\"adapter\", \"url\", and \"branch\" properties in workspace.schema.json which lets\ninvalid source entries pass validation; update each of those property\ndefinitions (\"path\", \"adapter\", \"url\", \"branch\") to include \"minLength\": 1 so\nempty strings are rejected at schema-validation time and invalid workspace files\nfail early.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py`:\n- Around line 48-64: When a --source is provided the code replaces ctx with\nadapter_ctx and later artifact handling falls back to resolving the artifact\nfrom artifact_path.parent (losing the selected source); update the\nartifact-resolution logic so that when args.source is set you resolve the\nartifact relative to the selected source (use adapter_ctx or sc workspace root /\nsc.path) rather than the current cwd, and do not drop the WorkspaceContext\nprematurely—adjust the block that assigns ctx = adapter_ctx and the\nsingle-artifact branch to use adapter_ctx (or sc) for path resolution; apply the\nsame fix to the other occurrence around lines 129-147.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/where_used.py`:\n- Line 19: The tuple returned by resolve_target_and_artifacts is being\ndestructured into target_id, ctx, artifacts_to_scan, path_to_source, err but ctx\nis never used; rename that binding to a throwaway name (e.g., _ctx or _) in the\ndestructuring so linters stop flagging it (update the line with\nresolve_target_and_artifacts(...) to assign target_id, _ctx, artifacts_to_scan,\npath_to_source, err).\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_info.py`:\n- Around line 61-64: The code currently ignores the error returned from\nload_artifacts_meta(adapter_dir), which can leave adapter_found true while\nhiding metadata loading failures; update the branch around load_artifacts_meta\nto set info[\"metadata_error\"] to the err value when err is truthy (and only\npopulate info[\"artifact_count\"] and info[\"system_count\"] when meta is valid),\ni.e., after calling load_artifacts_meta(adapter_dir) check if err and assign it\nto info[\"metadata_error\"] so callers can see registry/metadata failures while\npreserving adapter_found; keep using meta.iter_all_artifacts() and meta.systems\nonly when meta is non-null.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py`:\n- Around line 49-54: The _compute_source_path function must always return a path\nrelative to the output directory, but Path.relative_to only succeeds when entry\nis a descendant; replace the fallback that returns an absolute path with a true\nrelative-path computation (use os.path.relpath or Path(entry).relative_to\nsemantics implemented via pathlib/os.path) to compute entry relative to\noutput_dir and then convert to posix form; update the function so its return is\nalways the relative path to output_dir (using the symbol _compute_source_path\nand the parameters entry: Path, output_dir: Path) and only fall back to as_posix\nfor any unexpected failure while preserving portability.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py`:\n- Around line 381-382: The code currently calls CypilotContext.load(sc.path) and\nthus ignores the configured adapter location; update resolve_adapter_context\n(and the related calls in the other block around lines 508-513) to call\nCypilotContext.load(sc.adapter_dir) so the explicitly-configured adapter path\n(and the fallback behavior in _load_reachable_source() that uses\nexplicit_adapter) is honored; locate uses of sc.path in resolve_adapter_context\nand replace them with sc.adapter_dir (or use explicit_adapter when appropriate)\nto ensure metadata/context is loaded from the configured adapter, not the repo\nroot.\n- Around line 662-684: The current --artifact branch only accepts artifacts\nunder project_root by using artifact_path.relative_to(project_root) and\nmeta.get_artifact_by_path(rel_path), which rejects source-routed artifacts that\nlive outside project_root; instead, when artifact_path.relative_to(project_root)\nfails, resolve the artifact_path and search the Cypilot registry (via\nCypilotContext.load(...) and ctx.meta) for entries whose recorded filesystem\nlocation or source field matches the resolved artifact_path (e.g., iterate meta\nentries and compare artifact_meta.source or the artifact's absolute path) and\nappend matches to artifacts_to_scan before returning the \"not in registry\"\nerror.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py`:\n- Around line 200-213: After constructing the workspace config with\ncls.from_dict (the call assigning cfg), run the config validation immediately\n(call cfg.validate() or the project's validate method) and if validation fails\n(raises or returns errors), return None and a clear error string instead of\nreturning the unvalidated cfg; apply the same change to the inline loader path\naround the similar from_dict call at the other location so invalid inline\nconfigs (e.g., sources with url or empty paths) are rejected up-front before\nresolve_source_path() runs.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/SKILL.md`:\n- Around line 179-186: Update the contradictory statement in SKILL.md: either\nchange \"Cypilot has exactly TWO core workflows\" to \"THREE core workflows\" if\n{.core/workflows/workspace.md} is intended as a core workflow, or explicitly\nmark {.core/workflows/workspace.md} as a specialized sub-workflow (e.g.,\n\"workspace is a specialized sub-workflow\") and keep the \"TWO core workflows\"\nphrasing; ensure the three referenced files generate.md and analyze.md remain\nlabeled as core workflows and workspace.md is clearly labeled per your chosen\nfix.\n\nIn `@tests/_test_helpers.py`:\n- Around line 43-56: run_cli_in_project currently calls cypilot.cli.main(args)\nwhich can flip a process-global JSON mode; capture the global JSON-mode state\nbefore calling main and restore it in the finally block so tests don't leak mode\nbetween runs. Specifically, inside run_cli_in_project save the current JSON-mode\n(how the CLI exposes it, e.g. a module/global flag or getter in cypilot.cli),\ncall main(args) as before, and in the finally ensure you reset that JSON-mode\nback to the saved value (in addition to restoring cwd) so later tests observe\nthe original mode.\n\nIn `@tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py`:\n- Around line 2578-2582: This test currently unconditionally calls\nsymlink.symlink_to(real_code), which makes it fail on environments without\nsymlink permissions; instead either (a) guard the symlink creation by checking\nfor symlink support and permissions and skip the test when unavailable (e.g.,\nuse pytest.skip or unittest.skipIf when os.symlink is missing or creating a\nsymlink raises OSError/PermissionError), or (b) avoid creating a real symlink\nand mock Path.symlink_to or the resolution path to raise the ValueError you want\nto exercise; update the code around the symlink variable (the src / \"ext.py\"\ncreation and symlink.symlink_to(real_code) call) to use one of these approaches\nso the test is portable.\n\n---\n\nOutside diff comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py:\n- Around line 85-103: The branch handling args.source currently returns early\nand skips codebase scanning; to avoid incorrect zero results, add an explicit\nguard in the list-ids command (before collecting artifacts) that checks if\nargs.source is set AND args.include_code is true and, if so, surface a clear\nerror via ui.result or ui.error and return non-zero (reject the unsupported\ncombination) — update the top-level handler that uses args.source,\nargs.include_code, artifacts_to_scan, collect_artifacts_to_scan, and\nctx.meta.iter_all_codebase to perform this check so the code path never proceeds\nwith an unsupported combination.\n\nIn `@tests/test_cli_py_