a, and then append to artifacts_to_scan (preserving\n> artifact_meta.kind) rather than using the old relative_to + get_artifact_by_path\n> logic.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>architecture/specs/cli.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `250-261`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Keep the usage lines in sync with the new workspace flags.**\n> \n> The option tables now document `validate --local-only/--source` and `list-ids --source`, but the synopsis blocks above still omit those flags. The command grammar is inconsistent as written.\n> \n> \n> \n> Also applies to: 307-317\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In `@architecture/specs/cli.md` around lines 250 - 261, The synopsis lines for the\n> CLI commands are missing the new workspace flags, causing inconsistency with the\n> option tables; update the command usage lines for the validate command (the \"cpt\n> validate\" synopsis) to include the --local-only and --source SOURCE flags and\n> likewise update the \"list-ids\" synopsis to include --source SOURCE so the\n> grammar matches the option tables and reflects the new workspace flags.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> <details>\n> <summary>tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `363-371`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n> \n> **Fix the undefined name `F821` error on line 371.**\n> \n> The return type annotation `\"cypilot.utils.artifacts_meta.ArtifactsMeta\"` still triggers Ruff F821 because the `cypilot` module is not in scope (the import happens inside the function body). Use a `TYPE_CHECKING` import to bring `ArtifactsMeta` into scope for type hints, or drop the return annotation entirely.\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In `@tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py` around lines 363 - 371, The F821 arises\n> because the return annotation uses \"cypilot.utils.artifacts_meta.ArtifactsMeta\"\n> while the cypilot module is only imported inside the function body; fix by\n> adding a typing TYPE_CHECKING guard at the top of the module (from typing import\n> TYPE_CHECKING) and inside do: if TYPE_CHECKING: from\n> cypilot.utils.artifacts_meta import ArtifactsMeta, then change the\n> _bootstrap_kit return annotation to use ArtifactsMeta (unquoted) so the name is\n> available to the type checker but not imported at runtime; alternatively remove\n> the return annotation entirely if you prefer.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (21)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`86-100`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`--source` filter still does not apply to `--include-code` code scanning.**\n\nThe artifact collection correctly respects the `--source` filter when in workspace mode. However, the code scanning path (lines 164-210) still iterates `ctx.meta.iter_all_codebase()` and `ctx.project_root`, which scans the primary repo's codebase regardless of the `--source` filter.\n\nThis means `list-ids --source <name> --include-code` will mix the filtered source's artifacts with the primary repo's code references — the same issue flagged in a previous review.\n\n,\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py around\nlines 86 - 100, The --include-code path currently ignores the --source filter by\nalways scanning ctx.meta.iter_all_codebase() and ctx.project_root; update the\ncode-scanning branch in list_ids.py so it mirrors the artifact collection logic:\nif not args.source keep the existing\nctx.meta.iter_all_codebase()/ctx.project_root behavior, but if args.source and\nis_workspace find the matching source object in ctx.sources (like the earlier\nblock that checks sc.name == args.source) and iterate that source's metadata\ncode entries (e.g., sc.meta.iter_all_codebase() or equivalent) building paths\nrelative to sc.path, and do NOT scan ctx.project_root; ensure you only append\ncode entries from the matched sc and skip unreachable or meta-less sources.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>schemas/artifacts.schema.json (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`239-243`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Update `path` descriptions to reflect source-relative resolution.**\n\nThe new `source` field descriptions correctly explain that paths resolve relative to the source root when present. However, the existing `path` property descriptions (lines 230, 332, 365) still state paths are \"resolved relative to project root\" without mentioning the source override behavior. This makes the schema self-contradictory for consumers.\n\nConsider updating the `path` descriptions to clarify:\n- \"Resolved relative to project root unless `source` is set, in which case it is resolved relative to the named workspace source root.\"\n\n,\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@schemas/artifacts.schema.json` around lines 239 - 243, Update the \"path\"\nproperty descriptions in schemas/artifacts.schema.json to state that paths are\nresolved relative to the project root unless the sibling \"source\" field is\npresent, in which case they are resolved relative to the named workspace source\nroot; locate each \"path\" property near the \"source\" property and replace the\nexisting \"resolved relative to project root\" text with this clarified wording so\nthe schema no longer contradicts the \"source\" description.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`410-411`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Load the adapter context from `sc.adapter_dir`, not `sc.path`.**\n\n`CypilotContext.load(sc.path)` rediscoveres from the repo root and can pick the wrong adapter when the workspace config points at a non-default adapter directory or the repo contains multiple adapters. This should load from the resolved adapter directory directly.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py` around lines 410 - 411, The\ncode calls CypilotContext.load(sc.path) which causes discovery from the repo\nroot; change it to load from the resolved adapter directory instead by calling\nCypilotContext.load with sc.adapter_dir. Update the usage of CypilotContext.load\nin the function where sc (the workspace config) is available so it uses\nsc.adapter_dir rather than sc.path to avoid selecting the wrong adapter when\nnon-default or multiple adapters exist.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`537-543`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Configured adapter paths should win over auto-discovery.**\n\n`explicit_adapter` is only used as a fallback here, so a repo with several adapters can ignore the adapter path declared in the workspace manifest. Prefer the configured adapter first, then fall back to `find_cypilot_directory(...)` only when no explicit adapter was provided.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py` around lines 537 - 543, The\ncode currently calls find_cypilot_directory(resolved_path) first and only uses\nexplicit_adapter as a fallback; change the logic so the configured\nexplicit_adapter wins: first check if explicit_adapter is not None and points to\na valid adapter directory (explicit_adapter.is_dir() and (explicit_adapter /\n\"AGENTS.md\").exists()), and if so set adapter_dir = explicit_adapter; otherwise\ncall adapter_dir = find_cypilot_directory(resolved_path) as the fallback. Update\nthe block around adapter_dir, explicit_adapter and find_cypilot_directory to\nreflect this ordering.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`689-705`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`--artifact` still bypasses workspace-aware artifact resolution.**\n\nThis helper rebuilds a plain `CypilotContext` from `artifact_path.parent` and only checks `meta.get_artifact_by_path(rel_path)`, so artifacts that live in another workspace source are rejected even when the active workspace registry references them via `source`. Reuse the workspace-aware collection path here instead of reimplementing single-repo lookup.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py` around lines 689 - 705, The\ncode reconstructs a local CypilotContext via\nCypilotContext.load(artifact_path.parent) and calls\nctx.meta.get_artifact_by_path(rel_path), which bypasses workspace-aware\nresolution; instead stop rebuilding a single-repo context and reuse the\nworkspace-aware collection/registry path used elsewhere in the module (i.e. the\nworkspace-level artifact collection that respects source references) so\nartifacts from other workspace sources are found; replace the\nCypilotContext.load(...) + ctx.meta.get_artifact_by_path(...) logic with a call\ninto the existing workspace-aware collector/registry and append matches to\nartifacts as before (keep using artifact_path and artifact_meta.kind).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`46-50`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Reject invalid source roles instead of defaulting them to `full`.**\n\nA typo in `role` silently widens the source to the most permissive mode, and `validate()` can no longer catch it because the bad value has already been discarded. `from_dict()` should fail fast here instead of warning and coercing.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py` around lines 46 - 50, The\ncurrent from_dict() logic quietly coerces invalid raw_role values to \"full\";\ninstead, when raw_role is present and not in VALID_ROLES, raise an exception so\ncallers fail fast. Replace the print+default behavior in the block handling\nraw_role (the variables raw_role and role) with raising a ValueError (or custom\nexception) that includes the invalid value, the source name, and the list of\nVALID_ROLES; keep valid values unchanged and let validate() handle further\nchecks.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`131-146`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Don't collapse workspace mode in the `--artifact` path.**\n\nOnce `ctx` is replaced with a plain `CypilotContext` here, the rest of the command loses workspace state, so later remote-ID expansion behaves as if `--local-only` were always on for `validate --artifact ...`. It also breaks `--source`-scoped relative artifact paths. Keep the original `WorkspaceContext` around separately and use the matched adapter context only for repo-local metadata/template loading.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py` around lines 131 - 146,\nThe code overwrites the original workspace context (ctx) when resolving\n--artifact, losing workspace state; instead preserve the WorkspaceContext (check\nisinstance(ctx, WorkspaceContext)) by saving it to a new variable (e.g.,\nworkspace_ctx) and only replace/assign a separate repo-local context variable\n(e.g., repo_ctx or matched_ctx from determine_target_source) for repo-local\nmetadata/template loading and artifact-relative resolution; leave the original\nctx (workspace_ctx) intact so later logic (remote ID expansion, --source-scoped\nrelative paths) continues to use the workspace-level context while local\noperations use the matched_ctx/CypilotContext.load result.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>requirements/workspace.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`128-130`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Keep the `path` field docs aligned with inline workspace semantics.**\n\nThis row says `path` is always resolved relative to the workspace file, but the later path-resolution section says inline `[workspace]` paths resolve relative to the project root. Please describe both modes here so the contract is internally consistent.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@requirements/workspace.md` around lines 128 - 130, Update the `path` field\ndocumentation to describe both resolution modes: when the workspace is defined\nin a workspace file, state that `path` is resolved relative to the workspace\nfile location; when the workspace is defined inline via `[workspace]`, state\nthat `path` is resolved relative to the project root. Reference the `path` field\nand the inline `[workspace]` semantics so the row matches the later\npath-resolution section and clarifies precedence with `url`.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/adapter_info.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`338-362`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Surface workspace load errors instead of flattening everything to `active: false`.**\n\nThis currently makes “no workspace configured” indistinguishable from “workspace config exists but is invalid/unreadable”, which is exactly the case users run `info` to diagnose. Preserve `ws_err` (and unexpected exception text) in `config[\"workspace\"]` so broken manifests are visible.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/adapter_info.py` around lines 338 -\n362, When calling find_workspace_config, stop discarding the error and surface\nit in config[\"workspace\"]; capture the tuple as (ws_cfg, ws_err) (not _ws_err),\nand when ws_cfg is None set config[\"workspace\"] = {\"active\": False, \"error\":\nstr(ws_err)} if ws_err is not None (otherwise keep just active:false); also in\nthe except Exception as e handler set config[\"workspace\"] = {\"active\": False,\n\"error\": str(e)} so unexpected exceptions are preserved — update references\naround find_workspace_config, ws_cfg, ws_err and the except block accordingly.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/SKILL.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`167-173`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Put `--json` back into the workspace quick commands.**\n\nThese `ALWAYS run ... directly` lines still contradict the Agent-Safe Invocation section and would hand an agent human-formatted output instead of stable JSON. Add `--json` as the first argument to `workspace-init`, both `workspace-add` forms, and `workspace-info`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/SKILL.md around lines 167 - 173, Update the\nAgent-Safe invocation examples in SKILL.md so the CLI always emits stable JSON:\nprepend the --json flag as the first argument to the shown commands — i.e.,\nchange the workspace-init invocation to run `python3\n{cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py --json workspace-init`,\nboth workspace-add variants to `...cypilot.py --json workspace-add ...`\n(including the inline and auto-detect forms), and workspace-info to\n`...cypilot.py --json workspace-info`; ensure the `ALWAYS run ... directly`\nlines for workspace-init, both workspace-add forms, and workspace-info reflect\nthese --json-prefixed invocations so agents receive JSON output.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>architecture/features/workspace.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`359-367`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**The `--source` override is still documented after all return paths.**\n\nAs written, Steps 2-3 already return for both the matched and fallback cases, so Step 4 is unreachable. Put `inst-validate-source-flag` before the longest-prefix scan.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@architecture/features/workspace.md` around lines 359 - 367, The documented\nunreachable --source override should be handled before the longest-prefix scan:\nmove the inst-validate-source-flag step to precede inst-target-foreach-source\n(after inst-target-resolve-abs), and implement it to look up the named source,\nresolve its adapter context (using\ncpt-cypilot-algo-workspace-resolve-adapter-context), and immediately return\n(source, adapter_context) or (source, primary_context) as appropriate so the\nsubsequent longest-prefix scan and the existing return paths\n(inst-target-return-source, inst-target-return-source-no-adapter,\ninst-target-return-primary) remain correct and reachable only when no explicit\n--source is provided.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`141-153`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Make the missing-workspace error conditional on non-inline mode.**\n\nStep 4 explicitly allows `workspace-add --inline` to go straight to `core.toml`, but the error scenario and Step 6 still read as unconditional. The flow should only fail when `--inline` is absent.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@architecture/features/workspace.md` around lines 141 - 153, The\nmissing-workspace error must be conditional on non-inline mode: update the\nworkspace-add flow (inst-user-workspace-add) so that after inst-add-find-root\nand inst-add-if-inline-flag, the branch that calls\ncpt-cypilot-algo-workspace-find-config (inst-add-find-ws) only triggers the \"no\nworkspace config found\" error (inst-add-if-no-ws) when the --inline flag is not\nset; if --inline is set, bypass inst-add-find-ws and proceed to add to the\ninline/core.toml config instead.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>architecture/specs/cli.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`729-731`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add a language to the new fenced command blocks.**\n\nThese three new fences are still bare and will keep failing MD040. Tag them as `text` or `bash`.\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 770-772, 815-817\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n````\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@architecture/specs/cli.md` around lines 729 - 731, Add a language tag (e.g.,\nbash or text) to the new fenced code blocks that currently contain bare commands\nsuch as the \"cpt workspace-init [--root DIR] [--output PATH] [--inline]\n[--force] [--dry-run]\" block (and the other similar command fences referenced),\nso each triple-backtick fence becomes ```bash (or ```text) to satisfy MD040;\nlocate the fenced blocks by the command text and update the opening fence only.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>architecture/DECOMPOSITION.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`596-602`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Use `cpt` for the public CLI examples.**\n\nThis API block still uses `cypilot`, but the executable documented elsewhere in this PR is `cpt`. Keeping the agent keyword here blurs the CLI surface.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@architecture/DECOMPOSITION.md` around lines 596 - 602, Replace the outdated\nexecutable name \"cypilot\" with \"cpt\" across the documented CLI API block so the\nexamples match the public CLI; update each command line (e.g., \"cypilot\nworkspace-init\", \"cypilot workspace-add\", \"cypilot workspace-info\", \"cypilot\nvalidate\", \"cypilot list-ids\") to use \"cpt\" instead to keep the CLI surface\nconsistent with the rest of the PR.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`553-557`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Document the standalone workspace fallback in the discovery scope.**\n\nThis scope bullet still reads as if discovery is driven only by the `core.toml` `workspace` key, but the runtime also auto-discovers `.cypilot-workspace.toml` at `project_root`.\n \n\nBased on learnings, `find_workspace_config()` checks the `workspace` key first and then falls back to a standalone `.cypilot-workspace.toml` at `project_root`, with no parent-directory walk-up.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@architecture/DECOMPOSITION.md` around lines 553 - 557, Update the \"Scope\"\nbullet under discovery to document the standalone workspace fallback: state that\ndiscovery first honors the `core.toml` `workspace` key (string path or inline\ndict) and then, if absent, calls `find_workspace_config()` which falls back to a\nstandalone `.cypilot-workspace.toml` located at `project_root` (no\nparent-directory walk-up). Mention that discovery does not search parent\ndirectories and that this behavior affects `CypilotContext` → `WorkspaceContext`\ncreation and `SourceContext` per source.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>architecture/features/core-infra.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`379-381`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Include the project-root standalone fallback in this context-loading step.**\n\n`WorkspaceContext.load(primary_ctx)` can also succeed via a standalone `.cypilot-workspace.toml` at `project_root` when `core.toml` has no `workspace` key. As written, this flow makes the workspace upgrade path look narrower than the implemented discovery behavior.\n \n\nBased on learnings, `find_workspace_config()` first checks the `workspace` key in `core.toml`, then falls back to `.cypilot-workspace.toml` at `project_root`, with no parent-directory traversal.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@architecture/features/core-infra.md` around lines 379 - 381, Update the\ncontext-loading step to note that WorkspaceContext.load(primary_ctx) uses\nfind_workspace_config(), which first checks core.toml's workspace key and then\nfalls back to a standalone .cypilot-workspace.toml at project_root (no parent\ntraversal); explicitly mention this project-root standalone fallback in step 7\n(inst-ctx-workspace-upgrade) so the workspace upgrade/discovery path matches the\nimplemented behavior.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`2809-2836`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Expand the compressed helper/assertion blocks.**\n\nThis diff still has multiple semicolon-packed statements that Ruff flags as `E702`, so the test file won't pass lint as-is.\n\n  \n```shell\n#!/bin/bash\nruff check tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py --select E702 -n\n```\n\n\nAlso applies to: 2883-2893, 2992-3073, 3171-3191\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py` around lines 2809 - 2836, Several class and\nvariable definitions in the test (e.g., class _FakeMeta, class _FakeInnerCtx,\nclass _FakePrimary and their attribute assignments like systems = []; kits = {};\nregistered_systems = set(); _errors = []) use semicolon-compressed statements\nwhich trigger Ruff E702; expand each semicolon-separated statement into its own\nline so each assignment or def is on its own line (also apply same fix to other\ncompressed blocks around the indicated ranges), keeping behavior the same —\nlocate these symbols (_FakeMeta, _FakeInnerCtx, _FakePrimary, ws_ctx,\nvalidate_cmd.cmd_validate, WorkspaceContext) and replace compressed lines with\nseparate statements to satisfy the linter.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`48-53`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Compute a real relative path here.**\n\n`Path.relative_to()` only works for descendants. If the workspace file lives beside or above the scanned repos, this falls back to an absolute path and the generated manifest becomes machine-specific.\n\n  \n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n+import os\n+\n def _compute_source_path(entry: Path, output_dir: Path) -> str:\n     \"\"\"Compute relative source path from the output location.\"\"\"\n-    try:\n-        return str(entry.relative_to(output_dir).as_posix())\n-    except ValueError:\n-        return str(entry.as_posix())\n+    return Path(os.path.relpath(entry, output_dir)).as_posix()\n```\n</details>\n\n```shell\n#!/bin/bash\npython - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nentry = Path(\"/tmp/repos/service-a\")\noutput_dir = Path(\"/tmp/workspace\")\ntry:\n    print(entry.relative_to(output_dir))\nexcept Exception as exc:\n    print(type(exc).__name__)\nPY\n```\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py` around lines 48 -\n53, The current _compute_source_path uses Path.relative_to which only works for\ndescendant paths and falls back to an absolute path for siblings/parents; change\nit to compute a real relative path using os.path.relpath (or\npathlib.PurePath/resolve) so the manifest is portable. In _compute_source_path,\ncall os.path.relpath(entry, output_dir) (or Path(entry).resolve() and\nPath(output_dir).resolve() then os.path.relpath on those), normalize the result\nto POSIX separators (e.g., replace backslashes with '/') and return that string;\nkeep the fallback to entry.as_posix() only for unexpected errors.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`318-323`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Register non-default standalone manifests in `core.toml`.**\n\nThis only writes the standalone manifest. Any result outside `project_root / .cypilot-workspace.toml`—for example when `--root` points elsewhere or `--output` is set—won't be rediscovered by `workspace-add`/`workspace-info`, because no `workspace = \"<path>\"` reference is persisted into `core.toml`.\n\n \n\nBased on learnings: `find_workspace_config()` checks `core.toml` first and only auto-discovers a standalone `.cypilot-workspace.toml` at `project_root`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_init.py` around lines 318 -\n323, The standalone write path currently only writes the manifest file and does\nnot persist a reference into core.toml, so non-default locations (when\nargs.output or --root differs) are not discoverable by\nfind_workspace_config()/workspace-add/workspace-info; after calling\n_write_standalone (and on success), update core.toml to register the new\nworkspace path (e.g., add workspace = \"<path>\" under the appropriate section) —\nimplement this either inside _write_standalone or immediately after its return\nin the else branch, using the resolved output_path (or scan_root /\nWORKSPACE_CONFIG_FILENAME) and existing core.toml update helpers (or a small\nhelper like register_workspace_in_core) so find_workspace_config() will\nrediscover non-default standalone manifests.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_info.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`61-66`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Surface `load_artifacts_meta()` return errors.**\n\nThis helper only reports thrown exceptions. If `load_artifacts_meta()` returns an `err` string, the failure is dropped and the source just shows missing counts with no explanation.\n\n \n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n         meta, err = load_artifacts_meta(adapter_dir)\n-        if meta and not err:\n+        if err:\n+            info[\"metadata_error\"] = err\n+            return\n+        if meta:\n             info[\"artifact_count\"] = sum(1 for _ in meta.iter_all_artifacts())\n             info[\"system_count\"] = len(meta.systems)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_info.py` around lines 61 -\n66, The current try/except only records raised exceptions but ignores a returned\nerror from load_artifacts_meta(adapter_dir); update the handling around\nload_artifacts_meta in workspace_info.py so that when load_artifacts_meta\nreturns a non-empty err you set info[\"metadata_error\"] (or append) to include\nthat err string and skip populating counts, otherwise proceed to compute\ninfo[\"artifact_count\"] and info[\"system_count\"] from meta; reference the\nvariables meta and err and the call load_artifacts_meta(adapter_dir) to locate\nwhere to add this early error check.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/SKILL.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`167-173`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Keep the workspace quick commands JSON-only.**\n\nThese agent-facing examples still omit `--json`, so following them switches workspace flows back to human-formatted stdout and violates the “Agent-Safe Invocation” contract defined a few lines above.\n\n \n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-ALWAYS run `python3 {cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py workspace-init [--root <dir>] [--output <path>] [--inline] [--force] [--dry-run]` directly WHEN user invokes `cypilot workspace init`\n+ALWAYS run `python3 {cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py --json workspace-init [--root <dir>] [--output <path>] [--inline] [--force] [--dry-run]` directly WHEN user invokes `cypilot workspace init`\n\n-ALWAYS run `python3 {cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py workspace-add --name <name> (--path <path> | --url <url>) [--branch <branch>] [--role <role>] [--adapter <path>]` directly WHEN user invokes `cypilot workspace add <name> <path>` — auto-detects inline workspace and routes accordingly (no `--inline` flag needed when inline workspace already exists)\n+ALWAYS run `python3 {cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py --json workspace-add --name <name> (--path <path> | --url <url>) [--branch <branch>] [--role <role>] [--adapter <path>]` directly WHEN user invokes `cypilot workspace add <name> <path>` — auto-detects inline workspace and routes accordingly (no `--inline` flag needed when inline workspace already exists)\n\n-ALWAYS run `python3 {cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py workspace-add --inline --name <name> --path <path> [--role <role>] [--adapter <path>]` directly WHEN user invokes `cypilot workspace add --inline <name> <path>` — forces inline add; Git URL sources not supported in inline mode\n+ALWAYS run `python3 {cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py --json workspace-add --inline --name <name> --path <path> [--role <role>] [--adapter <path>]` directly WHEN user invokes `cypilot workspace add --inline <name> <path>` — forces inline add; Git URL sources not supported in inline mode\n\n-ALWAYS run `python3 {cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py workspace-info` directly WHEN user invokes `cypilot workspace info`\n+ALWAYS run `python3 {cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py --json workspace-info` directly WHEN user invokes `cypilot workspace info`\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/SKILL.md` around lines 167 - 173, The examples for\nagent-invoked workspace commands (the python3\n{cypilot_path}/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot.py invocation lines for\nworkspace-init, workspace-add (both inline and non-inline forms), and\nworkspace-info) must include the --json flag so they remain Agent-Safe; update\neach listed command to append --json (and ensure the inline vs non-inline\nworkspace-add forms preserve their existing flags like --inline, --name, --path,\n--url, --branch, --role, --adapter) so the agent receives machine-readable JSON\noutput.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🟡 Minor comments (9)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DESIGN.md-224-227 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`224-227`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Mention standalone workspace auto-discovery here.**\n\nThis paragraph currently reads as if workspace mode only comes from the `core.toml` `workspace` key, but the implementation also auto-discovers `.cypilot-workspace.toml` at `project_root`. Based on learnings: `find_workspace_config()` first checks `core.toml`, then falls back to a standalone `.cypilot-workspace.toml` at `project_root`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DESIGN.md around lines 224 - 227, Update the\nDESIGN text to mention standalone workspace auto-discovery: state that\nWorkspaceContext.load() (via find_workspace_config()) first checks the core.toml\nworkspace key and, if absent, falls back to locating a standalone\n.cypilot-workspace.toml at project_root; clarify that this fallback is\nintentional and that find_workspace_config() implements that precedence so\nreaders know workspace config can come from either core.toml or the standalone\nfile.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>requirements/workspace.md-111-120 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`111-120`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**The inline example is missing the required `version` field.**\n\n`_parse_inline_workspace()` rejects inline workspace definitions without `version`, so this example is not loadable as written. Please add `version = \"1.0\"` to the inline `core.toml` example.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@requirements/workspace.md` around lines 111 - 120, The inline core.toml\nexample is missing the required version field which causes\n_parse_inline_workspace() to reject it; update the inline `core.toml` sample for\nthe workspace (the block containing [workspace.sources.docs] and\n[workspace.sources.shared-kits]) to include a top-level version = \"1.0\" entry so\nthe parser accepts the inline workspace definition.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/specs/cli.md-729-731 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`729-731`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add a language tag to the new synopsis fences.**\n\nThese three fenced blocks are bare triple-backtick blocks