ke\nclear that [resolve] only covers workdir and namespace rules and does not\nprovide a global ref.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/workspace.md-67-70 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`67-70`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Fix the CLI spec reference path.**\n\nThis link points to `../specs/CLISPEC.md`, but the spec file in this PR is `../specs/cli.md`. As written, the reference will be dead on case-sensitive filesystems.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/workspace.md around lines 67 - 70,\nUpdate the CLI Spec link target so it points to the actual spec filename (use\n\"../specs/cli.md\" instead of \"../specs/CLISPEC.md\") in the \"CLI Spec\" entry;\nedit the markdown line that currently reads the CLI Spec link and replace the\nincorrect target so the link resolves on case-sensitive filesystems.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/workspace.md-114-115 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`114-115`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Use `cpt` in command examples, not `cypilot`.**\n\nThese flow steps document the executable as `cypilot`, but the CLI spec in this PR reserves `cypilot` for agent prompts and uses `cpt` as the command-line entry point.\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 177-177\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/workspace.md around lines 114 - 115,\nReplace the CLI executable name used in the flow steps from \"cypilot\" to \"cpt\"\nin the command examples (e.g., change \"cypilot workspace-init ...\" to \"cpt\nworkspace-init ...\") so the docs match the CLI spec; update all occurrences in\nthis file around the referenced flow steps (see identifiers\ninst-user-workspace-init and inst-find-project-root and the later occurrence at\nthe noted location) to use \"cpt\" instead of \"cypilot\".\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/workspace.md-427-427 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`427-427`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**This DoD narrows the supported `workspace-add` contract.**\n\nLine 427 says inline mode happens only when `--inline` is specified and that every source entry includes a `path`, but the rest of this PR documents two broader behaviors: `workspace-add` auto-detects an existing inline workspace, and standalone configs can add URL-backed sources. Please align this DoD with the actual command contract.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/workspace.md at line 427, Update the\nDoD for the workspace-add command to match the implemented contract: state that\ninline mode is selected either by the --inline flag or when an existing inline\nworkspace is auto-detected, and clarify that a source entry must include name\nand role while the path field may be a filesystem path or an external URL\n(allowing standalone configs to add URL-backed sources); keep adapter path\noptional, and preserve the requirements that type mismatches produce helpful\nerrors and all config writes preserve existing entries and settings for both\ninline and standalone modes.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/cypilot.clispec-752-760 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`752-760`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add the missing type token for `--inline`.**\n\nEvery other option in this spec uses the `name / <type> / description` shape. Line 759 drops the `<boolean>` field, which makes the workspace-add option table ambiguous for spec consumers.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-  --inline  Add source inline to config/core.toml instead of standalone workspace file\n+  --inline  <boolean>  Add source inline to config/core.toml instead of standalone workspace file\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/cypilot.clispec around lines 752 - 760, The\nOPTIONS table is missing the type token for the --inline flag; update the\nOPTIONS entry for \"--inline\" to follow the same \"name / <type> / description\"\npattern (i.e., change \"--inline  Add source inline to config/core.toml ...\" to\n\"--inline  <boolean>  Add source inline to config/core.toml ...\" in the OPTIONS\nblock so the workspace-add option table is unambiguous).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_info.py-163-180 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`163-180`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Show `metadata_error` in human mode too.**\n\n`_enrich_with_artifact_counts()` already writes `metadata_error` into the result, but `_fmt_source()` never renders it. In non-JSON runs, a broken registry looks like a healthy source with inexplicably missing counts.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n     if src.get(\"warning\"):\n         ui.substep(f\"    warning: {src['warning']}\")\n+    if src.get(\"metadata_error\"):\n+        ui.substep(f\"    metadata error: {src['metadata_error']}\")\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_info.py` around lines 163 -\n180, _fmt_source currently never prints the \"metadata_error\" field that\n_enrich_with_artifact_counts populates, causing broken registries to look\nhealthy in human output; update _fmt_source to check src.get(\"metadata_error\")\n(or is not None) and call ui.substep to render a line like \"    metadata_error:\n{src['metadata_error']}\" (place it near the artifacts/warning lines) so the\nhuman-mode output shows the error just as JSON mode does; reference symbols:\n_fmt_source, _enrich_with_artifact_counts, ui.substep, and the src dict key\n\"metadata_error\".\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`100-112`: **Consider using list unpacking for cleaner syntax.**\n\nThe Ruff RUF005 suggestion to use `[\"git\", *args]` instead of `[\"git\"] + args` is a minor style improvement.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested style improvement</summary>\n\n```diff\n         result = subprocess.run(\n-            [\"git\"] + args,\n+            [\"git\", *args],\n             cwd=cwd,\n             capture_output=True,\n             text=True,\n             timeout=_GIT_TIMEOUT,\n         )\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py` around lines 100 - 112,\nThe list concatenation in the subprocess.run call should use list unpacking for\ncleaner syntax: replace [\"git\"] + args with [\"git\", *args] inside the function\nthat calls subprocess.run (referring to the local variable args and the\nsubprocess.run invocation that uses _GIT_TIMEOUT and returns\nresult.returncode/result.stdout/result.stderr) so the command list is built with\n[\"git\", *args] for readability.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>ℹ️ Review info</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>⚙️ Run configuration</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defau