view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 14**\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> Some comments are outside the diff and can’t be posted inline due to platform limitations.\n> \n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>⚠️ Outside diff range comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/specs/cli.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `304-319`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Bring the `list-ids` spec back in sync with the parser.**\n> \n> This section still documents `--system` and `--format`, but `skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py` does not accept either flag. It also omits currently supported flags like `--artifact`, `--regex`, `--all`, and `--include-code`, so the spec now points users at unsupported invocations.\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n> \n> ```\n> Verify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n> \n> In @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/specs/cli.md around lines 304 - 319, The CLI\n> spec for the list-ids command is out of sync with the implementation in\n> list_ids.py: remove references to flags not accepted by the parser (`--system`,\n> `--format`) and add the currently supported flags (`--artifact`, `--regex`,\n> `--all`, `--include-code`) and `--pattern`/`--kind` semantics so the documented\n> options table and synopsis match the actual parser; update the example\n> invocation and options table in the list-ids section so each documented flag\n> corresponds to a real CLI argument parsed by list_ids.py and include short\n> descriptions matching the implementation.\n> ```\n> \n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_info.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`14-22`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Adapter validation inconsistent with workspace-init.**\n\nThe fallback validation checks for `AGENTS.md` existence, but `workspace_init.py` validates adapters by checking for the `config/` subdirectory (line 37: `(candidate / \"config\").is_dir()`). This inconsistency could cause `workspace-info` to report an adapter as missing when `workspace-init` would have accepted it.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🛠️ Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n def _probe_source_adapter(resolved: Path, explicit_adapter: Optional[Path]) -> Optional[Path]:\n     \"\"\"Find the adapter directory for a reachable source.\"\"\"\n     from ..utils.files import find_cypilot_directory\n\n     found = find_cypilot_directory(resolved)\n     if found is None and explicit_adapter is not None:\n-        if explicit_adapter.is_dir() and (explicit_adapter / \"AGENTS.md\").exists():\n+        if explicit_adapter.is_dir() and (explicit_adapter / \"config\").is_dir():\n             found = explicit_adapter\n     return found\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_info.py\naround lines 14 - 22, The fallback validation in _probe_source_adapter currently\naccepts an explicit_adapter only if AGENTS.md exists, which conflicts with\nworkspace_init's validation that checks for a config/ subdirectory; update\n_probe_source_adapter (the explicit_adapter branch) to check that\nexplicit_adapter is a directory and contains a \"config\" subdirectory (e.g.\n(explicit_adapter / \"config\").is_dir()) so the adapter acceptance logic matches\nworkspace_init's (and keep the existing use of find_cypilot_directory for the\nprimary resolution).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`86-102`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Filter by source on top of the shared workspace scan instead of re-walking `sc.meta`.**\n\nThis branch skips the primary registry entirely and rebuilds a per-source artifact walk. That misses primary-registry artifacts whose `artifact.source == args.source`, and it bypasses the artifact-role rules already enforced elsewhere for `codebase`/`kits` sources. Reuse the shared workspace collection flow here, then apply the source filter to its results.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`740-772`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Resolve `--artifact` against the workspace registry, not just the nearest repo.**\n\nThis helper still checks only the `meta` loaded from `artifact_path.parent`. A source-routed workspace artifact that lives outside that repo’s `project_root` will still fall through to “Artifact not in Cypilot registry” even when the active workspace registry references it. Prefer the current workspace context when available, or upgrade the discovered base context before matching paths.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py around\nlines 740 - 772, The function _resolve_single_artifact currently only queries\nctx.meta (from CypilotContext.load(artifact_path.parent)), so artifacts that are\nregistered in the active workspace registry are missed; update the logic to\nprefer the active workspace context when available and to try matching against\nthe workspace registry if the local ctx lookup fails. Concretely: after loading\nctx = CypilotContext.load(artifact_path.parent), fetch workspace_ctx =\nCypilotContext.active() (or equivalent) and attempt computing rel_path relative\nto both ctx.project_root and workspace_ctx.project_root (handling ValueError)\nand call ctx.meta.get_artifact_by_path(rel_path) then\nworkspace_ctx.meta.get_artifact_by_path(rel_path) if the first lookup returns\nnothing; if workspace_ctx yields a match, return that context (workspace_ctx)\nand the artifact tuple, otherwise preserve the existing error behavior. Ensure\nreferences use _resolve_single_artifact, CypilotContext.load,\nCypilotContext.active (or current workspace accessor), and\nmeta.get_artifact_by_path.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`740-772`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Resolve `--artifact` against the workspace registry, not just the nearest repo.**\n\nThis helper still checks only the `meta` loaded from `artifact_path.parent`. A source-routed workspace artifact that lives outside that repo’s `project_root` will still fall through to “Artifact not in Cypilot registry” even when the active workspace registry references it. Prefer the current workspace context when available, or upgrade the discovered base context before matching paths.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py` around lines 740 - 772, The\nhelper _resolve_single_artifact currently loads context with\nCypilotContext.load(artifact_path.parent) and only checks that ctx.meta, which\nmisses workspace-registered artifacts outside the repo; change resolution to\nprefer the active workspace context when present (e.g., obtain a workspace\nCypilotContext via the existing API such as CypilotContext.current() or\nequivalent) and use its project_root/meta to compute relative paths and call\nmeta.get_artifact_by_path first; if that fails, fall back to the original\ndiscovered context (from CypilotContext.load) or \"upgrade\" the discovered\ncontext to the workspace context before calling ctx.meta.get_artifact_by_path so\nsource-routed workspace artifacts are found. Ensure you still return the same\ntuple shape (ctx, artifacts, error_message) and keep use of artifact_path,\nctx.project_root, and meta.get_artifact_by_path in the matching logic.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`109-122`: **Consider using spread syntax for subprocess args.**\n\nThe static analysis suggests using `[\"git\", *args]` instead of concatenation for clarity and slight performance benefit.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Minor style improvement</summary>\n\n```diff\n     try:\n         result = subprocess.run(\n-            [\"git\"] + args,\n+            [\"git\", *args],\n             cwd=cwd,\n             capture_output=True,\n             text=True,\n             timeout=_GIT_TIMEOUT,\n         )\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py around\nlines 109 - 122, Replace the list concatenation used when invoking\nsubprocess.run with spread-unpacking for clarity: change the arguments\nexpression from [\"git\"] + args to [\"git\", *args] in the subprocess.run call that\nuses the variable args (the block returning (result.returncode, result.stdout,\nresult.stderr)); keep the same cwd, capture_output, text, and timeout parameters\nand preserve the existing exception handling for FileNotFoundError and\nsubprocess.TimeoutExpired.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cursor/commands/cypilot-workspace.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-4`: **Missing blank line between header and content.**\n\nMinor formatting: add a blank line after line 2 for visual separation between the header and the instruction.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested formatting</summary>\n\n```diff\n # /cypilot-workspace\n \n+\n ALWAYS open and follow `{cypilot_path}/.core/workflows/workspace.md`\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cursor/commands/cypilot-workspace.md around lines 1 - 4, The header line \"#\n/cypilot-workspace\" is immediately followed by content with no blank line;\ninsert a single blank line after the header (the line containing \"#\n/cypilot-workspace\") so the instruction \"ALWAYS open and follow\n`{cypilot_path}/.core/workflows/workspace.md`\" is separated visually and\nconforms to Markdown formatting.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>requirements/workspace.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`128-131`: **Table entry for `path` could be more precise.**\n\nLine 130 states the path is \"resolved relative to workspace file location,\" but Lines 165-168 clarify that inline `[workspace]` paths resolve relative to the project root. Consider updating the table description to note both modes, or adding \"See [Path Resolution](`#path-resolution`)\" for clarity.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `path`   | string | Yes (unless `url` is set)         | —           | Local filesystem path, resolved relative to workspace file location. Takes precedence over `url` when both set. |\n+| `path`   | string | Yes (unless `url` is set)         | —           | Local filesystem path. See [Path Resolution](`#path-resolution`) for resolution rules. Takes precedence over `url` when both set. |\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@requirements/workspace.md` around lines 128 - 131, Update the `path` table\nentry to clarify resolution behavior for both standalone workspace files and\ninline `[workspace]` blocks: change the description for the `path` field to\nmention that it is resolved relative to the workspace file location for\nstandalone `.cypilot-workspace.toml` files and relative to the project root for\ninline `[workspace]` configs (or append \"See Path Resolution\" with a link\n`(`#path-resolution`)`), so readers can find the detailed rules; modify the `path`\nrow in the table (the `path` field entry) accordingly.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`2167-2197`: **Assert the dispatched handler, not just “some CLI exit code”.**\n\nThese tests pass for `0`, `1`, and `2`, which also covers argparse failures and many unrelated command errors. That means a miswired subcommand can still look green here. Patch the target `cmd_*` function or assert a command-specific output shape so the dispatcher branch is actually protected.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py` around lines 2167 - 2197, The tests only check\nprocess exit codes via _run_cli_dispatch, so miswired subcommands still pass;\nupdate each relevant test (test_dispatch_spec_coverage, test_dispatch_migrate,\ntest_dispatch_migrate_config, test_dispatch_workspace_init,\ntest_dispatch_workspace_add, test_dispatch_workspace_info, test_dispatch_agents,\ntest_json_flag) to assert the actual handler was invoked or produced a\ncommand-specific output: either mock/patch the target handler functions\n(cmd_spec_coverage, cmd_migrate, cmd_migrate_config, cmd_workspace_init,\ncmd_workspace_add, cmd_workspace_info, cmd_agents, and the self-check handler)\nand assert they were called with expected args, or assert that _run_cli_dispatch\nreturned/printed a unique string or JSON shape only produced by that handler, so\nthe dispatcher branch is verified rather than any exit code.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DESIGN.md:\n- Line 224: Update the workspace discovery docs to describe the two-step\ndiscovery used by find_workspace_config(): first check the core.toml workspace\nkey (string path or inline dict) and, if absent, auto-discover a standalone\n.cypilot-workspace.toml at project_root as an intentional fallback; mention this\nbehavior in the WorkspaceContext.load() description and in any text that\ncurrently only references core.toml so readers know the standalone file is\nauto-detected.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/workspace.md:\n- Around line 161-162: The DoD text is inconsistent: the description for\nworkspace-add / inst-add-auto-detect-inline states auto-routing to inline when\nan existing workspace is inline, but a later DoD paragraph implies inline only\napplies with a --inline flag; update the DoD text so both places match the\nimplemented contract by explicitly stating that workspace-add auto-detects\ninline workspaces and auto-routes to inline add without requiring --inline\n(remove or reword the language that implies inline is only enabled via the\n--inline flag), and ensure the references to inst-add-auto-detect-inline and\nworkspace-add reflect this same behavior.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/requirements/workspace.md:\n- Around line 267-278: The CLI docs omit the Git-source update contract and\nstill imply implicit fetching; update the workspace command table and the\nexplanatory text (the block mentioning \"cloned or fetched as needed\") to\nexplicitly state the Git behavior: \"clone on first resolution; subsequent\nfetch/update must be performed only via the `workspace-sync` command\" and add\n`workspace-sync` to the table alongside `workspace-init`, `workspace-add`,\n`workspace-info`, `validate`, and `list-ids`; ensure the table row for\nGit/url-managed sources notes clone-on-first-resolution and that updates are\nperformed only with `workspace-sync`, and update the surrounding prose to\nreflect the same contract.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/cli.py:\n- Around line 121-144: The new workspace dispatch helpers (_cmd_workspace_init,\n_cmd_workspace_add, _cmd_workspace_info, _cmd_workspace_sync) are outside the\nexisting code↔artifact traceability blocks; wrap each helper block with the\nappropriate `@cpt-`* markers (e.g., add a matching `@cpt-start/`<tag> before the\nfirst helper and `@cpt-end/`<tag> after the last, or surround each function with\n`@cpt-`<name>-start and `@cpt-`<name>-end) so these entry points are covered by\ncode↔artifact traceability per the coding guidelines.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py:\n- Around line 596-600: When calling load_artifacts_meta(adapter_dir) capture and\nrecord failures on the SourceContext so broken remote registries aren't silently\ndropped: if load_artifacts_meta returns an err, set the SourceContext's error\nfield (e.g., sc.error or SourceContext.error) to that err while leaving meta as\nNone; only set meta and reg_systems from m when err is falsy (preserve the\nexisting m/err check). Update the same pattern for the other block mentioned\n(lines ~604-614) so any err returned by load_artifacts_meta is stored on the\nSourceContext for later visibility.\n\nIn `@schemas/workspace.schema.json`:\n- Around line 118-135: Add a workspace-level default branch property to the\nresolve_config object: add a \"default_branch\" property (type: string) to the\n\"resolve_config\" schema with a concise description like \"Workspace-level default\nbranch for Git sources; can be overridden per-source\" so configs that rely on a\nworkspace default validate correctly; update the \"resolve_config\" properties\nblock (the object named resolve_config) to include this field and ensure it\nrespects additionalProperties:false.\n- Around line 69-99: The schema currently allows both path and url together and\npermits branch on path-only entries; change the validation to make local (path)\nand git-backed (url) sources mutually exclusive and only allow branch for\ngit-backed entries by replacing the current anyOf with a oneOf having two\nsubschemas: one subschema requires [\"path\"] and explicitly forbids\n[\"url\",\"branch\"] (e.g., include \"not\": {\"required\":[\"url\"]} and \"not\":\n{\"required\":[\"branch\"]} or equivalent) and the other subschema requires [\"url\"]\nand forbids [\"path\"] while allowing \"branch\"; ensure the existing property\ndefinitions for \"path\",\"url\",\"branch\",\"adapter\",\"role\" are referenced/kept\ninside the appropriate subschema so validation enforces exclusivity and branch\nonly for url-based sources.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/adapter_info.py`:\n- Around line 352-358: The info command is eagerly calling\nws_cfg.resolve_source_path(name) which can clone Git URL sources and perform\nnetwork I/O; change the loop that builds ws_info[\"sources\"] to avoid\nresolve_source_path for remote/git URLs: detect Git/URL sources from src.path or\nsrc.role, and for those set \"reachable\" to a non-mutating value like \"not\ncloned\" (or probe the local cache directory existence only) instead of calling\nresolve_source_path; for local/path sources still test reachability with\nresolved.is_dir() via resolve_source_path as before. Ensure you update the loop\nthat iterates ws_cfg.sources.items() and references resolve_source_path/name/src\nto use this non-mutating check so info no longer mutates the workspace or\ntriggers network I/O.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py`:\n- Around line 79-80: The workspace mode uses a primary-only set for kind\ninference because _match_system_prefix() currently reads ctx.registered_systems;\nchange it to use the workspace-wide set when is_workspace is true by calling\nctx.get_all_registered_systems() instead of ctx.registered_systems (retain\nctx.registered_systems for non-workspace cases), so that artifacts_to_scan /\nremote IDs get proper kind values during kind inference in\n_match_system_prefix().\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_add.py`:\n- Around line 143-152: The code ignores workspace-discovery errors returned by\nfind_workspace_config(project_root) and proceeds to _add_to_inline(), allowing\ncreation of an inline config next to a malformed standalone file; change the\nunpack to capture the error (e.g., existing, ws_err =\nfind_workspace_config(project_root)), and if ws_err is not None return a fatal\nerror via ui.result (status \"ERROR\" with the error message) and return 1 so you\ndo not call _add_to_inline(); keep the existing parallel-config check for\nexisting workspaces intact (check existing.is_inline only after confirming\nws_err is None).\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_info.py`:\n- Around line 44-46: The warning message uses src.path which can be None for\nURL-only Git sources; update the unreachable branch in the function that builds\ninfo (the block checking reachable and setting info[\"warning\"]) to prefer a\nresolved path or URL—use src.checkout_path if available, otherwise src.url, and\nfall back to src.path—so the message reads something like \"Source directory not\nreachable: <best available identifier>\" instead of showing \"None\".\n- Around line 14-22: The helper _probe_source_adapter currently probes the repo\nfirst and only uses explicit_adapter as a fallback, which can return a wrong\nnested adapter; change the logic to first check explicit_adapter: if\nexplicit_adapter is provided and points to a directory containing \"AGENTS.md\",\nreturn explicit_adapter immediately, otherwise fall back to calling\nfind_cypilot_directory(resolved). Apply the same reorder/fix to the similar\nprobe helper referenced around lines 48-52 so explicit adapter paths are always\nhonored before repo-wide discovery.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py`:\n- Around line 596-600: When load_artifacts_meta(adapter_dir) returns an error,\nrecord that error onto the SourceContext so the remote registry failure is\nvisible instead of silently making sc.meta None; specifically, in the block\nwhere m, err = load_artifacts_meta(adapter_dir) (and the similar block at the\nlater 604-614 region), set the SourceContext.error (or appropriate field on the\ncurrent SourceContext instance) to err when err is truthy, and only set\nmeta/reg_systems when m is present and err is falsy—ensure you're updating the\nsame SourceContext instance used later (e.g., the object that uses sc.meta) so\nworkspace scans can see the recorded error.\n\nIn `@vulture_whitelist.py`:\n- Line 7: The code imports WorkspaceConfig and calls a nonexistent method\nget_reachable_sources which will raise AttributeError when vulture_whitelist is\nimported; open the WorkspaceConfig implementation to find the correct API (e.g.,\nthe actual method or property that returns reachable/source files such as\nget_sources, sources, reachable_sources_property, or similar) and update\nvulture_whitelist to call that real method/property instead of\nget_reachable_sources, or alternatively add a thin wrapper method named\nget_reachable_sources on WorkspaceConfig that delegates to the existing\nimplementation; ensure you update all occurrences (including the other reported\nspot) to reference the corrected symbol so imports no longer fail.\n\n---\n\nOutside diff comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/specs/cli.md:\n- Around line 304-319: The CLI spec for the list-ids command is out of sync with\nthe implementation in list_ids.py: remove references to flags not accepted by\nthe parser (`--system`, `--format`) and add the currently supported flags\n(`--artifact`, `--regex`, `--all`, `--include-code`) and `--pattern`/`--kind`\nsemantics so the documented