er `url` when both set. |\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@requirements/workspace.md` at line 132, Update the \"path\" table row text to\nexplicitly state both resolution behaviors: when specified in the workspace file\nit is resolved relative to the workspace file location, but when provided inline\nin a [workspace] source it is resolved relative to the project root; also keep\nthe note that `path` takes precedence over `url` when both are set. Reference\nthe `path` table row in requirements/workspace.md and the inline `[workspace]`\nsource behavior described around lines discussing project root resolution.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/workspace.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`65-65`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Unify `workspace-add` inline contract (auto-route vs flag-only wording).**\n\nLine 65 and Lines 512-513 imply inline operation requires `--inline`, but Line 164 states existing inline workspaces are auto-routed without the flag. Please align these sections to one behavior.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested diff</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| `cpt-cypilot-actor-user` | Runs `workspace-init`, `workspace-add` (with `--inline` flag for inline mode), `workspace-info`, `workspace-sync`; uses `validate --local-only` and `list-ids --source` |\n+| `cpt-cypilot-actor-user` | Runs `workspace-init`, `workspace-add` (auto-routes to inline when existing workspace is inline; `--inline` can force inline creation path), `workspace-info`, `workspace-sync`; uses `validate --local-only` and `list-ids --source` |\n```\n\n```diff\n-The system **MUST** provide a `workspace-add` command (with `--inline` flag for inline mode) to add sources to existing workspace configs. Each source entry **MUST** include name, path, optional adapter path, and role. `workspace-add` **MUST** operate on standalone config by default, and on inline config when `--inline` is specified. Type mismatches **MUST** produce errors directing users to the correct flag. If a source with the same name already exists, `workspace-add` **MUST** return an error unless `--force` is specified; with `--force`, the existing entry is silently replaced. All config writes **MUST** preserve existing entries and settings.\n+The system **MUST** provide a `workspace-add` command to add sources to existing workspace configs. Each source entry **MUST** include name, path, optional adapter path, and role. `workspace-add` **MUST** operate on standalone configs by default and **MUST** auto-route to inline updates when the detected workspace is inline (without requiring `--inline`). The `--inline` flag **MAY** be used to force inline mode when no standalone workspace exists. Type mismatches **MUST** produce errors directing users to the correct mode. If a source with the same name already exists, `workspace-add` **MUST** return an error unless `--force` is specified; with `--force`, the existing entry is silently replaced. All config writes **MUST** preserve existing entries and settings.\n```\n</details>\n\n \n\n\nAlso applies to: 512-513\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/features/workspace.md at line 65, The\ndocumentation is inconsistent about whether inline workspaces require the\n--inline flag or are auto-routed; decide on one behavior (either flag-only or\nauto-route) and update all mentions to match: revise the\n`cpt-cypilot-actor-user` row that lists `workspace-add` (currently saying\n`--inline`), the paragraph that currently describes existing inline workspaces\nas auto-routed, and the other references around the later section so they all\nstate the chosen behavior for `workspace-add`/inline mode consistently; ensure\nverbs and examples (e.g., `workspace-init`, `workspace-add`, `workspace-info`,\n`workspace-sync`) reflect the single contract you choose.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`91-96`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Reject absolute template outputs instead of silently rewriting them.**\n\nLine 93 strips leading `/` before validation, so absolute template outputs can be accepted as rewritten relative paths. That weakens the safety contract in this function’s docstring and can hide misconfiguration.\n \n\n<details>\n<summary>🛠️ Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n def _apply_template(template: str, org: str, repo: str) -> str:\n@@\n-    result = template.replace(\"{org}\", org).replace(\"{repo}\", repo)\n-    # Strip leading slash that appears when org is empty (\"{org}/{repo}\" → \"/repo\")\n-    result = result.lstrip(\"/\")\n-    # Reject path traversal: absolute paths or \"..\" segments\n-    if not result or result.startswith(\"/\") or \"..\" in Path(result).parts:\n+    rendered = template.replace(\"{org}\", org).replace(\"{repo}\", repo)\n+    if not rendered:\n+        raise ValueError(f\"Unsafe path template result: {rendered!r}\")\n+\n+    # Allow the default empty-org case only (\"{org}/{repo}\" -> \"/repo\")\n+    if org == \"\" and template.strip() == \"{org}/{repo}\" and rendered.startswith(\"/\"):\n+        rendered = rendered[1:]\n+\n+    path_obj = Path(rendered)\n+    # Reject path traversal: absolute paths or \"..\" segments\n+    if path_obj.is_absolute() or \"..\" in path_obj.parts:\n+        raise ValueError(f\"Unsafe path template result: {rendered!r}\")\n+\n+    result = rendered\n+    if not result:\n         raise ValueError(f\"Unsafe path template result: {result!r}\")\n     return result\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py around\nlines 91 - 96, The code currently calls result = result.lstrip(\"/\") before\nvalidating, which can hide absolute template outputs; remove the lstrip(\"/\")\nrewrite and validate the raw template expansion instead: after computing result\n= template.replace(\"{org}\", org).replace(\"{repo}\", repo) check if not result or\nresult.startswith(\"/\") or \"..\" in Path(result).parts and raise the ValueError as\nshown, only then (if you still need to normalize) perform any safe\nnormalization; update the logic around the variable result and the Path(...)\ncheck in git_utils.py so absolute paths are rejected rather than silently\nrewritten.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`171-194`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**`--source` is not applied to code scanning when `--include-code` is enabled.**\n\nLine 173 still scans `ctx.meta`/`ctx.project_root`, so source-filtered runs can include primary-repo code hits.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n     if args.include_code and not args.artifact and ctx:\n-        # Scan codebase entries from context\n-        for cb_entry, _system_node in ctx.meta.iter_all_codebase():\n-            code_path = (ctx.project_root / cb_entry.path).resolve()\n+        code_meta = ctx.meta\n+        code_root = ctx.project_root\n+        if args.source and is_workspace:\n+            source_ctx = next(\n+                (sc for sc in ctx.sources.values()\n+                 if sc.name == args.source and sc.reachable and sc.meta is not None and sc.path is not None),\n+                None,\n+            )\n+            if source_ctx is None:\n+                ui.result({\"status\": \"ERROR\", \"message\": f\"Source '{args.source}' not found or not reachable\"})\n+                return 1\n+            code_meta = source_ctx.meta\n+            code_root = source_ctx.path\n+\n+        # Scan codebase entries from selected context\n+        for cb_entry, _system_node in code_meta.iter_all_codebase():\n+            code_path = (code_root / cb_entry.path).resolve()\n             extensions = cb_entry.extensions or [\".py\"]\n@@\n-                if rel and ctx.meta.is_ignored(rel):\n+                if rel and code_meta.is_ignored(rel):\n                     continue\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py around\nlines 171 - 194, The code-path that scans codebase entries (when\nargs.include_code is true) ignores the args.source filter; update the loop that\niterates ctx.meta.iter_all_codebase() to skip entries not matching the requested\nsource: check args.source (or args.source.split/normalize if multi) and continue\nwhen cb_entry.source (or the entry's source/origin attribute) does not match;\nthis filtering should be applied before resolving cb_entry.path and before\ncollecting files so ctx.meta/ctx.project_root scans only include entries from\nthe requested source.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>architecture/features/workspace.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`443-450`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Reorder `inst-validate-source-flag` so it is not documented after a return step.**\n\nThe current ordering places the override step after the default return path, which reads as unreachable in a sequential algorithm.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested wording</summary>\n\n```diff\n-1. [x] - `p1` - Resolve target file to absolute path - `inst-target-resolve-abs`\n-2. [x] - `p1` - **FOR EACH** source in `workspace.sources` (ordered by path length descending for longest-prefix match) - `inst-target-foreach-source`\n+1. [x] - `p1` - `validate --source` / `--artifact` override: narrow working context to the matched source adapter for registry and project root, while retaining the original `WorkspaceContext` for workspace-level operations - `inst-validate-source-flag`\n+2. [x] - `p1` - Resolve target file to absolute path - `inst-target-resolve-abs`\n+3. [x] - `p1` - **FOR EACH** source in `workspace.sources` (ordered by path length descending for longest-prefix match) - `inst-target-foreach-source`\n@@\n-3. [x] - `p1` - **RETURN** `(None, primary_context)` — file belongs to primary repo - `inst-target-return-primary`\n-4. [x] - `p1` - `validate --source` / `--artifact` override: narrow working context to the matched source adapter for registry and project root, while retaining the original `WorkspaceContext` for workspace-level operations (cross-repo path routing, ID expansion, config validation) - `inst-validate-source-flag`\n+4. [x] - `p1` - **RETURN** `(None, primary_context)` — file belongs to primary repo - `inst-target-return-primary`\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@architecture/features/workspace.md` around lines 443 - 450, Move the\n`inst-validate-source-flag` step out of its current position after\n`inst-target-return-primary` so it is evaluated before any return; specifically,\nafter `inst-target-return-source-no-adapter` (or immediately after the\nmatched-source logic) insert `inst-validate-source-flag` to narrow the working\ncontext when `--source`/`--artifact` overrides are present, then proceed to the\nremaining return steps (`inst-target-return-source` /\n`inst-target-return-primary`) so the override is not documented as unreachable.\nEnsure the description references `WorkspaceContext` and that\nregistry/project-root narrowing occurs only for the matched source adapter while\nkeeping workspace-level semantics intact.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_adapter_info.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`615-670`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Preserve and restore the previous JSON mode instead of hard-coding `True`.**\n\nThe tests mutate a process-global flag and always restore to `True`, which can leak state into surrounding tests when the prior mode was different.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧪 Suggested patch</summary>\n\n```diff\n     def test_workspace_active_in_human_output(self):\n         \"\"\"Human formatter renders active workspace info.\"\"\"\n         from cypilot.commands.adapter_info import _human_info\n-        from cypilot.utils.ui import set_json_mode\n+        from cypilot.utils.ui import is_json_mode, set_json_mode\n+        prev_json_mode = is_json_mode()\n         set_json_mode(False)\n         try:\n@@\n             self.assertIn(\"inline (core.toml)\", output)\n             self.assertIn(\"2\", output)\n         finally:\n-            set_json_mode(True)\n+            set_json_mode(prev_json_mode)\n\n@@\n     def test_workspace_error_in_human_output(self):\n         \"\"\"Human formatter renders workspace error as warning.\"\"\"\n         from cypilot.commands.adapter_info import _human_info\n-        from cypilot.utils.ui import set_json_mode\n+        from cypilot.utils.ui import is_json_mode, set_json_mode\n+        prev_json_mode = is_json_mode()\n         set_json_mode(False)\n         try:\n@@\n             output = buf.getvalue()\n             self.assertIn(\"config parse failed\", output)\n         finally:\n-            set_json_mode(True)\n+            set_json_mode(prev_json_mode)\n\n class TestHumanInfoFormatterBranches(unittest.TestCase):\n@@\n     def setUp(self):\n-        from cypilot.utils.ui import set_json_mode\n+        from cypilot.utils.ui import is_json_mode, set_json_mode\n+        self._prev_json_mode = is_json_mode()\n         set_json_mode(False)\n\n     def tearDown(self):\n         from cypilot.utils.ui import set_json_mode\n-        set_json_mode(True)\n+        set_json_mode(self._prev_json_mode)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_adapter_info.py` around lines 615 - 670, Capture the previous JSON\nmode before calling set_json_mode(False) and restore that saved value instead of\nhard-coding set_json_mode(True); e.g., in tests (both the try/finally blocks in\ntest_workspace_active_in_human_output/test_workspace_error_in_human_output and\nthe TestHumanInfoFormatterBranches setUp/tearDown) read the current mode into a\nlocal variable (using the existing API to query the current mode — e.g. a getter\nor module-level flag from cypilot.utils.ui), call set_json_mode(False) for the\ntest, and then call set_json_mode(previous_mode) in the finally/tearDown so the\nprior global state is preserved.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`119-122`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Redact clone stderr before logging failures.**\n\nLine 121 still prints raw `err`. Git often echoes the full remote URL in stderr, which can leak tokens/userinfo to logs.\n\n \n<details>\n<summary>🔐 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n     rc, _out, err = _run_git(clone_args)\n     if rc != 0:\n-        print(f\"Warning: git clone failed for {_redact_url(url)}: {err}\", file=sys.stderr)\n+        safe_err = err.replace(url, _redact_url(url))\n+        print(f\"Warning: git clone failed for {_redact_url(url)}: {safe_err}\", file=sys.stderr)\n         return None\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/git_utils.py` around lines 119 - 122,\nThe git clone failure log prints raw stderr which can leak credentials; before\nlogging in the clone block that calls _run_git (where rc, _out, err =\n_run_git(clone_args)), sanitize/redact any URLs in the err output using the\nexisting _redact_url helper (or a small sanitizer that strips userinfo from\nURLs) and then include the redacted stderr in the process instead of raw err\n(e.g., build a message like \"git clone failed for <redacted url>:\n<redacted_err>\" and print that to sys.stderr); ensure you reference the same\n_run_git and _redact_url helpers so the change is localized to this clone\nfailure path.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`2803-2811`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Expand semicolon-compressed fake classes to pass Ruff E702.**\n\nLines 2803 and 2811 still use multiple statements per line, which is a lint blocker.\n\n \n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n         class _FakeMeta:\n-            systems = []; kits = {}\n+            systems = []\n+            kits = {}\n             def get_artifact_by_path(self, _rel): return None\n             def iter_all_artifacts(self): return iter([])\n             def is_ignored(self, _rel): return False\n@@\n         class _FakeInnerCtx:\n             meta = _FakeMeta()\n             project_root = Path(\"/fake\")\n             adapter_dir = Path(\"/fake/adapter\")\n-            registered_systems = set(); kits = {}; _errors = []\n+            registered_systems = set()\n+            kits = {}\n+            _errors = []\n             def get_known_id_kinds(self): return set()\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py` around lines 2803 - 2811, The fake helper\nclasses use multiple statements on one line (semicolon compression) causing Ruff\nE702; expand each statement into its own line: split the combined assignments\nand dict/sequence initializations in the _FakeMeta class (systems = []; kits =\n{}) and the _FakeInnerCtx block (registered_systems = set(); kits = {}; _errors\n= []) into separate lines, and ensure method definitions like\nget_artifact_by_path, iter_all_artifacts, and is_ignored remain as standalone\ndef lines with their returns on the next line so each statement is on its own\nline.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`2540-2545`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Guard symlink-dependent test setup for environments without symlink privilege.**\n\nLine 2544 can fail on Windows/restricted CI and make this test flaky.\n\n \n<details>\n<summary>🧪 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-            (src / \"ext.py\").symlink_to(outside_file)\n+            try:\n+                (src / \"ext.py\").symlink_to(outside_file)\n+            except (OSError, NotImplementedError, PermissionError):\n+                self.skipTest(\"Symlink creation is not supported in this environment\")\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@tests/test_cli_py_coverage.py` around lines 2540 - 2545, The test creates a\nsymlink using outside_file and (src / \"ext.py\").symlink_to(outside_file) which\ncan fail on Windows or restricted CI; wrap the symlink creation in a try/except\nthat catches OSError/PermissionError/NotImplementedError and fallback to a\nnon-symlink approach (e.g., copy the file with shutil.copy or call pytest.skip\nwith an explanatory message), or first test for os.symlink support via\nhasattr(os, \"symlink\") and os.geteuid() where available; update the block that\nwrites outside_file and the symlink_to call to use this guarded logic so the\ntest is not flaky in environments without symlink privilege.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DESIGN.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`230-230`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Document the full workspace discovery order (not just `core.toml`).**\n\nLine 230 currently implies `WorkspaceContext.load()` only discovers workspace config through `core.toml.workspace`. It should also document the fallback auto-discovery of `.cypilot-workspace.toml` at `project_root`; otherwise the behavior is described inaccurately.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested wording update</summary>\n\n```diff\n-... WorkspaceContext.load() discovers workspace config via the core.toml `workspace` key (string path or inline dict).\n+... WorkspaceContext.load() discovers workspace config in two steps: (1) `core.toml` `workspace` key (string path or inline dict), then (2) fallback auto-discovery of `.cypilot-workspace.toml` at `project_root`.\n```\n</details>\n\n \n\nBased on learnings: `find_workspace_config()` first checks `core.toml.workspace`, then falls back to `.cypilot-workspace.toml` at `project_root`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/DESIGN.md at line 230, The documentation\ncurrently states WorkspaceContext.load() discovers workspace config only via\ncore.toml.workspace; update the DESIGN.md wording to document the full discovery\norder: call out that find_workspace_config() first checks core.toml.workspace\n(string path or inline dict) and, if absent, falls back to auto-discovering a\n.cypilot-workspace.toml file at the project_root; mention that the same\nprecedence applies when resolving named sources to SourceContext and that\nresolve_artifact_path behavior (returning Optional[Path]) remains unchanged.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`792-809`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n\n**Make `--artifact` lookup workspace-aware.**\n\nLine 792+ resolves `--artifact` against a standalone `CypilotContext` and local relpath lookup only. This can reject valid workspace/source-routed artifacts outside that context’s `project_root`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-def _resolve_single_artifact(\n-    artifact_arg: str,\n-) -> Tuple[\n-    Optional[CypilotContext],\n+def _resolve_single_artifact(\n+    artifact_arg: str,\n+) -> Tuple[\n+    Optional[Union[CypilotContext, WorkspaceContext]],\n     List[Tuple[Path, str]],\n     Optional[str],\n ]:\n@@\n-    ctx = CypilotContext.load(artifact_path.parent)\n-    if not ctx:\n-        return None, [], \"Cypilot not initialized. Run 'cypilot init' first.\"\n-\n-    try:\n-        rel_path = artifact_path.relative_to(ctx.project_root).as_posix()\n-    except ValueError:\n-        rel_path = None\n-\n-    artifacts: List[Tuple[Path, str]] = []\n-    if rel_path:\n-        result = ctx.meta.get_artifact_by_path(rel_path)\n-        if result:\n-            artifact_meta, _system_node = result\n-            artifacts.append((artifact_path, str(artifact_meta.kind)))\n+    base_ctx = CypilotContext.load(artifact_path.parent)\n+    if base_ctx is None:\n+        ctx = get_context()\n+        if ctx is None:\n+            return None, [], \"Cypilot not initialized. Run 'cypilot init' first.\"\n+    else:\n+        ws_ctx = WorkspaceContext.load(base_ctx)\n+        ctx = ws_ctx if ws_ctx is not None else base_ctx\n+\n+    all_artifacts, _ = collect_artifacts_to_scan(ctx)\n+    artifacts = [(p, k) for p, k in all_artifacts if p == artifact_path]\n@@\n     if not artifacts:\n         return None, [], f\"Artifact not in Cypilot registry: {artifact_arg}\"\n     return ctx, artifacts, None\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/context.py` around lines 792 - 809, The\nlookup currently only tries ctx.project_root-relative paths (CypilotContext.load\n+ artifact_path.relative_to + ctx.meta.get_artifact_by_path) which rejects\nartifacts in other workspace roots; change the logic so that if relative_to\nfails or get_artifact_by_path returns nothing, you also attempt workspace-aware\nlookups: try artifact_path.as_posix() and artifact_path.resolve().as_posix()\nagainst ctx.meta.get_artifact_by_path, and if still not found iterate any other\nworkspace/project contexts available from the loaded context (e.g., other roots\nexposed by the CypilotContext or ctx.workspace list) and call each context’s\nmeta.get_artifact_by_path until a match is found, returning that match (preserve\nartifact_arg in the final error).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`233-236`: **Narrow exception handling when loading workspace TOML.**\n\nCatching `Exception` here can hide unrelated bugs; prefer expected parse/I/O exception classes.\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        except Exception as e:\n+        except (OSError, ValueError) as e:\n             return None, f\"Failed to read workspace file {workspace_path}: {e}\"\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/utils/workspace.py around\nlines 233 - 236, Replace the broad except Exception around the toml_utils.load\ncall with targeted exception handling: catch IO-related errors (e.g.,\nFileNotFoundError, OSError) and TOML parse errors from the TOML library used\n(e.g., toml.TomlDecodeError or the parse exception type your toml_utils wraps)\nand return the same error message for those cases; for any other unexpected\nexceptions raised by toml_utils.load, re-raise them so they are not silently\nswallowed. Reference the toml_utils.load call and the workspace_path variable\nwhen making the change.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`89-104`: **Return a clear error when `--source` does not exist in the workspace.**\n\nThe current flow falls through to `{count: 0}` on unknown source names, which makes typos indistinguishable from a true “no IDs found” result. Consider validating `args.source` against `ctx.sources` before scanning.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested patch</summary>\n\n```diff\n         else:\n             # --source filter: skip primary, scan only matching remote source\n             if is_workspace:\n+                if args.source not in ctx.sources:\n+                    ui.result({\n+                        \"status\": \"ERROR\",\n+                        \"message\": f\"Source '{args.source}' not found\",\n+                        \"available\": sorted(ctx.sources.keys()),\n+                    })\n+                    return 1\n                 from ..utils.context import get_expanded_meta as _get_expanded_meta\n                 for sc in ctx.sources.values():\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/list_ids.py` around lines 89 - 104,\nWhen running with --source in list_ids.py the code silently returns {count: 0}\nif args.source doesn't match any ctx.sources; add an explicit validation before\nthe loop in the is_workspace branch: check args.source exists as a key or\nmatching sc.name in ctx.sources and, if not found, raise or exit with a clear\nerror message mentioning the unknown source name (use args.source) so callers\ncan distinguish typos from \"no IDs found\"; update the is_workspace block that\ncurrently iterates ctx.sources (refer to variables args.source, ctx.sources, and\nartifacts_to_scan) to perform this check and short-circuit with an explanatory\nerror.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for all review comments with AI agents</summary>\n\n````\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nInline comments:\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/architecture/specs/cli.md:\n- Around line 784-785: The docs currently contradict on workspace-add behavior\nfor local paths: update the description for the CLI flag `--path PATH` under\n`workspace-add` to be consistent with the chosen behavior (either \"validated at\nadd-time and returns an error if directory not found\" or \"accepted at add-time\nand reachability reported at runtime\"); explicitly state which behavior applies,\nalign the sentence for `--path PATH` with other workspace flow docs that\ndescribe reachability checks, and ensure the `--url URL` line remains unchanged;\nreference `workspace-add` and the flags `--path PATH`/`--url URL` when making\nthe single-line wording consistent.\n- Around line 731-733: The fenced command blocks in\n.bootstrap/.core/architecture/specs/cli.md (for the command snippets \"cpt\nworkspace-init [--root DIR] ...\", \"cpt workspace-add --name NAME ...\", \"cpt\nworkspace-info\", and \"cpt workspace-sync [--source NAME] ...\") are unlabeled and\ntrigger markdownlint MD040; edit each of those triple-backtick blocks to add a\nlanguage identifier (use bash) immediately after the opening backticks so they\nbecome ```bash ... ``` for each of the four command snippets to satisfy the\nlinter.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/requirements/workspace.md:\n- Line 132: Update the table entry for the `path` field to clarify both\nresolution modes: state that when `path` is used in a standalone workspace file\nit is resolved relative to the workspace file's location, but when used in an\ninline workspace configuration it is resolved relative to the project root; also\nkeep the note that `path` takes precedence over `url` when both are set.\nReference the `path` table entry and the inline workspace behavior described\nelsewhere to ensure consistency.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/validate.py:\n- Around line 125-133: The current code path discards previously collected\nworkspace-level errors by overwriting ctx_errors when handling workspace config\nin --artifact mode; instead, preserve and merge these errors: locate the block\nusing ws_ctx, _find_ws, _ws_cfg and the loop over _ws_cfg.validate() that\ncurrently pushes constraints_error items, and change the logic so you do not\nreassign ctx_errors but extend it (e.g., append each constraints_error or use\nctx_errors.extend([...])) so workspace-config validation errors are retained\nalongside any artifact-mode errors.\n- Line 16: Ruff is flagging undefined names in type annotations\n(WorkspaceContext, CypilotContext, SystemNode) used in functions like\n_resolve_source_context and elsewhere; fix by deferring or resolving their\nimports: either add \"from __future__ import annotations\" at the top of the\nmodule to postpone evaluation of annotations, or wrap the actual imports for\nWorkspaceContext, CypilotContext, and SystemNode in a typing.TYPE_CHECKING block\n(e.g., \"if TYPE_CHECKING: from ... import WorkspaceContext, CypilotContext,\nSystemNode\") so names are available for type checkers but not at runtime,\nensuring all annotations compile without F821.\n\nIn @.bootstrap/.core/skills/cypilot/scripts/cypilot/commands/workspace_add.py:\n- Around line 63-65: The code currently copies args.url into source_info\nexposing credentials; change the logic where url = getattr(args, \"url\", None) to\nkeep the raw URL for persistence but insert a redacted URL into source_info\n(e.g., parse url with urllib.parse.urlparse, drop username and password, rebuild\nnetloc without credentials) so the emitted JSON/human formatter only receives\nthe sanitized version; update the b