derabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 3**\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Due to the large number of review comments, Critical, Major severity comments were prioritized as inline comments.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🟡 Minor comments (29)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/consistency-checklist.md-127-134 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`127-134`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Minor terminology gap: \"normative edges\" introduced but not formally defined.**\n\nThe Definitions section establishes key terms like \"Dependency edge\" but doesn't define \"normative\" edge intent, which is introduced later at line 179 and used in checklist items (e.g., DEP-DOC-001 at line 254). For a document about documentation consistency, formally defining this distinction in the Definitions section would strengthen clarity.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed enhancement</summary>\n\nAdd to the Definitions section after the \"Dependency edge\" entry:\n\n```diff\n - **Dependency edge**: a reference from one document to another (link, \"see …\", \"ALWAYS open …\", \"Extends: …\", path mention).\n+- **Normative edge**: a dependency edge that readers MUST follow to understand or comply with the referring document (e.g., \"ALWAYS open …\", required protocol). Contrast with informational edges that provide optional context.\n - **Canonical source**: the single document that owns a concept; other docs should link to it rather than restate it.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/consistency-checklist.md around lines 127 - 134, Add a\nformal definition for the term \"Normative edge\" immediately after the existing\n\"Dependency edge\" definition: explain that a normative edge is a dependency edge\nthat establishes authoritative, required behavior or ownership (i.e., points to\nthe canonical source that must be followed or updated), and distinguish it from\nnon-normative/citation edges; reference this term wherever used later (e.g.,\nDEP-DOC-001 and the text that introduces \"normative\" at line 179) so those\nchecklist items clearly map to the definition and intent.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/CDSL.md-258-260 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`258-260`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Clarify whether comment syntax is part of the marker format.**\n\nThe marker format `@cpt-begin:{cpt-id}:p{N}:inst-{id}` and the example `# `@cpt-begin`:...` could be ambiguous. Is `@cpt-begin` the marker itself (requiring language-specific comment prefixes like `#` or `//`), or is `# `@cpt-begin`` the complete marker format?\n\nFor consistency with the exclusion markers shown later (lines 324-326), which explicitly show comment syntax variants, consider clarifying that the comment prefix is language-specific.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n - For codebase traceability, every implemented instruction marker maps to code via paired Cypilot block markers wrapping non-empty code.\n-   - Format: `@cpt-begin:{cpt-id}:p{N}:inst-{id}` ... code ... `@cpt-end:{cpt-id}:p{N}:inst-{id}`\n-   - Example: `# `@cpt-begin`:cpt-system-feature-x-algo-validate:p1:inst-check-input`\n+   - Format: `@cpt-begin:{cpt-id}:p{N}:inst-{id}` ... code ... `@cpt-end:{cpt-id}:p{N}:inst-{id}` (prefixed with language-specific comment syntax)\n+   - Examples:\n+     - Python: `# `@cpt-begin`:cpt-system-feature-x-algo-validate:p1:inst-check-input`\n+     - Rust/C++: `// `@cpt-begin`:cpt-system-feature-x-algo-validate:p1:inst-check-input`\n+     - HTML/Markdown: `<!-- `@cpt-begin`:cpt-system-feature-x-algo-validate:p1:inst-check-input -->`\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/CDSL.md around lines 258 - 260, The documentation is\nambiguous about whether the comment prefix is part of the marker; update the\nCDSL text to state that the marker token is the bare string\n`@cpt-begin`:{cpt-id}:p{N}:inst-{id} (and `@cpt-end`:...) and must be placed inside\nthe host language's comment prefix (e.g., \"# `@cpt-begin`...\" for Python, \"//\n`@cpt-begin`...\" for JS/TS), mirroring how exclusion markers are shown later;\ninclude one or two inline examples showing both comment-prefixed variants and\nreference the marker names (`@cpt-begin` and `@cpt-end`) so readers know to wrap the\ntoken in language-specific comments.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/CDSL.md-428-428 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`428-428`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Clarify regex pattern for nested numbered lists.**\n\nThe regex `^\\d+\\.` in check S.1 only matches numbered lists at the start of a line, but CDSL allows indented nested steps (as shown in examples throughout the document). Consider clarifying whether this regex should match all step lines or just top-level steps, or provide an alternative pattern for nested steps.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-| S.1 | Uses numbered markdown lists (1, 2, 3...) | YES | Regex: `^\\d+\\.` for step lines |\n+| S.1 | Uses numbered markdown lists (1, 2, 3...) | YES | Regex: `^\\s*\\d+\\.` for step lines (including nested) |\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/CDSL.md at line 428, S.1's pattern only matches\nnumbers at start-of-line; update the guidance to either clarify that S.1 is\nintended for top-level steps only or replace the pattern to also accept\nindented/nested steps by allowing optional leading whitespace (i.e., adjust the\nregex used in the rule), and update the example text in CDSL.md to show nested\nnumbered steps so reviewers know which behavior is expected; reference the S.1\nrule and the existing `^\\d+\\.` pattern when making the change.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/code-checklist.md-234-234 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`234-234`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Use formal language in YAGNI principle title.**\n\nThe informal contraction \"Aren't Gonna\" reduces the professional tone of the document. Based on static analysis feedback.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-### ENG-CODE-009: You Aren't Gonna Need It (YAGNI)\n+### ENG-CODE-009: You Aren't Going to Need It (YAGNI)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/code-checklist.md at line 234, The heading \"###\nENG-CODE-009: You Aren't Gonna Need It (YAGNI)\" uses informal contraction;\nupdate the title to a formal phrasing such as \"### ENG-CODE-009: You Are Not\nGoing to Need It (YAGNI)\" (or \"You Will Not Need It (YAGNI)\") by replacing the\nstring exactly where the heading appears so the identifier ENG-CODE-009 and the\nYAGNI acronym remain intact.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/artifacts-registry.md-937-939 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`937-939`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Duplicate note on artifact paths.**\n\nLines 937 and 939 contain nearly identical notes about artifact paths and `artifacts_dir`. This appears to be an accidental duplication.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n **Note**: Artifact paths are FULL paths relative to `project_root`. The `artifacts_dir` defines the default base directory for NEW artifacts — subdirectories for specific kinds (`specs/`, `ADR/`) are defined by the kit.\n\n-**Note**: Artifact paths are FULL paths relative to `project_root`. The `artifacts_dir` defines the default base directory for NEW artifacts — subdirectories for specific kinds (`features/`, `ADR/`) are defined by the kit.\n-\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/artifacts-registry.md around lines 937 - 939, Remove\nthe duplicated note about artifact paths (the lines containing \"Artifact paths\nare FULL paths relative to `project_root`\" and the `artifacts_dir` description),\nleaving a single canonical note; ensure the remaining note references\n`project_root`, `artifacts_dir`, and the kit subdirectory examples (`specs/` or\n`features/`, and `ADR/`) correctly and delete the redundant instance.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/artifacts-registry.md-667-667 (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`667-667`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n\n**Add language specifier to fenced code block.**\n\nStatic analysis flagged a fenced code block without a language specifier, which reduces readability and syntax highlighting.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n **Example**:\n-```\n+```text\n project_root: \"..\"\n artifact path: \"architecture/PRD.md\"\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n````\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/artifacts-re