1, The TOC link \"[Phase 4:\nSync FEATURE](`#phase-4-sync-feature-featuremd-traceability-mode-on-only`)\" points\nto a non-existent anchor; update the link fragment to match the actual heading\nslug or rename the target heading so they match. Locate the TOC entry text\n\"Phase 4: Sync FEATURE\" and the target heading (the heading that should be\n\"feature.md traceability mode on only\" or similar), then either change the\nfragment to the correct slug derived from that heading or adjust the heading\ntext so its generated anchor equals\n\"#phase-4-sync-feature-featuremd-traceability-mode-on-only\". Ensure the final\nslug follows the Markdown renderer's anchor rules (lowercase, hyphens, no extra\ncharacters) so markdownlint no longer flags an invalid fragment.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (13)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/CDSL.md (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`363-417`: **Add language specifiers to fenced code blocks.**\n\nThe error message templates use fenced code blocks without language specifiers. While these are templates rather than actual code, adding `text` as the language identifier would improve markdown linting compliance and make the formatting intent explicit.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n ### Invalid CDSL Syntax\n \n **If CDSL content contains prohibited syntax**:\n-```\n+```text\n ⚠️ Invalid CDSL syntax detected: {description}\n```\n\nApply similar changes to all error template blocks (lines 366-371, 378-382, 388-393, 400-404, 410-415).\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n````\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/CDSL.md around lines 363 - 417, The markdown\nerror-template fenced code blocks in the CDSL spec lack language specifiers;\nupdate each triple-backtick block that contains plain-text templates (e.g., the\nblocks for \"Invalid CDSL syntax\", \"Missing Required Tokens\", \"Duplicate\nInstruction IDs\", \"Unbalanced Cypilot Markers in Code\", and \"Empty Cypilot Block\nin Code\") to use a \"text\" language tag (change ``` to ```text) so linting\nrecognizes them as plain text; look for the templates containing symbols like\ninst-{id}, cpt-begin/cpt-end, and the sample warning lines and add the language\nspecifier to each fenced block.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`343-355`: **Consider adding a note about escaped backticks in the example.**\n\nThe escaped backticks (`\\`\\`\\``) on lines 349-352 are necessary for proper rendering but might confuse readers who try to copy the example literally. Consider adding a brief note that the backslashes are for documentation purposes only.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n **Example**:\n \n+*Note: Backslashes before backticks below are for documentation rendering only; use regular backticks in actual code.*\n+\n ```markdown\n Real implementation:\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/CDSL.md around lines 343 - 355, The documentation\nexample uses escaped backticks (\\`\\`\\`) around the Rust snippet which are\nnecessary for rendering but can confuse readers copying the example; add a brief\none-line note immediately above or below the example block (near the\ncpt-begin/cpt-end markers like cpt-example-feature-z-algo-w:p1:inst-example)\nstating that the backslashes are only for documentation rendering and should be\nremoved when copying the code into an editor. Ensure the note is short and\nclearly references the example so readers know to use plain triple backticks in\ntheir own files.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`102-106`: **Consider clarifying placeholder notation.**\n\nThe curly brace notation (`{instruction}`, `{short-id}`) effectively indicates placeholders, but adding a brief note could prevent misinterpretation as literal syntax. For example: \"Note: `{...}` indicates placeholders to be replaced with actual content.\"\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n **Required format**:\n \n+*Note: Curly braces `{...}` below indicate placeholders to be replaced with actual content.*\n+\n - Numbered step: 1. [ ] - `p1` - {instruction} - `inst-some-job`\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/CDSL.md around lines 102 - 106, Add a short clarifying\nnote under the \"Required format\" header explaining that curly braces like\n`{instruction}` and `{short-id}` represent placeholders to be replaced (not\nliteral syntax), e.g. a single sentence \"Note: `{...}` indicates placeholders to\nbe replaced with actual content.\" Place this sentence directly above or below\nthe existing bullet list so readers see it when scanning the formats for\nnumbered, nested and bullet steps.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/execution-protocol.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`54-76`: **Compaction recovery prevents context loss.**\n\nThe recovery protocol (lines 54-76) addresses a real problem: after conversation summarization, agents may lose awareness of active workflows. The detection signals (lines 61-64) and 3-step recovery (lines 67-70) provide a systematic approach.\n\nConsider whether agents can reliably detect compaction from conversation summaries alone, or if explicit markers/metadata would improve reliability.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/execution-protocol.md around lines 54 - 76, The\ncompaction recovery section relies only on parsing conversation summaries to\ndetect compaction, which can be unreliable; update the protocol to require and\ncheck for an explicit compaction marker/metadata in addition to the current\ndetection signals (e.g., a standardized flag or header like \"compaction: true\"\nattached to message metadata), and modify the recovery steps so the agent runs\n`cypilot adapter-info` and reloads specs from `{cypilot_adapter_path}/AGENTS.md`\nwhenever the explicit marker is present or when any current detection signal\n(`\"This session is being continued from a previous conversation\"`, summaries\nmentioning `/cypilot-generate` or `/cypilot-analyze`, or Cypilot tasks in the\ntodo list) is observed; also add a short note in the protocol that agents MUST\nprefer the explicit marker over heuristics when both conflict.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/code-checklist.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`330-331`: **Clarify authentication/authorization check scope.**\n\nLines 330-331 state checks should be \"present where required by the design/spec\" but this could be interpreted as only checking documented requirements. Consider clarifying that reviewers should also flag missing auth/authz where security requires it but design doesn't mention it.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Consider clarifying security check scope</summary>\n\n```diff\n - [ ] Authentication checks present where required by the design/spec (routes, handlers, entrypoints)\n-- [ ] Authorization checks present for operations that mutate or access protected resources (as defined by design/spec)\n+- [ ] Authorization checks present for operations that mutate or access protected resources (review both design/spec requirements AND security best practices)\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/code-checklist.md around lines 330 - 331, The\nchecklist items \"Authentication checks present where required by the design/spec\n(routes, handlers, entrypoints)\" and \"Authorization checks present for\noperations that mutate or access protected resources (as defined by\ndesign/spec)\" are ambiguous; update the wording to require reviewers to flag\nmissing authentication/authorization even when the design/spec omits them.\nChange both entries to explicitly say reviewers must verify auth/authz is\nimplemented per design and also raise issues when security best-practices demand\nauth/authz for routes/handlers/operations not specified in the spec, so\nreviewers know to flag undocumented but necessary controls.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/CLISPEC.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`127-148`: **Type system covers common CLI needs.**\n\nThe 8 supported types (`<string>`, `<number>`, `<boolean>`, `<path>`, `<slug>`, `<url>`, `<email>`, `<json>`) provide good coverage for typical CLI arguments. The constraint examples (lines 143-147) show how to express validation rules in descriptions.\n\nConsider adding types for common CLI patterns like `<enum>` or `<list>` if not covered by existing types.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/CLISPEC.md around lines 127 - 148, Add two new supported types to\nthe \"Type System\" section: document `<enum>` (a value restricted to a finite set\nof options) and `<list>` (a comma-separated or repeated values list of a base\ntype), update the \"Supported Types\" list under the \"Type System\" heading to\ninclude them, and extend the \"Type Constraints\" examples to show how to express\nenum options and list item types (e.g., `mode  <enum>  required  one of\n(dev,prod,test)` and `tags  <list:string>  optional  comma-separated`). Ensure\nthe entries are consistent with the existing style used for\n`<string>`/`<number>` and the ARGUMENTS example block so readers can see\nconcrete usage for `<enum>` and `<list>`.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/prompt-engineering.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`45-55`: **Add language specifier to analysis layers code block.**\n\nThe fenced code block displaying the analysis layers overview should have a language specifier for consistency.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Add language specifier</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n Layer 1: Document Classification     → What type of instruction is this?\n```\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n````\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/prompt-engineering.md around lines 45 - 55, The fenced\ncode block that lists the analysis layers (starting with the line \"Layer 1:\nDocument Classification     → What type of instruction is this?\") lacks a\nlanguage specifier; update the opening fence to include a language tag (e.g.,\n```text) so the block is consistently rendered and highlighted, ensuring the\nblock header is changed from ``` to ```text while leaving the inner lines (Layer\n1...Layer 9) unchanged.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/requirements/kit-constraints.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`231-231`: **Capitalize \"Markdown\" as a proper noun.**\n\nPer LanguageTool hint, \"markdown\" should be \"Markdown\" when referring to the formatting language.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Fix capitalization</summary>\n\n```diff\n-The validator walks the artifact's markdown headings and checks that:\n+The validator walks the artifact's Markdown headings and checks that:\n```\n</details>\n\nAlso applies to line 241.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n```\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/requirements/kit-constraints.md at line 231, Update the\ncapitalization of the formatting language name from \"markdown\" to \"Markdown\"\nwherever it appears in the descriptive text—specifically change the sentence\n\"The validator walks the artifact’s markdown headings and checks that:\" and the\nanalogous occurrence referenced later (also noted in the review) so both\ninstances read \"Markdown\".\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/kits/sdlc/guides/BROWNFIELD.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`42-48`: **Add language specifiers to fenced code blocks for better readability.**\n\nMultiple fenced code blocks throughout this guide lack language specifiers. While the content is correct, adding language hints improves syntax highlighting and documentation quality.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Example fix for scenario code blocks</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n 1. cypilot reverse FEATURE from src/auth/     → Creates FEATURE from code\n```\n</details>\n\nThis applies to code blocks at lines 42, 56, 70, 84, 118, 165, 220, 262, 319, 373, 437, 446, 455, and 464.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Prompt for AI Agents</summary>\n\n````\nVerify each finding against the current code and only fix it if needed.\n\nIn @.cypilot/kits/sdlc/guides/BROWNFIELD.md around lines 42 - 48, The fenced\ncode blocks showing CLI examples (e.g., lines containing \"cypilot reverse\nFEATURE from src/auth/\" and the other numbered \"cypilot ...\" steps) lack\nlanguage hints—add an appropriate language specifier (for example ```text or\n```bash) to each of those fenced blocks so they get proper syntax highlighting;\nupdate every block that contains the cypilot command examples (the snippets with\n\"cypilot reverse FEATURE...\", \"cypilot decompose from features\", \"cypilot make\nDESIGN ...\", etc.) to include the specifier.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.cypilot/kits/sdlc/artifacts/PRD/examples/example.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-2`: **Remove leading blank line.**\n\nThe file starts with an empty line before the