architecture/ directory (or add new files with the\nexpected names if necessary).\n\nIn `@prompts/pr/prd-review.md`:\n- Around line 1-15: The prompt in prompts/pr/prd-review.md references\nnon-existent docs (/docs/spec-templates/PRD.md and docs/checklists/PRD.md);\nupdate those references to the actual PRD locations (e.g., architecture/PRD.md\nand examples/overwork_alert/architecture/PRD.md) so the review workflow won't\nfail — search for the strings \"/docs/spec-templates/PRD.md\" and\n\"docs/checklists/PRD.md\" in prompts/pr/prd-review.md and replace them with the\ncorrect paths, ensuring any relative/path formatting in the prompt matches the\nrepository structure and that the \"PR Review Focus (Requirements)\" guidance\nstill points to the right checklist file.\n\nIn `@skills/cypilot/SKILL.md`:\n- Line 174: Typo: remove the duplicated word in the rule text; replace \"STOP\nWHEN user user cancel or exit\" with \"STOP WHEN user cancel or exit\" in the line\nthat reads 'ALWAYS ask user \"analyze (read-only) or generate (modify)?\" WHEN\nintent is UNCLEAR: help, look at, work with, handle and STOP WHEN user user\ncancel or exit' so the rule correctly reads with a single \"user\" and preserves\nthe rest of the sentence.\n- Line 162: The text uses an undefined variable `{project_root}` in the\ninstruction that runs `python3 {project_root}/.cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py\nlist`; fix this by either (A) adding a `{project_root}` entry to the Variables\nsection (similar format to `{cypilot_path}` and `{cypilot_mode}`) or (B)\nreplacing `{project_root}` with the already-defined `{cypilot_path}` if they\nrefer to the same location; update the line in SKILL.md accordingly so the\ncommand reads either `python3 {project_root}/.cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py list`\nwith `{project_root}` defined in Variables or `python3\n{cypilot_path}/.cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py list` if you choose the\nreplacement.\n\nIn `@skills/scripts/pr.py`:\n- Around line 749-752: The current hardcoded append call (ln.append with \"-\n**Status**: ✅ RESOLVED — AI VERIFIED\") wrongly claims verification; change this\nto only add the \"AI VERIFIED\" suffix when an explicit verification check passes\n(e.g., call or flag such as is_thread_verified/verify_resolution for the\nPR/thread) and otherwise append a neutral status like \"- **Status**: ✅ RESOLVED\"\nor \"- **Status**: ✅ RESOLVED — UNVERIFIED\"; update the code path that builds the\nstatus string (the ln.append site) to use the verification boolean rather than\nalways including \"AI VERIFIED\".\n- Around line 2-8: The module-level docstring contains a Unicode en dash in the\nfirst line (\"PR review helper – fetch PR diffs...\") which triggers Ruff RUF002;\nupdate the top-level triple-quoted string in skills/scripts/pr.py to use a plain\nhyphen (\"PR review helper - fetch PR diffs...\") instead of the en dash so the\ndocstring only contains ASCII hyphens.\n- Around line 910-919: The current \"status ALL\" branch crashes when PRS_DIR is\nmissing and only checks local .prs entries; change it to query GitHub like\n\"fetch ALL\": call _list_open_prs() to get the list of open PR IDs, load excludes\nvia _load_exclude_list(), iterate the sorted PR IDs, skip ones in excludes\n(printing the same \"Skipping PR #...\" message), and for each remaining PR\nconstruct the local meta path (os.path.join(PRS_DIR, d, \"meta.json\")) and call\nstatus(d) only if that meta file exists; this removes the direct\nos.listdir(PRS_DIR) usage and avoids FileNotFoundError while including unfetched\nopen PRs from GitHub.\n- Around line 171-174: The fetch(pr_number: str) function currently uses\nunvalidated pr_number to build filesystem paths and likely to construct CLI\nargs; validate and sanitize pr_number before use by (1) enforcing an allowed\npattern (e.g., only digits or a strict regex for repo/number) and rejecting\ninputs containing path separators or traversal sequences, (2) normalizing the\njoined path with os.path.normpath and verifying the resulting path is inside\nPRS_DIR before calling os.makedirs, and (3) when invoking external commands\n(e.g., gh), pass pr_number as a separate argument (not interpolated into a shell\nstring) or explicitly escape/validate it to avoid CLI injection. Ensure checks\nreference fetch and PRS_DIR so the guard prevents path traversal and unsafe CLI\nusage.\n\nIn `@workflows/pr-status.md`:\n- Around line 43-98: Update the workflow to point to the real script location\nand resolve the missing config: replace occurrences of\n\".cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py\" with \"./skills/scripts/pr.py\" so the documented\nScript entry and the listed Run commands (e.g., \"python3\n.cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py list/status/reorder\") use the existing pr.py, and\neither add the referenced configuration file pr-review.json at the expected\n{cypilot_adapter_path} or remove the \"{cypilot_adapter_path}/pr-review.json\"\nConfig entry (and any template references) so the workflow no longer references\na non-existent pr-review.json; verify updates touch the workflow entries that\nname pr.py, the Config line referencing pr-review.json, and any templates block.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>skills/cypilot/SKILL.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`162-225`: **Inconsistent path references: hardcoded vs variable-based.**\n\nThroughout the PR workflow sections, paths alternate between variable-based (`{cypilot_path}`, `{project_root}`) and hardcoded (`.cypilot/`, `python3 .cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py`). This inconsistency makes the instructions harder to maintain and prevents proper path customization via the config file.\n\nExamples:\n- Line 162: `{project_root}/.cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py`\n- Line 201: `python3 {project_root}/.cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py list`\n- Line 210: `python3 {project_root}/.cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py`\n- Line 214: `.cypilot/templates/pr/`\n\n\n\nConsider standardizing on variable-based paths throughout. Since the Variables section defines `{cypilot_path}` as the base, use it consistently:\n\n```diff\n-ALWAYS run `python3 {project_root}/.cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py list` directly WHEN...\n+ALWAYS run `python3 {cypilot_path}/skills/scripts/pr.py list` directly WHEN...\n```\n\n```diff\n-7. Use templates from `.cypilot/templates/pr/`\n+7. Use templates from `{cypilot_path}/templates/pr/`\n```\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>prompts/pr/code-review.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-18`: **Generic prompt title conflicts with Rust-specific content.**\n\nThe file is titled \"Code Review Prompt\" (line 1) and the focus areas start generically (lines 5-10), but line 11 introduces highly Rust-specific tooling: \"Cargo, clippy, dylint, and rustfmt conformance.\" Lines 16-18 also reference Rust-specific files (`RUST.md`, `clippy.toml`, `Cargo.toml`).\n\nThis creates confusion: is this a generic code review prompt that should work for any language, or a Rust-specific prompt? The generic title suggests the former, but the content is the latter.\n\n\n\n**Option 1**: Rename to `rust-code-review.md` and make the Rust focus explicit:\n\n```diff\n-# Code Review Prompt\n+# Rust Code Review Prompt\n\n-Review the following PR code changes.\n+Review the following PR Rust code changes.\n```\n\n**Option 2**: Generalize the prompt and create language-specific variants:\n\n```diff\n - Correctness and edge cases\n - Code style and idiomatic patterns\n - Performance implications\n - Test coverage\n - Security vulnerabilities\n-- Cargo, clippy, dylint, and rustfmt conformance\n-- New code has unit tests and end to end tests in testing/ folder\n+- Language-specific linting and formatting\n+- New code has unit tests and integration tests\n - Mistakes and potential misbehaviors\n\n Use `docs/checklists/CODE.md` as the structured review guide when available.\n-Refer to `guidelines/DNA/languages/RUST.md` for Rust-specific conventions.\n+Refer to language-specific guidelines in `guidelines/DNA/languages/` as applicable.\n Refer to `guidelines/SECURITY.md` for security requirements.\n-Refer to `clippy.toml` and `Cargo.toml` workspace lints for lint rules.\n+Refer to project linting configuration files for lint rules.\n```\n\nThen create `prompts/pr/rust-code-review.md` with the Rust-specific details.\n\n---\n\n`12-12`: **Consider addressing grammar suggestion from static analysis.**\n\nThe static analysis tool suggests hyphenating \"end to end\" → \"end-to-end\" when used as a compound modifier. While this is a minor style issue, it would improve professional presentation.\n\n\n\n```diff\n-- New code has unit tests and end to end tests in testing/ folder\n+- New code has unit tests and end-to-end tests in testing/ folder\n```\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>workflows/pr-status.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`43-47`: **Hardcoded paths conflict with variable-based configuration.**\n\nLines 43-46 use hardcoded paths (`.cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py`, `.cypilot/templates/pr/`) instead of the variable-based approach used elsewhere (`{cypilot_path}`). This is inconsistent with the SKILL.md approach and prevents path customization via `.cypilot-config.json`.\n\n\n\nConsider using variables consistently:\n\n```diff\n-**Script**: `python3 .cypilot/skills/scripts/pr.py`\n+**Script**: `python3 {cypilot_path}/skills/scripts/pr.py`\n **Config**: `{cypilot_adapter_path}/pr-review.json`\n-**Templates**: `.cypilot/templates/pr/`\n+**Templates**: `{cypilot_path}/templates/pr/`\n```\n\nApply the same pattern to lines 60, 68, and 98 where the script is invoked.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pull/64#pullrequestreview-3785780223","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyber-constructor/pulls/64","author_association":"NONE","_links":{"html"